@knowledge aterm
@viewpoints specification domain skill generic

@description {
  Aterm language, CLI and Graph behavior, corpus workflows, modeling methods and naming principles.
}
@scope {
  This Knowledge owns Aterm's language, public behavior and reusable usage and modeling guidance.
  Define every input, default, condition, refusal and state change that affects an operation.
  Define result information and meaningful ordering; leave incidental wording and layout open.
  Keep one authoritative owner for each obligation; compose shared guidance through section references.
  Use language for notation and vocabulary; operations for public tool behavior; graph for Graph behavior.
  Preserve graph subgroups for rendering.structure, rendering.nodes, rendering.edges, layout, operations and views.
  Graph coverage includes authored, derived and Hidden Groups, visible representatives, directed edges,
  navigation, reading, layout choices, viewport changes, interaction feedback and revision handling.
  It includes recursive Compound Nodes and bounded candidate selection for Ports, Anchors and Connection Points.
  Intermediate obstacle-routing waypoints and saved graph sessions remain outside scope.
  A UI framework, internal class structure and incidental visual styling remain implementation choices.
  Use usage for reusable guidance sources and runtime input contracts; shipped operational Skill bodies live in aterm_skills.
  Use usage for corpus workflows, modeling for the question-driven method and principles.naming for naming.
  The Naming Principle remains generally applicable; placement here does not restrict it to Aterm.
  Use natural English noun phrases for subjects and criteria, and verb phrases for behavior.
  Retain semantic heads identifying Commands, Events and reusable guidance independently of declaration Term Kind.
  The Knowledge supplies the namespace; local names retain only meaningful qualifiers and word order.
  Repository architecture, build and development rules belong to aterm_development.
  General Skill theory belongs to skill_foundations; the worked example belongs to vending_machine.
  Ship this complete Knowledge with vending_machine; its references must resolve within that shipped set.
  Viewpoints own their Term Kind names; qualify overlapping names with the original Viewpoint.
}


principle _Consistent_Naming_Principle_ in principles.naming = {
  The principle that names identify concepts consistently within a declared vocabulary and preserve established meanings.
.applicability
  - Apply to choosing or reviewing names whose meanings are part of a shared API, domain model or implementation vocabulary.
  - A proposed rename does not by itself authorize changing an existing public contract.
.contract
  - Use the same canonical name for the same concept within the applicable vocabulary.
  - Use natural English word order: a noun phrase for a subject or criterion, and a verb phrase for an operation.
  - Let the Knowledge namespace supply the domain; do not repeat it as a routine local-name prefix.
  - Judge name sufficiency for a reader who knows the project's purpose and core domain concepts, without assuming they know every supporting Definition.
  - Core domain concepts may use their established short names when unambiguous within that Knowledge; name other subjects with enough meaningful qualification to distinguish their roles in that context.
  - Check the first vocabulary as a connected set before using its names as patterns; neither missing role modifiers nor redundant context should propagate.
  - Name the subject identified by the Definition; the name itself must expose what that subject is.
  - Retain semantic head nouns such as Principle, Command, Event, Procedure, Workflow and Skill when they identify the subject. Declaration Term Kind does not replace that meaning.
  - Distinguish an act from a Command requesting it, an Event recording it and reusable instructions for performing it. A verb phrase can name the act itself.
  - A familiar function, CLI or button label does not establish a proper name for a different modeled subject.
  - Do not create additional Terms merely to instantiate each role; retain only independently needed subjects.
  - Keep modifiers that distinguish meanings inside the same Knowledge, as in Graph Node and Query Result.
  - Give distinct concepts names that make their difference visible in the context where those names are used.
  - Prefer an established industry term when its meaning matches; state the semantic difference before choosing a different term.
  - Do not resolve an ambiguous concept merely by selecting a more attractive spelling; identify and settle the meaning first.
.explanation
  - Name the defined subject itself; retain meaningful heads and distinguish behavior from Commands, Events and reusable guidance.
.validation
  - A name-to-meaning mapping and representative uses must support each naming decision.
  - Unexplained synonyms for one concept, incompatible meanings under one name, and familiar terms used with unstated different meanings are violations.
  - Identify the semantic head without relying on declaration Term Kind or a surrounding invoke, follow or read to supply the missing subject.
  - Read each name with its Definition and representative uses; grammatical fluency alone does not establish a sound identity.
.examples
  - Context: the vocabulary contains both Graph Search results and Text Search results.
  - Result — BAD: the name does not identify which result it denotes.
  - Graph Search Result — GOOD: the name identifies the relevant operation.
  - Context: Graph Search Result is already the canonical name for this meaning.
  - Search Outcome — BAD: a new synonym gives the same meaning a second name.
  - Graph Search Result — GOOD: reuse preserves the existing meaning.
  - Context: a Knowledge already supplies the domain.
  - Repeating the project name on every Term — BAD.
  - Knowledge, Search Space and Insert Coin — GOOD.
  - Context: Plugin and Document are the core concepts of a document collaboration project.
  - Plugin and Document — sufficient; Document Plugin and Plugin Document — redundant in this vocabulary.
  - The Plugin supplies a renderer: Renderer — insufficient context; Plugin Renderer — clear role.
  - A location inside a Document: Anchor — unclear; Document Anchor — sufficient.
  - Adoc Plugin Renderer repeats the Knowledge's project context; Plugin Renderer retains the meaningful qualifier.
  - Context: naming a component by its role.
  - Adapter Git — BAD: a classification label precedes the subject.
  - Git Adapter — GOOD: natural modifier and head-noun order.
  - For a criterion: Ownership — BAD; Single Owner Principle — GOOD.
  - For a Command: Create Task — BAD; Task Creation Command — GOOD.
  - For the act itself: Insert Coin — GOOD.
  - For an Event: Task Created alone hides the event subject; Task Creation Event names it.
  - For reusable guidance: Analyze Impact — BAD; Impact Analysis Workflow — GOOD.
  - Pythagorean Theorem and Principle of Relativity retain the head naming what they are.
.remarks
  - Language-specific spelling conventions remain caller inputs.
  - Chosen: this corpus separates English words with underscores and capitalizes each word; proper names and established acronyms keep their spelling.
  - Chosen: a product or domain subject may keep its proper name; the namespace removes redundant qualification, not the subject itself.
  - A Knowledge can supply domain context; it cannot replace the semantic head identifying a subject.
  - Chosen: naming distinguishes behavior from guidance or messages about behavior; it does not encode every declaration Term Kind mechanically.
}


rule _Reference_Integrity_Rule_ in usage = {
  The rule for preserving exact Term identities and resolvable references during corpus work.
.applicability
  - Apply to Term selection, references, Term Declaration edits and renames.
.contract
  - Find an existing name with `aterm term list '*Word*'` before using it.
  - Preserve the exact case and spelling of canonical Term names.
  - Quote globs so the shell does not expand them.
  - Every recognized unescaped Term token must resolve within its Knowledge; qualified tokens resolve in the named Knowledge. Markdown code exclusions still apply.
  - Use local names in Term Declarations and qualified IDs in cross-Knowledge references and durable selectors.
  - Check the target Knowledge's scope before applying a naming convention; a scoped experiment is not a corpus-wide rename rule.
  - Use `aterm term rename` to change a Term and its references together.
  - Write an obsolete or hypothetical name as plain words or escape its leading underscore.
  - Close unresolved-reference draft warnings before handoff.
.explanation
  - Preserve canonical identities and resolve declarations, corpus references and selected external references.
.validation
  - `aterm corpus check` reports no unresolved references or duplicate Term Declarations.
  - Rename review covers every declaration and reference intended by the change.
.examples
  - Context: changing an existing Term name.
  - Edit only its declaration — BAD.
  - Run rename and review the complete diff — GOOD.
  - Context: documenting a placeholder rather than referencing a real Term.
  - Unescaped unknown Term token — BAD.
  - Escaped `\_Placeholder_` — GOOD.
}


rule _Specification_Synchronization_Rule_ in usage = {
  The rule for keeping implementation changes and their owning semantic specification consistent.
.applicability
  - Apply before implementation, code changes or review.
.contract
  - Find the Term Declarations that own the affected behavior before editing code.
  - Read each full Term Declaration through show or view; read its Knowledge scope through plain-text term view --detail or JSON/YAML output.
  - Do not infer scope from filenames.
  - Establish missing specification boundaries from the task before implementation.
  - Record chosen boundaries explicitly.
  - Compare required scope coverage with the existing Term Declarations.
  - Treat a missing in-scope obligation as a specification gap.
  - Add requirements supported by the source to the owning Term Declarations.
  - Record new behavior decisions as Chosen in the Viewpoint's rationale section.
  - When code and specification disagree, choose the correction supported by user intent, evidence and scope. Either may change; existing code alone does not justify weakening the contract.
  - Record that decision and synchronize the owning Term Declarations and implementation.
  - Do not silently widen scope.
  - Do not promote incidental implementation details into requirements.
  - Keep consequential unresolved choices visible and ask the user when evidence cannot settle them.
  - Every code update must include its corresponding specification update in the same task.
  - Specify changed inputs, defaults, conditions, refusals, result information and state changes.
  - Preserve an existing contract when it already states the intended behavior.
  - In that case, record the correction rationale and verification in its rationale section.
  - Do not invent behavioral changes merely to create a specification diff.
  - Compare code changes, specification changes and scope before handoff.
  - Resolve or explicitly report every in-scope gap found during the work.
  - Run behavior-relevant tests as well as structural corpus checking.
  - Identify affected Term Declarations, verification and unresolved gaps in the handoff.
.explanation
  - Resolve differences using intended behavior and evidence, then verify both specification and implementation.
.validation
  - Every affected behavior maps to an owning Term Declaration.
  - Every code update has a corresponding specification update.
  - Verification supports the behavior claimed in the handoff.
  - Structural checking alone does not establish semantic coverage.
.examples
  - Context: a contract already requires an error for an invalid flag.
  - Change the contract to accept the flag because code currently ignores it — BAD.
  - Correct the implementation and record its verification — GOOD.
  - Context: the user chooses a new supported default.
  - Change only code — BAD.
  - Update the owning default contract and implementation together — GOOD.
.remarks
  - Chosen: use the owning Viewpoint's rationale section; not every Viewpoint offers remarks.
}


explanation _Corpus_Notation_Guide_ in usage = {
  A guide to reading Aterm declarations, references, Relations and Knowledge scope.
.relations
  references _Syntax_
  references _vending_machine:Product_
  references _vending_machine:Slot_
.explanation
  - Description is the reader-facing account of what the Knowledge contains; Scope is the writer-facing coverage boundary. Both are optional multiline header blocks after @viewpoints and before Term Declarations, using the same block syntax.
  - A Term is one exact name, written as an underscored token such as `\_Like_This_`.
  - A Term Declaration declares a Term under a Term Kind and supplies its section content.
  - A Relation is a labeled directed connection between two Terms.
  - A Knowledge is our current model of a subject, stored in one .trm file with directives and Term Declarations.
  - A Home is an .aterm directory whose aterm.yaml selects corpus sources and Viewpoints.
  - Each (Knowledge ID, local Term name) pair has exactly one Term Declaration across the corpus.
  - `skill.procedure` and `domain.procedure` are different Term Kinds; each Term must choose one Term Kind for its single Term Declaration.
  - Use a short Term Kind only when one declared Viewpoint supplies that name.
  - `procedure` and `skill.procedure` identify the same Term Kind when skill is its only declared owner; declaring both for one Term is a duplicate.
  - Repeated Term declarations are always errors within one Knowledge, regardless of Term Kind or Group.
  - A Term Declaration opens with its Term Kind, local Term name, optional `in group.path` and `= {` at column zero; Term names in declarations never use Knowledge-qualified IDs; Term Kind names may use Viewpoint qualifiers.
  - Group path segments use lowercase_snake_case; dots create nested Groups within the Knowledge.
  - Example: `procedure \_Purchase_Product_ in machine.procedures = { ... }`.
  - Group membership belongs to the single Term Declaration of a Term.
  - A Group path does not change Term identity, references or Relations.
  - Use Group paths for authored organization; do not invent has_part or other Relations to force display grouping.
  - `term list --tree` presents File / Group / Term Declaration after selection, with counts of matching descendant Term Declarations.
  - --with-fileline adds Term Declaration locations; JSON/YAML retain the ordinary structured result.
  - Graph-derived part Groups and temporary Hidden Groups do not appear in the authored CLI tree.
  - Use a complete-Term Declaration `term edit` to change the Group path; `term show`, `term view`, `term rename` and `term format` preserve it.
  - The initial unheaded body is definition.
  - Later section headers use `.name` at column zero.
  - Body lines are indented two spaces and the closing brace is at column zero.
  - The owning Viewpoint determines content sections; every Term Kind permits .relations immediately after definition.
  - Every recognized unescaped Term token in Markdown content is a reference; fenced and indented code are opaque.
  - Every non-self reference must have a target declaration in this Term Declaration's .relations; declaration order does not matter.
  - A section reference selects an actual section key: `\_Name_.contract`.
  - Put `\_Name_.contract†` alone on a body line to insert that section at that position in Markdown.
  - Use `\_Name_.explanation†` for explanatory content and `\_Name_.procedure†` for action instructions.
  - body is a section only when the target Term Kind declares it.
  - Expansion inserts content without an added heading and preserves nested Markdown and Knowledge context.
  - Missing sections, duplicate Terms and expansion cycles fail checking.
  - A plain section reference without a dagger does not expand.
  - Whole-Term `\_Name_†` makes view append the complete Term Declaration of that Term as required context.
  - Use `aterm term view TERM --output markdown` to read a Skill with in-place section expansion.
  - A .relations line has the form `phrase target` without a bullet, heading or source token.
  - The source is the declaring Term; use a built-in name or a one-to-five-word phrase.
  - Structural built-ins: is_a = Generalization; has_part = Parthood; has_member = Membership; has_state = State membership.
  - Other built-ins: instance_of, has_property, depends_on, realizes, references.
  - Built-in names are exact lowercase; unknown underscore names are errors.
  - Example: `has_part \_Order_Line_`; cardinality and lifecycle rules stay in content.
  - Example: `has_state \_Hidden_` on a Visibility State Model declares a permitted State, not its current State; initial, transition and exactly-one rules stay in content.
  - Ordinary phrases remain association; built-ins do not copy Contracts or infer edges.
  - Declare the whole target in .relations; put section expansion in the content where the guidance applies.
  - A .relations target may still carry a whole-Term dagger; it cannot carry a section selector.
  - Content sections state conditions and detailed rules; use `term occurrences` to find each target's occurrences.
  - Use headings only when grouping helps the reader; only .relations declares Relations.
  - A Knowledge can declare one optional `@scope { ... }` block after its @viewpoints directive.
  - Description tells readers what this Knowledge contains. Scope tells writers what belongs here, what must be covered and what belongs elsewhere.
  - Its Markdown body is nonempty and indented two spaces.
  - Its closing brace is at column zero.
  - Default text `term show` and `term view` omit Scope; plain-text `term view --detail` includes // Scope: annotations, and JSON/YAML retain Scope.
  - The // Knowledge:, // Viewpoint: and // Scope: annotations do not replace source directives.
  - A view may combine Term Declarations from several Knowledges and is not a complete .trm source file as-is.
  - `--section` returns only section text, so it does not replace the initial full read.
  - Each Knowledge has a required @knowledge <lowercase_snake_case_id> before @viewpoints. The filename is independent of identity.
  - Local references resolve in the owning Knowledge. Cross-Knowledge references use qualified spelling, for example \_aterm:Term_.
  - CLI exact names resolve automatically when unique; ambiguity returns candidates and --knowledge guidance. Use qualified selectors in durable references.
.validation
  - Compare the explanation with `aterm term show _vending_machine:Slot_` on the shared Sample.
  - Use the syntax contract when exact notation is needed: _Syntax_.
.examples
  - Context: reading the shared Sample's Slot Term Declaration.
  - `aterm term show _vending_machine:Slot_` exposes its Term Kind, definition, contract and remarks.
  - `_vending_machine:Slot_ contains _vending_machine:Product_` describes a Relation declared by `contains \_Product_` in Slot's .relations.
  - Slot price belongs to the Slot rather than the Product.
  - The same Product can have different prices in different Slots.

  ```trm
  // Viewpoint: specification
  concept _Slot_ in inventory.concepts = {
    A position in the machine that holds a stock of one _Product_ at one price.
  .relations
    contains _Product_
  .contract
    - A _Slot_ must contain at most one _Product_ kind.
    - A _Slot_ stock count must not be negative.
    - A _Slot_ price must be a positive amount.
    - A _Slot_ with a stock count of zero is empty.
  .remarks
    Price belongs to the _Slot_, not the _Product_: the same drink can cost more in the front row.
  }
  ```
  - Context: applying a shared Rule inside a Skill.
  - Put `\_Custom_Rule_.contract†` inline in a sentence — BAD.
  - Put it alone below an optional Apply heading — GOOD.
  - Execute an action merely because its instructions were expanded — BAD.
  - Apply the expanded guidance under the caller's stated conditions — GOOD.

  ```trm
  .relations
    applies _Custom_Rule_
  .contract
    ### Apply the shared Rule

    _Custom_Rule_.contract†
  ```
}


explanation _Viewpoint_Usage_Guide_ in usage = {
  A guide to how a Knowledge selects bound Viewpoints and interprets their Term Kinds, sections and reading guidance.
.explanation
  - The first directive declares @knowledge <id>; the next declares Viewpoints, such as `@viewpoints skill`.
  - A Knowledge may name several Viewpoints, including vocabularies with overlapping Term Kind names.
  - Qualify overlapping names: `skill.procedure` selects the skill Viewpoint; `domain.procedure` selects domain.
  - Qualification must name a Viewpoint already declared by @viewpoints.
  - Its filename does not choose its vocabulary.
  - aterm.yaml binds each Viewpoint name to a file.
  - A Knowledge may use only bound Viewpoints.
  - A Viewpoint offers Term Kinds, sections, questions and writing guidance.
  - Every Term Kind shares the same structural .relations section outside its content Schema.
  - The generic Viewpoint offers only Term Kind term.
  - Generic definition is the unheaded body.
  - Generic classification is optional free-form category text.
  - Classification labels such as concept or policy do not select another Term Kind.
  - Generic description is optional explanatory content.
  - The skill Viewpoint offers skill, explanation, principle, rule, constraint, procedure and resource.
  - Step is a role within a Procedure, not a separate Term Kind.
  - A Skill need not prescribe a Procedure.
  - A Skill's description is discovery text.
  - Its explanation supplies explanatory content and examples supplies contextual comparisons.
  - The .relations section holds formal Relations; contract holds obligations and procedure holds action order when actions are prescribed.
  - Section daggers insert selected reading context; whole-Term daggers append complete Term Declarations.
  - Neither form prescribes execution.
.validation
  - Compare declared Term Kinds and sections with `aterm viewpoint view <name>`.
.examples
  - Context: classifying a Term in the generic Viewpoint.
  - Declare Term Kind policy because classification says policy — BAD.
  - Declare Term Kind term and write policy in classification — GOOD.
  - `aterm viewpoint list` lists the bound vocabularies.
  - `aterm viewpoint view skill` prints this vocabulary's original Markdown, including frontmatter and guidance.
}


skill.procedure _Corpus_Query_Procedure_ in usage = {
  A Procedure for selecting and running corpus read queries that answer the stated information need, then reading the applicable content and context.
.relations
  references _Query_Service_
  uses _GraphQL_Query_
  uses _Discover_Terms_
  uses _Prepare_Semantic_Index_
  applies _Reference_Integrity_Rule_
  uses _Corpus_Notation_Guide_
  uses _Command_Reference_
  references _vending_machine:Coin_
  references _vending_machine:Product_
  references _vending_machine:Purchase_Product_
  references _vending_machine:Slot_
.inputs
  - Identify the question, known phrase or partial Term name.
  - Select a Home using normal discovery or an explicit --home path.
  - If the Home or intended subject is unclear, establish it before interpreting results.
.contract
  - Read matching follows _Query_Service_. Use --case-sensitive when exact case is part of the question; preserve returned canonical spelling when authoring references.
  - Use _GraphQL_Query_ when a read needs selected fields or nested Relation traversal; check response errors and pagination before interpreting its excerpt.
  - Apply _Reference_Integrity_Rule_ to resolve exact names before using them in follow-up queries.
  - Use _Corpus_Notation_Guide_ to read the Term Declaration's meaning and scope before acting on its content.
  - Consult _Command_Reference_ for options outside the examples.

  #### Reference Integrity

  **Applicability**

  _Reference_Integrity_Rule_.applicability†

  **Contract**

  _Reference_Integrity_Rule_.contract†

  **Validation**

  _Reference_Integrity_Rule_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Reference_Integrity_Rule_.examples†

  #### Command Reference

  **Location**

  _Command_Reference_.location†

  **Explanation**

  _Command_Reference_.explanation†

  **Usage**

  _Command_Reference_.usage†

  **Validation**

  _Command_Reference_.validation†
.explanation
  - Use _Discover_Terms_ when the intent is known but the canonical Term is not.
  - `aterm corpus discover 'compare several concepts together' --limit 5 --output json` returns ranked candidates and section evidence.
  - `aterm corpus discover 'compare several concepts together' --search-in definition` ranks only Definition chunks; default output is a Term / Score table. Use --detail to read Definitions and source evidence.
  - Use _Prepare_Semantic_Index_ with `aterm corpus index build` once before semantic queries; `corpus index status` reports cache readiness without inference.
  - Model download is local preparation. Questions and corpus text remain local; subsequent queries need no network.
  - `--mode lexical` uses word matching without a Model. Do not silently substitute it after semantic failure.
  - Rank and similarity are retrieval evidence, never confidence or an authored Relation.
  - Read a candidate with `term view <qualified-id>` before relying on its contract; follow explicit Relations with graph commands.
  - In overview, an incoming arrow points toward the selected Term.
  - `_vending_machine:Purchase_Product_ dispenses from _vending_machine:Slot_` appears as an incoming Relation for Slot.
  - `_vending_machine:Slot_ contains _vending_machine:Product_` appears as an outgoing Relation for Slot.
  - Context: a corpus and TypeScript source must both be searched.
  - `aterm corpus search Query --external 'core=src/**/*.{ts,tsx}'` selects external text explicitly.
  - Top-level externalSources can select it by default for `corpus search` and `term occurrences`.
  - Explicit external options replace the configured selection.
  - Named globs resolve relative to the Workspace or use absolute paths.
  - External results carry physical locations and labels such as ext.core.
  - External files remain outside the corpus and are unaffected by corpus filters.

.procedure
  1. Choose the query from the purpose table below.
  2. Quote any glob and run the query against the intended Home.
  3. If no match identifies the subject, refine the phrase or pattern rather than inventing a name.
  4. Read the full matching Term Declaration and scope before narrowing to a section for implementation work.
  5. Read required context with view when the task needs the Term Declaration's dependencies.
.outputs
  - Return exact Term names, matching source locations or Term Declaration content appropriate to the question.
  - Distinguish an empty result from a tool error.
.validation
  - Confirm that the result answers the requested question in the intended Home.
  - Do not treat a summary as the complete structured result.
.examples
  | Need | Command |
  | --- | --- |
  | A remembered phrase | `aterm corpus search denomination` |
  | A partial name | `aterm term list '*Coin*'` |
  | File / Group / Term Declaration organization | `aterm term list --knowledge vending_machine --tree` |
  | Matching branches with Term Declaration locations | `aterm term list '*Slot*' --knowledge vending_machine --tree --with-fileline` |
  | The complete Term Declaration of one Term | `aterm term show _vending_machine:Coin_` |
  | Term Declarations and required context | `aterm term view _vending_machine:Coin_` |
  | Incoming and outgoing Relations | `aterm graph overview _vending_machine:Slot_ --edges relation` |
  | Lines mentioning a Term | `aterm term occurrences _vending_machine:Coin_` |
  | Complete structured output | `aterm term view --output json _vending_machine:Coin_` |
  | One section after a full read | `aterm term show _vending_machine:Coin_ --section definition` |
.remarks
  - Chosen: the examples assume the shared Sample is present for Coin and Slot queries.
}


skill.procedure _Corpus_Change_Procedure_ in usage = {
  A Procedure for editing corpus content while preserving references, synchronizing affected specifications and verifying affected behavior.
.relations
  applies _Reference_Integrity_Rule_
  applies _Specification_Synchronization_Rule_
  uses _Corpus_Notation_Guide_
.inputs
  - Identify the intended change and the owning Term Declarations.
  - Read their full content and Knowledge scopes before narrowing the edit.
.contract
  - Apply _Reference_Integrity_Rule_ to preserve Term identities and close reference warnings before handoff.
  - Apply _Specification_Synchronization_Rule_ when the task implements, changes or reviews behavior.
  - Follow _Corpus_Notation_Guide_ to preserve each Term Kind's declared section structure.

  #### Reference Integrity

  **Applicability**

  _Reference_Integrity_Rule_.applicability†

  **Contract**

  _Reference_Integrity_Rule_.contract†

  **Validation**

  _Reference_Integrity_Rule_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Reference_Integrity_Rule_.examples†

  #### Specification Synchronization

  **Applicability**

  _Specification_Synchronization_Rule_.applicability†

  **Contract**

  _Specification_Synchronization_Rule_.contract†

  **Validation**

  _Specification_Synchronization_Rule_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Specification_Synchronization_Rule_.examples†

  **Remarks**

  _Specification_Synchronization_Rule_.remarks†
.explanation


  #### Reading Notation

  **Explanation**

  _Corpus_Notation_Guide_.explanation†

  **Validation**

  _Corpus_Notation_Guide_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Corpus_Notation_Guide_.examples†
.procedure
  1. Run `aterm corpus check` before touching corpus files.
  2. Record existing diagnostics and repair them within the task scope; do not bury them in the intended change or claim an invalid corpus is clean.
  3. Select edit for content changes, rename for a local name, move for a Knowledge transfer or format for Relation ordering.
  4. Supply edit patches through stdin or --file; qualify Knowledge and Term Kind when needed. For a Group move, patch the complete Term Declaration opener.
  5. For external renames, review a combined --dry-run diff before saving.
  6. Make the intended changes and inspect any diagnostics.
  7. Run `aterm corpus check` again and fix every diagnostic introduced by the change.
  8. Inspect `aterm corpus diff` and the implementation diff where applicable.
  9. Run behavior-relevant verification before handoff.
.outputs
  - Return checked corpus changes and identify affected Term Declarations.
  - Report verification and any unresolved scope gaps.
.validation
  - The final corpus has no duplicate Term Declarations, unresolved references or invalid Relation declarations.
  - A Group-only change preserves Term Declaration identities and Relations; compare the selected tree before and after.
  - Every affected behavior satisfies the synchronization Rule.
.examples
  - `aterm term edit` receives a patch on stdin and locks the Home while validating the candidate.
  - New unresolved references are draft warnings rather than a finished model.
  - `*** Update Term: \_Order_.contract` selects a section when the Term Declaration is unambiguous.
  - `*** Update Term: skill.procedure \_Order_.procedure` selects the exact Viewpoint and Term Kind.
  - A short Term Kind selector is allowed only when it identifies one Term Declaration of the selected Term.
  - `aterm term rename \_Old_ \_New_` updates declarations and references together.
  - `aterm term rename \_Old_ \_New_ --external 'core=src/**/*.{ts,tsx}' --dry-run` previews external replacements too.
  - Omit --dry-run only after reviewing the intended diff.
  - External rename matches qualified Term tokens in comments, strings and code. Unqualified tokens remain literal text.
  - It does not infer code identifiers such as OldName.
  - Selected external files must be UTF-8 text; symlinks are not followed.
  - `aterm term move \_source:Term_ --to destination --dry-run` previews a transfer into an existing Knowledge.
  - Declare the same Viewpoint-qualified Term Kinds in the destination before move.
  - Move preserves the Term Kind owner, sections and Groups; it adds a qualifier if the destination makes a short Term Kind ambiguous.
  - The Term Declaration and incoming, outgoing and configured external references move together.
  - `aterm term format` sorts Relation assertions.
  - Context: two Knowledges declare the same local Term name.
  - Use an ambiguous unqualified edit selector — BAD.
  - Use the Knowledge-qualified Term in the selector — GOOD.
}


skill.procedure _Modeling_Method_Application_Procedure_ in usage = {
  A Procedure for selecting and applying the appropriate Term-modeling or Viewpoint-authoring method through a completed Report and structurally checked model.
.relations
  uses _Modeling_Method_Reference_
  uses _Viewpoint_Authoring_Method_
  applies _Reference_Integrity_Rule_
  references _aterm_skills:Modeling_Skill_
.inputs
  - Identify the source account and the subject to model.
  - Identify the intended Knowledge and its Viewpoints where they already exist.
.contract
  - Use the complete method from _Modeling_Method_Reference_ when creating Terms from a feature, document or conversation.
  - Use the complete method from _Viewpoint_Authoring_Method_ when creating or revising a Viewpoint vocabulary.
  - Apply _Reference_Integrity_Rule_ to reuse exact existing names and leave no unresolved references in the delivered model.

  #### Modeling Method Reference

  **Location**

  _Modeling_Method_Reference_.location†

  **Contract**

  _Modeling_Method_Reference_.contract†

  **Explanation**

  _Modeling_Method_Reference_.explanation†

  **Usage**

  _Modeling_Method_Reference_.usage†

  **Validation**

  _Modeling_Method_Reference_.validation†

  **Remarks**

  _Modeling_Method_Reference_.remarks†

  #### Viewpoint Authoring Method

  **Location**

  _Viewpoint_Authoring_Method_.location†

  **Usage**

  _Viewpoint_Authoring_Method_.usage†

  **Validation**

  _Viewpoint_Authoring_Method_.validation†

  #### Reference Integrity

  **Applicability**

  _Reference_Integrity_Rule_.applicability†

  **Contract**

  _Reference_Integrity_Rule_.contract†

  **Validation**

  _Reference_Integrity_Rule_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Reference_Integrity_Rule_.examples†
.procedure
  1. For a new Viewpoint, read and follow the Viewpoint method before modeling its subject.
  2. For new Terms, read _aterm_skills:Modeling_Skill_ and the intended Viewpoint guidance.
  3. Use its relevant guidance and a reasoning method suited to the task; preserve supported meaning, explicit uncertainty and coverage.
  4. State the supported conclusion’s boundary and remaining unknowns; defer decisions and actions that depend on them.
  5. When saving is requested, use one .trm file per Knowledge and run `aterm corpus check`; a model-only task returns its supported result without writing.
.outputs
  - Return the completed Report and a structurally valid model.
  - Identify unresolved questions instead of claiming closure while they remain open.
.validation
  - The chosen method and Viewpoint match the modeling task.
  - The chosen method supports the required outcomes and preserves source meaning, Scope and structural constraints.
  - Completion claims name their boundary and evidence; saved content passes corpus check and does not hide pending decisions.
.examples
  - `aterm term view _aterm_skills:Modeling_Skill_ --output markdown` reads the corpus modeling Skill.
  - `aterm viewpoint view specification` supplies specification vocabulary.
  - `aterm viewpoint view skill` supplies Skill vocabulary.
  - Context: the Report still has a known unknown.
  - Hand over an apparently complete file and hide the question — BAD.
  - Resolve it or explicitly report the incomplete work — GOOD.
}


skill.procedure _Server_Configuration_Procedure_ in usage = {
  A Procedure for configuring and running the filesystem-watching and HTTP query service for an Aterm Home.
.inputs
  - Select the Home whose sources the server will track.
  - Select a listen address, port and intended debounce behavior.
.procedure
  1. Set server.port in aterm.yaml; the initialized default is 43127.
  2. Keep server.host at its default 127.0.0.1 for local access, or set 0.0.0.0 for external IPv4 access. Use :: for IPv6 or a concrete local IP to select one interface.
  3. Set server.debounceMs to batch bursts of filesystem changes.
  4. Configure named externalSources globs when source text should be searched.
  5. Set server.watchExternal to false only when background source tracking is unwanted.
  6. Run `aterm server run`, or `aterm mcp run --with-server` for a client-managed stdio process, from the selected project or with an explicit --home.
  7. Open http://localhost:PORT locally or http://SERVER_IP:PORT remotely. Remote clients read the configured corpus without authentication.
  8. Leave loopback CLI control at its default, or configure server.controlToken with at least thirty-two characters to allow authenticated external controllers.
  9. For invalid configuration, start with explicit --home and --port, then repair through file read/write.
  10. Stop the server with Ctrl-C and restart it after changing its host, port or controlToken. Browser tabs reconnect with their existing UUIDs.
.outputs
  - The server tracks filesystem changes and serves read queries over HTTP.
  - CLI read queries use the server when available and otherwise read the filesystem.
  - Automatic local CLI and standalone stdio writes use the filesystem; hosted stdio, HTTP MCP and --server commands execute against the server.
  - Connect remote MCP clients to /mcp; place authentication and HTTPS at a proxy. Set server.publicOrigin to its exact external origin.
.validation
  - A changed Knowledge becomes visible through a later read after the debounce interval.
  - After a filesystem write, an immediate server read may return the previous snapshot; compare a newer server revision before judging a just-written change stale.
  - Hosted stdio and HTTP MCP writes publish their saved state before completion.
  - External `corpus search` and `term occurrences` remain available without the server.
  - Disabling background source tracking does not disable on-demand external search.
.examples
  - `server: { host: 127.0.0.1, port: 43127, debounceMs: 200, watchExternal: true }`
  - External IPv4 access: `server: { host: 0.0.0.0, port: 43127 }`.
  - `externalSources: { core: 'packages/core/src/**/*.ts' }`
  - Context: configure external sources for both standalone and server use.
  - Put source selection under server — BAD.
  - Use top-level externalSources — GOOD.
}


skill.procedure _Explorer_Presentation_Procedure_ in usage = {
  A Procedure for presenting Terms and an accompanying explanation in an existing browser Session and verifying command delivery and the resulting state.
.relations
  uses _Explorer_
  uses _Server_Configuration_Procedure_
  selects _UI_Session_
  applies _Control_UI_
  changes _Working_Set_
  sends _Markdown_Note_
.inputs
  - A task to show, compare or explain corpus Terms in the browser.
  - The target Home, its matching running server and a connected browser tab.
  - Use _Server_Configuration_Procedure_ if the intended Home has no running server.
.explanation
  - Term Declarations, References and Graph Explore share one ordered _Working_Set_.
  - Tree Term-name click replaces membership; a checkbox adds or removes membership.
  - Knowledge and Group checkboxes act on all matching descendants, including collapsed ones.
  - Reader links open Context; dragging a Term into the Main View adds it to the Working Set.
  - Graph Selection is independent of Working Set membership.
  - Structure shows the corpus hierarchy; Explore shows the Working Set as flat Term Nodes.
  - Context and Note share the right reader beside the current Main View.
  - A Session UUID identifies a tab across reload and reconnect; it does not persist Graph geometry.
  - Session lists sort by last active; last seen also advances on heartbeat contact.
.procedure
  1. Select the intended Home through the project directory or --home; use the project's prescribed local executable.
  2. Run `aterm ui session list`; inspect location, status, visibility and activity to find the intended tab.
  3. Run `aterm ui session view <session> --output json` to confirm its view, terms, reading target and readiness.
  4. Resolve the task's target from existing context; clarify only if it remains ambiguous, rather than guessing from recency alone.
  5. Pass its full UUID or unique prefix of at least six hexadecimal characters to --session, with exact qualified Term IDs.
  6. Choose the operation below; for a Graph explanation, set the Terms and wait for applied before sending the Note.
  7. When an explanation is requested, send it with `aterm ui note send --session <session> --file explanation.md --title 'Explanation'`.
  8. Treat only applied as success; inspect `aterm ui command view <command-uuid>` after an unknown outcome before deciding to retry.
  9. Inspect the resulting Session state and report what was shown.

  | Operation | Effect |
  | --- | --- |
  | `ui open TERM` | Replace the Working Set with one Term; open Term Declarations and Context |
  | `ui explore set TERMS...` | Replace the Working Set; select, arrange and fit in Explore |
  | `ui explore add TERMS...` | Add Terms; preserve surviving positions and camera |
  | `ui explore remove TERMS...` | Remove Terms; preserve surviving positions and camera |
  | `ui explore clear` | Empty the Working Set; preserve the reading target and camera |
  | `ui note send` | Cache and open a Note; preserve the Main View and Graph |
.outputs
  - An acknowledged browser state and, when requested, an accompanying Markdown Note.
  - A command UUID for inspecting its delivery outcome.
.validation
  - UI commands require a live server; they have no filesystem fallback and never open a browser tab by themselves.
  - Term inputs must be exact identities; UI commands do not accept globs or --knowledge.
  - After busy or disconnected, inspect readiness before issuing a new request.
  - Reuse --command-id with the same payload to inspect the existing record without redelivery.
  - Never send a new UUID blindly after timeout; after expiry or restart, compare browser state before repeating an action.
  - Confirm view, mode and ordered terms; term is the independent Context target, not Working Set membership.
  - Explore commands activate Explore and retain Context; membership changes support Undo and never add neighbors automatically.
  - A Note acknowledgement confirms caching and reader opening; Mermaid rendering may still be in progress.
  - Note input uses --file or stdin when omitted or -; qualified Term tokens become links, while unqualified tokens remain literal.
  - Notes cache the latest ten items per origin and Home; the Note tab opens history after reload.
  - Notes are explanations, not corpus updates or saved Graph workspaces; opening an old Note does not restore its captured view.
.examples
  - Replace a1b2c3 with the inspected Session UUID or unique prefix before running these examples.
  ```sh
  aterm ui session list
  aterm ui session view a1b2c3 --output json
  aterm ui open _vending_machine:Vending_Machine_ --session a1b2c3
  aterm ui explore set _vending_machine:Vending_Machine_ _vending_machine:Inventory_System_ --session a1b2c3
  aterm ui note send --session a1b2c3 --title 'Machine and inventory' <<'MARKDOWN'
  # Machine and inventory
  - _vending_machine:Vending_Machine_ coordinates the machine.
  - _vending_machine:Inventory_System_ organizes product stock and restocking.
  - Click a Term to read Context; drag it into the Main View to compare it.
  MARKDOWN
  aterm ui session view a1b2c3 --output json
  ```
  - Build an Explore graph, then use ui open to explain one Node — BAD: this replaces its Working Set.
  - Build an Explore graph, then send a Note with qualified Term links — GOOD: the graph stays visible.
}


skill.procedure _Skill_Installation_Management_Procedure_ in usage = {
  A Procedure for installing, updating and removing Home-managed Skill documents and their configured Agent connections, including conflict and failure recovery.
.inputs
  - Select the source Home before running a lifecycle command.
  - Inspect configured skills.sync and skills.uninstall commands before customizing them.
.contract
  - Preserve unmanaged files and user-modified managed Skill documents when conflicts occur.
  - Do not treat a failed command as successful installation.
  - Do not infer ownership of legacy Agent installations.
.explanation
  - Install every Skill in the combined package and Home catalog; supporting Term Kinds are not installed.
  - Each installed Skill has name and authored description frontmatter followed by its TOC; read actual bodies with skill view.
  - Edit source Skill Term Declarations and their supporting Terms; edit an authored Guide only when it is the Skill's source. Never edit generated Skill documents or manifest hashes.
  - Installed content is a generated snapshot; skill update refreshes the entire document from the source.
  - Updating files does not replace instructions already loaded into an Agent session.
  - Discover Skills with skill list; their TOCs list prerequisite reading and the selected Skill.
  - Supporting Term Kinds remain in the corpus; each skill Term Declaration is installed, including shared prerequisite knowledge.
  - Skill bodies teach reusable knowledge or application guidance.
  - Top-level skills.sync and skills.uninstall are Bash command strings.
  - Init supplies project-scoped installation to Claude Code and Codex through npx skills.
  - Default removal covers managed names across all Agents in that project.
  - This removes stale shared copies and may remove same-name connections from another Agent.
  - Omit both sync and uninstall for local Skill document generation only; discovery and reading use the same catalog.
  - Configured commands receive absolute paths and current or removed names through ATERM environment variables.
  - Consult lifecycle help and its contract before changing those commands.
  - A failed command leaves a pending manifest for recovery.
  - Unmanaged and user-modified files cause conflicts rather than silent overwrite.
  - Changing source does not itself alter Agent installations; run skill update to migrate the managed set and inspect synchronization.
.procedure
  1. Choose install for a new managed installation, update for synchronization or uninstall for removal.
  2. Run the selected lifecycle command against the source Home.
  3. Inspect local Skill document changes and any configured command output.
  4. On a conflict, inspect the named files before deciding how to resolve it.
  5. On command failure, retain the pending manifest and correct the cause.
  6. Retry update or uninstall when ready to reconcile the incomplete operation.
.outputs
  - Managed pointers live in the selected Home's skills directory, normally .aterm/skills.
  - The manifest records the managed installation inventory.
  - Update synchronizes names, descriptions and complete bodies with the current corpus.
  - Uninstall removes managed Skill documents and the Agent installations managed by its recorded commands.
.validation
  - Confirm the selected Home and the reported managed files.
  - Do not claim successful Agent synchronization after a configured command fails.
.examples
  - `aterm skill install` creates a new managed installation.
  - `aterm skill update` synchronizes the existing installation.
  - `aterm skill uninstall` removes the managed installation.
  - Use `aterm --home /path/to/.aterm skill update` from another project.
  - Context: a configured installer failed after local Skill document changes.
  - Delete the manifest and claim completion — BAD.
  - Correct the cause and retry reconciliation — GOOD.
}


resource _Command_Reference_ in usage = {
  The CLI help and command index used to select Aterm operations and their supported inputs.
.relations
  references _GraphQL_Query_
  references _Jq_Query_
  references _aterm_skills:Aterm_Basics_Skill_
  references _aterm_skills:Modeling_Skill_
  references _Query_Service_
  uses _Explorer_Presentation_Procedure_
  references _aterm_skills:Corpus_Authoring_Skill_
.location
  - `aterm --help` lists top-level commands.
  - `aterm <command> --help` describes syntax, options, behavior and recovery.
.explanation
  Read matching follows _Query_Service_: default case-insensitive, --case-sensitive for exact case.
  Example: `aterm term list '*skill*'` finds Skill names; `aterm term list '*Skill*' --case-sensitive` keeps exact case.

  | Command | Purpose |
  | --- | --- |
  | `corpus query` | GraphQL field selection and Relation traversal; jq root for section-value queries |
  | `corpus jq` | jq over the selected corpus JSON array; --knowledges selects input; output is an array of emitted values |
  | `term list` | Term Declaration selection by exact Term name or quoted glob; --tree shows File / Group / Term Declaration hierarchy; --unreferenced finds Term Declarations without incoming references |
  | `corpus search` | Literal phrase search in Term Declaration names and text; optional external text |
  | `corpus discover` | Ranked Term / Score table; --search-in definition restricts content; --detail shows Definitions and evidence; --mode lexical/semantic/hybrid and --limit control retrieval |
  | `corpus index build / status` | Prepare the embedded Model and Vector Index, or inspect readiness without inference |
  | `term show` | Copyable Term Declaration blocks without required-context expansion |
  | `term view` | Term Declarations and transitively required context |
  | `graph overview` | Selected Terms and incoming or outgoing connections |
  | `graph connect` | A connecting subgraph for two to ten Terms |
  | `graph path` | Routes between two Terms |
  | `term occurrences` | Term occurrences with nearby source lines; optional external text |
  | `graph relations` | Authored Relations selected by Viewpoint, file or phrase |
  | `corpus check` | Syntax, identity and reference checking; --root adds reachability checking |
  | `corpus diff` | Aterm changes from a commit to the working tree |
  | `term edit` | Locked, validated patches to Terms or keyed sections |
  | `term move` | Move every Term Declaration of selected Terms to an existing Knowledge and update references |
  | `term rename` | Term declarations and references, including selected external text |
  | `knowledge rename` | Knowledge ID and qualified references; filenames stay unchanged |
  | `term format` | Relation assertion ordering |
  | `viewpoint create / edit / delete` | Validate and manage whole Viewpoint documents and bindings |
  | `knowledge create / edit / delete` | Validate and manage whole Knowledges |
  | `file list / read / write / delete` | Workspace text and invalid-state repair |
  | `viewpoint list / view` | List names and descriptions; read original Viewpoint Markdown files |
  | `skill list / toc / view / remind` | Discover Skills and read their bodies |
  | `skill install / update / uninstall` | Manage the selected Home's Skill documents |
  | `knowledge list / view` | Inspect Knowledge metadata, Scope and Term Declaration inventory |
  | init | A new Home, default configuration, shipped Viewpoints and docs source |
  | `mcp run` | Stdio MCP with normal CLI dispatch; --with-server hosts HTTP and Explorer in the same process |
  | `server run` | Filesystem watching, Explorer, read service and MCP /mcp |
  | `ui session list / view` | Discover browser Session UUIDs and inspect their current state |
  | `ui open` | Replace the Working Set with one exact Term; open Term Declarations and Context in --session |
  | `ui explore set / add / remove / clear` | Change the shared Working Set in Explore; membership changes support Undo |
  | `ui command view` | Inspect a command UUID after timeout or connection loss |
  | `ui note send` | Send Markdown from --file or stdin to --session; cache the latest ten Notes in the browser |
  - `aterm skill view _aterm_skills:Aterm_Basics_Skill_` reads the packaged corpus usage Skill, including its conditional workflows.
  - `aterm skill view _aterm_skills:Modeling_Skill_ --viewpoint <name>` reads the packaged modeling Skill with the target vocabulary.
  - `aterm skill list` reports exact read IDs for methods, Guides and corpus Skills.
  - `aterm skill view ID` reads a discovered corpus Skill with section content expanded in place.

  ### Skill guidance
  - Select the project Home and local executable before requesting task guidance.
  - `aterm skill list` lists qualified Skill identities and activation descriptions.
  - `aterm skill remind _aterm_skills:Corpus_Authoring_Skill_` recalls shared writing guidance; `aterm viewpoint view skill` supplies Skill-specific authoring instructions.
.usage
  - Consult help before guessing an option.
  - Follow _Explorer_Presentation_Procedure_ for Session selection, Working Set commands, Note examples and delivery recovery.
  - Use --home to select an explicit Home when discovery is unsuitable.
  - Use --output json or --output yaml for complete structured results where the command returns a result.
  - Use --section on term show or term view only after reading the full Term Declaration and scope when implementing.
  - --output markdown prints compact Term Declaration and section headings, without Knowledge, Viewpoint, Scope, Group annotations or automatic Term links.
  - Generated Markdown ends with two empty lines; Skill reads default to that same rendering.
  - `--output markdown --section contract` reads only expanded Contract content.
  - --no-remark suppresses Remarks, including expansion of Remarks targets.
  - Every flat list uses an aligned table; term list --tree retains the authored Group hierarchy.
  - knowledge view separates metadata, Scope and Declarations; term view --detail also separates both reference directions.
.validation
  - Compare the command index with current CLI help.
  - Run example queries on the shared Sample when checking this guidance.
}


resource _Modeling_Method_Reference_ in usage = {
  The reading reference for the corpus modeling Skill and its selected vocabulary.
.relations
  uses _aterm_skills:Modeling_Skill_
.location
  - `aterm term view _aterm_skills:Modeling_Skill_ --output markdown`
  - `aterm viewpoint view <name>`
.contract
  - Run the Markdown read command in location and read the complete _aterm_skills:Modeling_Skill_ before applying the method.
  - This Resource supplies the read location; it does not inline another copy of that Skill.
.explanation
  - The Skill supplies questions, answer destinations, candidate discovery, closure and pruning.
  - The selected Viewpoint supplies admission criteria, Term Kinds, sections and questions.
  - The Report records decisions and closes known unknowns.
  - The finished file must pass corpus checking.
.usage
  - Substitute the Viewpoint the intended Knowledge declares.
  - Follow the Skill's control flow with that vocabulary.
.validation
  - The Skill view resolves every required instruction.
  - The Viewpoint read includes metadata and the guidance body.
.remarks
  - Chosen: the package entrypoint reads the same corpus Skill ; read the selected Viewpoint separately; there is no separately maintained modeling body.
}


resource _Viewpoint_Authoring_Method_ in usage = {
  The shipped method for authoring a vocabulary of Term Kinds, sections, questions and modeling guidance.
.relations
  references _aterm_skills:Viewpoint_Authoring_Skill_
.location
  - `aterm skill view _aterm_skills:Viewpoint_Authoring_Skill_`
.explanation
  - Read the authoritative Viewpoint authoring method.
.usage
  - Read the complete method before creating or revising a Viewpoint.
  - Use its admission and Keep criteria to make the intended modeling boundary explicit.
.validation
  - `aterm skill view _aterm_skills:Viewpoint_Authoring_Skill_` prints successfully.
.examples
  Use the owning Skill examples and the applicable Skill body section; source methods are provenance, not an extra reading prerequisite.
}


principle _Method_Selection_Principle_ in usage.information = {
  The principle that an Agent chooses a useful reasoning method for the task while preserving supported meaning, user constraints and evidence for its claims.
.applicability
  - Apply when using modeling, reading, authoring, review or operational guidance.
.contract
  - Treat reasoning sequences, question grids and report templates as recommended methods, not proof that thinking occurred.
  - Reorder, combine or replace reasoning steps when another approach better serves the task; retain the relevant questions and supported outcome.
  - Preserve user intent, Scope, canonical identity, schema and reference integrity, operation preconditions and required independent review.
  - Do not use methodological freedom to bypass version guards, verification, authorization or a supported behavioral contract.
  - Match investigation, reporting and verification effort to the change's impact and uncertainty. Reuse current evidence rather than repeat work to satisfy a route label.
  - Report material guidance gaps, contradictions and consequential departures with their practical impact; minor method choices need no approval ritual.
  - When a missing decision blocks an operation, pause that dependent operation and continue useful independent work within the authorized scope.
.explanation
  - Choose questions, examples, counterexamples, diagrams, experiments or another useful method from the available evidence.
  - A missing catalog entry does not require creating a Skill before making progress.
.validation
  - The result preserves required meaning and constraints, with evidence proportionate to the claim and explicit material uncertainty.
.examples
  - A State Transition example exposes the missing cancellation boundary before the question grid does — use it, then check relevant coverage.
  - Skip reference validation because the text looks plausible — BAD; validation is an operation constraint, not a thinking preference.
  - A misleading instruction blocks useful work — report the issue and use a supported alternative within scope.
}


rule _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_ in modeling = {
  The rule for distinguishing source-account evidence, authored choices and missing knowledge in modeling answers.
.contract
  - Answer from the account rather than from what similar systems usually do.
  - Record unsupported design decisions as Chosen instead of presenting them as source facts.
  - Write nothing when the account does not answer a question.
  - Write none when the account explicitly says there is no such thing.
  - Treat nothing as a known unknown; none is an account decision.
  - Record source-supported material excluded from Term admission as Given on its owner.
  - Record material retained from a chosen name as Chosen on its owner.
  - An absent scope does not grant unlimited implementation freedom.
  - Do not retain wording merely because it already existed; retain it because an answer needs it.
.validation
  - Each Given or Rules statement can be traced to the account or an explicit method rule.
  - Each decision unsupported by the account is visibly Chosen.
.examples
  - Context: the source does not say whether retry is allowed.
  - none — BAD: it asserts the source forbids retry.
  - nothing — GOOD: it preserves the missing decision.
  - Context: the source explicitly prohibits retry.
  - none — GOOD: the account decided there is no retry.
}


rule _Modeling_Closure_Rule_ in modeling = {
  The rule that a completion claim requires supported decisions and coverage for its stated boundary, while unresolved knowledge remains explicit.
.contract
  - State what the current model or operation claims to cover; assess closure for that boundary.
  - Treat structural stability as useful evidence, not sufficient proof of completeness or a mandatory question schedule.
  - Keep each material unknown Open until evidence or a supported Chosen, Non-goal or Dropped disposition addresses it; an open count closes nothing.
  - Resolve an unknown before authoring a Rule or performing an operation whose validity depends on its answer.
  - Supported independent facts and Rules may be saved alongside explicit uncertainty when Scope and the selected Schema permit it; preserve structural validity and existing contracts.
  - Do not call the whole model complete while in-scope unknowns remain. Report completed boundaries, pending questions and what each question blocks.
  - A model-only request returns evidence and drafts without authorizing writes. A request to save useful partial knowledge does not imply permission to invent missing decisions.
  - Expose consequential choices requiring user input instead of fabricating closure; continue authorized independent work.
.explanation
  - Unknowns constrain the claims and operations that depend on them, not every fact in the Knowledge.
.validation
  - Each completion claim has a clear boundary and supporting coverage and decisions.
  - Saved uncertainty is visible, permitted by the Schema and Scope, and not presented as an implementation commitment.
.examples
  - Save confirmed lodging and mark transport selection pending — GOOD when the travel Schema permits it.
  - Invent a bus departure to make Open: none — BAD.
  - Task identity is settled; retry policy is unknown — save the identity, defer the retry Rule and dependent implementation.
  - Stable names alone establish complete requirements — BAD.
.remarks
  Chosen: uncertainty is evaluated by its effect on a stated claim or operation. This replaces the former whole-model Open: none write gate; it does not relax schema validation, references or established behavioral contracts.
}


skill.procedure _Modeling_Account_Preparation_Procedure_ in modeling = {
  A Procedure for preparing a source-faithful account for modeling questions, retaining access to sources and describing their supported meaning and separating proposed decisions.
.relations
  applies _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_
.inputs
  - Identify whether the source is prose or a codebase, conversation or observed system.
.contract
  - Apply _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_ to preserve the distinction between observed facts and added decisions.

  #### Modeling Evidence

  **Contract**

  _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_.contract†

  **Validation**

  _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_.examples†
.procedure
  1. Preserve access to original sources and prepare a faithful account; quotation, synthesis, examples or a diagram may suit the task.
  2. Cover relevant inputs, results, changes, conditions and refusals without a sentence-count quota.
  3. Check every sentence against the source and strike unsupported statements.
  4. Compare every relevant original-source statement with the account or an explicit exclusion and reason; retain source locations and mark uninspected or unassessable material.
  5. Retain any deliberately adopted unsupported idea as a later Chosen decision rather than an account fact.
.outputs
  - Return the source-faithful account, source coverage and exclusions, unassessed material and separate proposed decisions.
.validation
  - Nothing enters the account that the source does not support.
  - Every relevant inspected source statement is retained or explicitly excluded with a reason; a sentence-count target cannot justify losing a condition.
.examples
  - Context: the source is an existing requirement paragraph.
  - Paraphrase it and quietly add normal product behavior — BAD.
  - Preserve its supported meaning and source location; mark added behavior Chosen — GOOD.
}


skill.procedure _Initial_Term_Identification_Procedure_ in modeling = {
  A Procedure for identifying and naming the initial subjects and acts that an account cannot do without, using the selected Viewpoint and existing vocabulary.
.relations
  follows _Consistent_Naming_Principle_
.inputs
  - Use the account and the loaded Viewpoint vocabulary.
.contract
  - Follow _Consistent_Naming_Principle_ to choose names that identify one meaning within their Knowledge.

  #### Consistent Naming

  **Applicability**

  _Consistent_Naming_Principle_.applicability†

  **Contract**

  _Consistent_Naming_Principle_.contract†

  **Validation**

  _Consistent_Naming_Principle_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Consistent_Naming_Principle_.examples†

  **Remarks**

  _Consistent_Naming_Principle_.remarks†
.explanation
  - A Knowledge is the namespace for its local Terms; qualified references identify Terms in another Knowledge.
  - A verb phrase can name a behavior. Commands, Events, criteria and reusable instructions need names identifying those subjects.
  - Do not infer the naming form from the declaration Term Kind; read what the Definition actually identifies.
  - Use the target Knowledge's naming convention; an experiment in another Knowledge does not change this one.
  - The later questions and coverage check complete the initial candidate set.
.procedure
  1. Identify indispensable nouns and verbs without trying to make the initial list complete. Establish the project purpose and core concepts as the naming context; test supporting names together for missing or redundant qualifiers before propagating their pattern.
  2. Run `aterm term list '*Noun*'` on the head noun before naming each seed or candidate.
  3. Reuse an existing canonical meaning rather than create a synonym.
  4. Distinguish a new meaning from a name already owned by another subject.
  5. Retain the semantic head identifying the subject, then add modifiers needed to distinguish it; Knowledge qualification can supply domain context.
  6. Check the name beside its Definition without using invoke, follow or read to supply a missing Command, criterion or guidance identity.
  7. Use a relevant code identifier as naming evidence when it supplies a supported distinction; do not copy incidental prefixes or grammatical form automatically.
  8. Give each seed a Term Kind offered by the selected Viewpoint.
.outputs
  - Return named seeds with provisional Term Kinds and their evidence.
.validation
  - Every seed has a supported meaning and an admissible provisional Term Kind.
  - Existing names were checked before new names were introduced.
}


skill.procedure _Term_Questioning_Procedure_ in modeling = {
  A Procedure for using Viewpoint questions and other useful probes to discover and check a candidate’s meaning, boundaries and missing knowledge.
.relations
  applies _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_
  has_part _Modeling_Answer_Interpretation_Procedure_
.inputs
  - Use the current candidates, account and selected Viewpoint.
.contract
  - Apply _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_ to preserve nothing, none, Given and Chosen distinctions in every answer.
  - Use _Modeling_Answer_Interpretation_Procedure_ to interpret evidence when it can change a modeling decision.

  #### Modeling Evidence

  **Contract**

  _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_.contract†

  **Validation**

  _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_.examples†
.explanation
  - Consider added Term Kind questions for affected Terms; skip repetitions already supported by current evidence.
  - Check what the questions missed; a repeated omission can reveal a guidance gap.
  - A refusal means the procedure does not run and changes nothing.
  - A negative result produced by a running procedure answers its output question, not its refusal question.
.procedure
  - Use the Viewpoint’s section questions as a coverage aid. The Schema fixes content destinations, not the order of reasoning.
  - Start with the question, example, counterexample or experiment most likely to clarify the current uncertainty.
  - Combine or reorder related questions and reuse supported answers; investigate applicable unanswered questions before relying on their conclusions.
  - Add practitioner questions when needed. Report recurring gaps and propose a Viewpoint improvement without making that update a prerequisite for the current authorized task.
  - Compare confusable candidates and inspect what the selected questions missed.
  - Record relevant answers and their destinations, identity changes and unknowns in a useful form.
  - Reconcile affected answers before relying on or saving the result; exploratory notes need not be rewritten after every thought.
  - Use _Modeling_Answer_Interpretation_Procedure_ for semantic distinctions; wording alone does not determine the model.
.outputs
  - Return answers with destinations, marked added questions, candidate changes and open knowledge gaps.
.validation
  - Relevant identity, behavior, boundary and coverage questions are supported or explicitly unresolved.
  - The caller receives newly discovered candidates and the uncertainty they introduce; checking coverage does not require replaying already answered questions.
.examples
  - Difference frame: `\_X_ differs from \_Y_ in ___`.
  - Missed-content frame: `The questions missed about \_X_: ___`.
  - Context: an account says a completed review may return findings.
  - Classify findings as refusal to review — BAD.
  - Record findings as a possible review result — GOOD.
}


skill.procedure _Modeling_Answer_Interpretation_Procedure_ in modeling = {
  A Procedure for classifying modeling answers into candidate changes, owner fields, Rules, Relations, distinctions and unresolved knowledge gaps.
.inputs
  - Take one answer, its destination section, the current names and earlier answers.
.contract
  - Treat answer shapes as diagnostic heuristics for Definition answers; identity, behavior and uses must justify a split, merge or State decision.
  - Use the selected Viewpoint's admission guidance before treating a phrase as a Term.
.explanation
  | Shape | Interpretation |
  | --- | --- |
  | Head noun of a definition answer | Category; not automatically a declaration Term Kind or a new Term |
  | Part excluded by the Viewpoint's admission guidance | Keep on its owner as Given rather than declare a Term |
  | A and B | Check for independent meanings; start and end can jointly define one interval |
  | A that B | Check ownership or meaningful specialization; an active subscriber can be a valid role |
  | A or B; A unless B | Check alternatives, subtypes, conditions or States from behavior; card or cash need not be temporal States |
  | Terms and fields stating must or must not | Record a Rule with the question's opening words |
  | A Term | Record a Relation for the later retelling |
  | Another admitted noun or verb phrase | Introduce a candidate with the admitted Term Kind |
  | nothing | Keep a known unknown Open |
  | none | Record the account's decision; no gap |
  | Whole answer duplicates another | Merge the duplicate meanings |
  | Whole difference answer establishes a distinction | Record Differs |
  | Whole answer promises less or more than the name | Rename to the supported meaning |
  - Name a new candidate using the account's noun for its subject.
  - A surviving name remains grounded in the account after a rename, split or merge.
.procedure
  1. Classify each answer part; identify a Definition's head noun as its category without ending interpretation of the whole answer.
  2. Inspect the whole Definition for combined meanings, borrowed properties and State alternatives; then compare the whole answer with the proposed name and other Terms' answers for duplicates and distinctions.
  3. Test a proposed rename, split or merge against supported meaning and representative uses; defer premature identity commitment.
  4. Reconcile affected earlier answers before relying on or saving the chosen model; keep exploratory alternatives identifiable in notes.
  5. Return changes and candidates to the caller; a candidate is not retained until pruning.
.outputs
  - Return candidate changes, owner fields, Rules, Relations, Differs statements and Open items.
.validation
  - Every answer part has a disposition or an explicit Open item.
  - Earlier and later answers consistently use the surviving identities.
.examples
  - Context: generic classification says procedure.
  - Automatically declare Term Kind procedure — BAD.
  - Follow the generic Viewpoint's admitted Term Kind and section meanings — GOOD.
}


skill.procedure _Modeling_Convergence_Procedure_ in modeling = {
  A Procedure for refining candidates until evidence supports the requested model boundary or identifies the questions that still block it.
.relations
  has_part _Term_Questioning_Procedure_
  uses _Unnamed_Subject_Detection_Guide_
  applies _Modeling_Closure_Rule_
  applies _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_
.inputs
  - Start with the current seeds, candidates, answers and Open items.
.contract
  - Use _Term_Questioning_Procedure_ to ask newly discovered candidates the relevant questions when needed.
  - Use _Unnamed_Subject_Detection_Guide_ to inspect all answers together at the end of each pass.
  - Apply _Modeling_Closure_Rule_ to distinguish a stable pass from the final Report's closure.

  #### Modeling Closure

  **Contract**

  _Modeling_Closure_Rule_.contract†

  **Validation**

  _Modeling_Closure_Rule_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Modeling_Closure_Rule_.examples†

  #### Modeling Evidence

  **Contract**

  _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_.contract†

  **Validation**

  _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Modeling_Evidence_Rule_.examples†
.explanation
  - Assess a provisional candidate by coherent identity, explanatory value, supported responsibilities and uses.
  - A useful candidate can expose more unknowns than it resolves. Unknown counts describe remaining work, not candidate quality.
  - Drop an unsupported or redundant candidate and revisit conclusions that relied on it; preserve material unknowns whose subjects still matter.

  > Discovering Reservation exposes expiry, cancellation and ownership questions — investigate; do not reject it because the count increased.

  - Use _Unnamed_Subject_Detection_Guide_ to inspect repeated unexplained conditions; these are clues, not automatic new Terms.
.procedure
  - Investigate new or materially changed candidates with relevant questions, examples and evidence.
  - Inspect shared responsibilities, missing Relations and source coverage as the model develops.
  - Revisit affected answers after meaningful identity or boundary changes; settle active references before application.
  - Assess provisional candidates by meaning and usefulness, not the number of unknowns they remove.
  - Continue while useful evidence can resolve consequential gaps within scope; return blocked questions when it cannot.
  - State the supported model boundary and remaining uncertainty under _Modeling_Closure_Rule_.
.outputs
  - Return the candidate set, answers, Relations, pass summaries and Report dispositions.
.validation
  - The claimed boundary is supported; further work and dependent operations are identified for material unknowns.
  - Dropping a candidate does not silently erase source obligations or unknowns that remain relevant.
.examples
  ```text
  pass N  <seeds> seeds + <candidates> candidates -> +New; rename Old -> New; or +0
          relations: +A/B; leak signals: none or <phrase>; open: <count>
  ```
}


skill.procedure _Candidate_Pruning_Procedure_ in modeling = {
  A Procedure for retaining only modeling candidates that earn an independent identity under the selected Viewpoint and recording the reasons for retention or removal.
.inputs
  - Use substantive answers and the Viewpoint's Keep table after the question loop.
.explanation
  - The criterion reads answers rather than future contract wording.
  - A category label, synonym or rationale alone does not establish independent content.
  - Explanatory content remains substantive even when its section is not named contract.
.procedure
  1. Ask of every seed and candidate whether it can be removed.
  2. Evaluate its answers using the Viewpoint's Keep rows.
  3. Keep it when any Keep row holds; drop it only when none holds.
  4. Record the verdict and supporting row for every candidate.
  5. Remove a dropped candidate’s Relations; reassign still-relevant unknowns and obligations to their supported owners instead of erasing them.
  6. Record Dropped with a reason, and Non-goal when an account sentence leaves coverage with it.
.outputs
  - Return retained Terms, Keep reasons and explicit drop or boundary records.
.validation
  - Every retained Term satisfies at least one Keep row.
  - Every dropped candidate leaves a trace explaining its removal.
.examples
  - Context: an Explanation supplies independently useful meaning.
  - Drop it merely because it is not an executable action — BAD.
  - Evaluate its substantive answers against the selected Keep rows — GOOD.
}


skill.procedure _Modeling_Coverage_Check_Procedure_ in modeling = {
  A Procedure for finding omitted meaning by retelling the pruned model and comparing it with the complete source account and Scope.
.inputs
  - Use retained Terms, recorded Relations, Rules, Given, Chosen and Non-goal lines.
.explanation
  - A rule need not appear as a Relation clause when it is already recorded on Rules.
  - A source sentence explicitly covered by Rules, Given or Non-goal is not an omitted Term.
  - Leak signals find content spread across answers; wholly omitted content may have no leak signal.
.procedure
  1. Retell the complete model within the stated review Scope using retained canonical Terms and their connections, and compare it with the original account. Scenarios, tables, diagrams and experiments can support this comparison without replacing the connected retelling.
  2. Make important subjects and their connections explicit; Term-verb-Term clauses are one useful form.
  3. Choose each Relation's verb explicitly during this retelling.
  4. When the Viewpoint admits actors and acts as Terms, use the actor as subject and the act as the verb Term.
  5. Treat an unnamed ordinary noun needed by a clause as a candidate gap.
  6. Compare every account sentence with the retelling and the Rules, Given and Non-goal records.
  7. Investigate uncovered meaning; it may require a Term, an owner field, a Rule or an explicit boundary rather than a new Term for every noun.
  8. Set the grid aside and state once what the whole account is about.
  9. If its subject or verb is not represented as a Term under the Viewpoint's admission rules, identify the gap.
  10. Ask once what a knowledgeable practitioner would name here that the questions missed.
  11. Mark those world-informed candidates Chosen and submit them to the same question and prune criteria.
  12. Compare scope obligations with the modeled content and explicit boundaries. After the caller resolves material gaps, repeat the retelling on the corrected whole rather than only the edited Terms.
.outputs
  - Return the retelling, whole-account statement and any gaps requiring another loop.
  - Return to the caller rather than invoke sibling stages or declare completion after finding a gap.
.validation
  - Every account sentence is represented or explicitly bounded out.
  - Every new candidate is returned for questioning and pruning before final declaration.
.examples
  - Whole-account frame: `This account is about how ___ ___ ___`.
  - Fluent retelling with an unexplained ordinary noun — BAD.
  - Check whether that noun reveals independent meaning or content missing from an existing owner — GOOD.
}


skill.procedure _Term_Declaration_Procedure_ in modeling = {
  A Procedure for writing retained identities, Definitions and Relation declarations as structurally valid Aterm Term Declarations.
.relations
  uses _Knowledge_Template_
.inputs
  - Use the retained Terms, selected Schemas, retelling and recorded distinctions.
.contract
  - Follow _Knowledge_Template_ for source notation rather than copying rendered annotations as directives.

  #### Knowledge Template

  **Contract**

  _Knowledge_Template_.contract†

  **Explanation**

  _Knowledge_Template_.explanation†

  **Usage**

  _Knowledge_Template_.usage†

  **Validation**

  _Knowledge_Template_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Knowledge_Template_.examples†
.explanation
  - A Relation can join two procedures.
  - is_a expresses Generalization; it does not copy Contracts or infer edges.
  - has_part expresses Parthood; has_member expresses Membership; neither implies lifecycle ownership.
  - has_state declares a permitted State in the Source's state space; it does not select a current or initial State, infer transitions or enforce exactly-one activation.
  - Use a built-in only when the evidence supports its meaning; otherwise keep an ordinary phrase.
  - A Term has one Term Declaration governed by its selected Term Kind Schema.
  - References identify the whole Term; a section selector chooses a present section of its single Term Declaration.
  - A whole-Term dagger appends the complete Term Declaration; a section dagger inserts only its selected content in Markdown.
  - Assign authored Group paths by the target Knowledge's organization; Group membership is independent of Parthood, Membership and other Relations.
.procedure
  1. Declare exactly one Term Declaration for each intended (Knowledge ID, local Term name) pair and select its Term Kind.
  2. Write each definition in one or two sentences from the supported answers.
  3. For each retelling Relation, place a phrase-and-target declaration on its active side in .relations.
  4. Write supported Rules whose required decisions are settled; leave dependent Rules pending and record permitted uncertainty explicitly.
  5. Declare every additional non-self reference in .relations; use references when no stronger connection is supported.
  6. Where a specific Term is a kind of a general Term, declare is_a and state the difference; when filling rule sections, insert the general Term's relevant rule section at the point of application.
  7. Validate the candidate with `aterm corpus check` or guarded authoring dry-run. Use an isolated corpus when needed to evaluate changed schemas or dependencies. Apply only within the task’s authorization and stated supported boundary.
.outputs
  - Return structurally checked Term Declarations with definitions and Relation declarations.
.validation
  - Each declaration follows its selected Term Kind's Schema.
  - The candidate corpus contains no duplicate (Knowledge ID, local Term name) pair or unresolved reference.
}


skill.procedure _Model_Term_Declaration_Completion_Procedure_ in modeling = {
  A Procedure for completing model Term Declarations by placing every settled modeling answer in its corresponding section and verifying the resulting model.
.relations
  applies _Modeling_Closure_Rule_
  uses _Knowledge_Template_
.inputs
  - Require supported answers, explicit material unknowns and structurally checked Term Declarations; identify the boundary being completed.
  - For an existing model, preserve supported meaning and reassess decisions affected by new answers.
.contract
  - Apply _Modeling_Closure_Rule_ to reopen the modeling loop if new answers expose an unresolved identity or coverage gap.
  - Follow _Knowledge_Template_ to preserve notation and declared section order while filling the Term Declarations.

  #### Modeling Closure

  **Contract**

  _Modeling_Closure_Rule_.contract†

  **Validation**

  _Modeling_Closure_Rule_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Modeling_Closure_Rule_.examples†

  #### Knowledge Template

  **Contract**

  _Knowledge_Template_.contract†

  **Explanation**

  _Knowledge_Template_.explanation†

  **Usage**

  _Knowledge_Template_.usage†

  **Validation**

  _Knowledge_Template_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Knowledge_Template_.examples†
.explanation
  - A Viewpoint with no rule section can finish with descriptive answers alone.
  - Do not invent a remarks section in a Viewpoint that does not offer it.
  - Definitions and behavioral rules must not depend on unstated obligations hidden only in rationale.
  - Keep concise connections in .relations and place detailed rules in the Schema content sections.
.procedure
  1. If new answers expose an identity or coverage gap, resolve it before writing dependent Rules; supported independent content may proceed under _Modeling_Closure_Rule_.
  2. Put every answer into the section that asked its question.
  3. Write actual obligations as Subject must behavior when condition in a section that accepts obligations.
  4. Keep definitions and explanations descriptive rather than invent obligations.
  5. Record classification as authored category text rather than a new declaration Term Kind.
  6. Put rules about a Relation in the owning Term Declaration's rule section under an ordinary heading when useful.
  7. Write other obligations in their owning sections; add headings only when grouping helps the reader.
  8. Put the no-partial-effect rule in the refusing procedure's rule section.
  9. Mark Chosen and Non-goal rationale in the Viewpoint's provided explanatory section.
  10. Place any obligation arising from that rationale in its owning rule section as well.
  11. Preserve the same subject wording across a Term's bullets.
  12. Compare the Rules and Differs records with the filled Term Declarations and resolve any missing content.
  13. Deliver one complete .trm file per Knowledge, update existing owning Knowledges where applicable, and use qualified cross-Knowledge references; run `aterm corpus check` and fix diagnostics.
.outputs
  - Return the complete checked model with every supported answer represented.
.validation
  - Every answer has its proper destination and every declared section is permitted.
  - The accepted model’s substantive content is represented; permitted pending questions remain visible and blocked commitments are not asserted.
  - The full corpus passes structural checking and scope coverage has been reviewed again.
.examples
  - Context: a generic Term's category is procedure.
  - Turn the category into a behavioral requirement — BAD.
  - Place procedure in classification — GOOD.
}


explanation _Unnamed_Subject_Detection_Guide_ in modeling = {
  A guide to recognizing repeated answer shapes that may reveal an unnamed modeling subject.
.explanation
  | Repeated signal | Possible missing subject | Follow-up |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | An unnamed phrase carrying state, quantity or a rule | Another subject lacks a name | Test the phrase against relevant questions and uses |
  | The same unless, except or only when condition | A state lacks an explicit owner or identity | Revisit the definition with the A-or-B row |
  | A state change expressed through nested conditions | Its states are not explicit | Ask the definition question for each branch |
  - Repetition is the signal, not the length of one Term's answers.
  - An actor or place repeated across answers is not automatically a Term.
  - Apply the Viewpoint's admission and Keep rules before retaining any candidate.
  - These signals expose leaked content, not content omitted without a trace.
.validation
  - Each proposed candidate has substantive evidence beyond repeated wording alone.
.examples
  - One detailed answer — not by itself a leak signal.
  - The same unexplained retry condition in several answers — a candidate leak signal.
}


resource _Modeling_Viewpoint_Reference_ in modeling = {
  A reading reference to the selected Viewpoints’ Term Kinds, ordered sections, questions, examples and modeling guidance.
.location
  - `aterm viewpoint view <name>`
  - Supply each Viewpoint declared by the intended Knowledge.
.usage
  - Read the metadata and guidance body before modeling.
  - Use section questions as coverage guidance; a frame helps express an answer and locate its destination, without fixing reasoning order.
  - Every Term Kind shares the structural .relations section; Viewpoint questions govern content sections.
  - Treat examples as demonstrations of shape and detail, not as subject content to copy.
  - Use What becomes a Term to distinguish candidates from content kept on its owner.
  - Use What earns a Term as the Keep table for pruning.
  - Do not infer Term Kinds from category words or assume a fixed section vocabulary.
.validation
  - The selected names are bound in the intended Home.
  - Every Knowledge declaration uses compatible selected vocabularies.
.examples
  - `aterm viewpoint view skill` supplies the Skill vocabulary.
  - `aterm viewpoint view generic` supplies generic's single Term Kind and free-form classification.
}


resource _Modeling_Report_Template_ in modeling = {
  An optional detailed report template for making retained meaning, evidence, decisions and closure inspectable.
.location
  - The template is embedded in this Term Declaration's explanation section.
.explanation
  ```text
  Terms:     retained Terms grouped by Term Kind
  Relations: every retelling clause, one per line
  Rules:     every must/must-not answer with its question's opening words, marked given or chosen
  Differs:   every established distinction
  Given:     account or method facts retained on owners outside Term admission
  Chosen:    each decision unsupported by the account
  Non-goal:  each account sentence deliberately outside the model
  Renamed:   Old -> New
  Split:     One -> A + B
  Merged:    A + B -> One
  Dropped:   rejected candidates and their reasons
  Asked:     each added + question and whether it yielded a Term, Relation, rule, field, other substantive section content or nothing
  Open:      each nothing without a Chosen, Non-goal or Dropped disposition
  ```
  - Given records what the account or the method decided.
  - Chosen records what the modeler decided and leaves it open to revision.
  - A chosen Rules line also counts as Chosen.
  - One explicit decision may close several known unknowns.
  - Non-goal is a chosen coverage boundary.
  - Use the fields useful for the task; a short change can use a concise evidence summary. Preserve material decisions, identity changes, boundaries and unknowns without a mandatory report shape.
  - End a modeling report with a connected explanation of the resulting model using canonical Terms, relevant conditions and Open boundaries; an inventory, diff or linked artifact does not replace that explanation. Scale it to the modeled scope, including a bounded revision.
  - An open count records how much is unresolved; it closes nothing.
  - Asked can reveal questions worth adding to the Viewpoint.
.usage
  - Maintain the records as candidates change rather than reconstruct them from memory at the end.
  - Use Open: none only for a stated boundary with no remaining unknowns; otherwise name pending questions and dependent operations.
.validation
  - The records explain every identity change, removal, decision and remaining boundary.
  - The final Rules and Differs records match the delivered Term Declarations.
}


resource _Knowledge_Template_ in modeling = {
  A source-notation template for declaring one checked Aterm Knowledge rather than a rendered read view.
.relations
  uses _Corpus_Notation_Guide_
.contract
  - Follow _Corpus_Notation_Guide_ for the shared notation and reference rules while authoring.
.explanation
  - A source file ends in .trm and declares @knowledge <id>, then @viewpoints after any leading // comments.
  - Optional @description and @scope blocks follow @viewpoints in either order, before Term Declarations.
  - Description tells readers what this Knowledge contains; Scope tells writers what belongs here and what must be covered.
  - A Term Declaration opens and closes at column zero; body content is indented two spaces.
  - Declare a local Term name and optionally in group.path before =; each Group segment uses lowercase_snake_case.
  - A Term has one authored Group path on its Term Declaration; that path never changes identity or implies Relations.
  - Inspect organization with `term list --tree` and change existing membership through a complete-Term Declaration edit.
  - The unheaded definition is nonempty and later sections follow the selected Schema order.
  - A .relations declaration has a built-in name or one-to-five-word phrase followed by a target; its source is the owning Term.
  - Declare the whole target in .relations; insert `\_Target_.contract†` alone in the content section where its rules apply.
  - Use the actual section key; body is not universal.
  - Read with --output markdown to expand selected content in place; a whole-Term dagger instead appends complete Term Declarations to view.
  - Use optional ### headings to group related content and a dash for bullets; .relations declarations use neither.
  - Name a referenced Term in the sentence that explains when or why it is used, rather than repeating the Relation as a heading.
  - Markdown # headings do not replace Knowledge directives or leading // comments.
  - Every recognized Term token must resolve; every non-self token must have a declaration in that Term Declaration's .relations. Write removed names as plain words.
  - A rendered // Viewpoint: or // Scope: line is an annotation, not a source directive.
  - Read output may combine Term Declarations from several Knowledges; preserve one .trm file per Knowledge and qualify cross-Knowledge references instead of copying the whole view into one file.

  ##### Reading Notation

  **Explanation**

  _Corpus_Notation_Guide_.explanation†

  **Validation**

  _Corpus_Notation_Guide_.validation†

  **Examples**

  _Corpus_Notation_Guide_.examples†
.usage
  - Substitute the target Knowledge's actual Viewpoints, scope, Term Kinds and Terms.
  - Resolve references to the rest of the selected corpus rather than create placeholder declarations.
.validation
  - The authored file passes `aterm corpus check` within its intended corpus.
.examples
  ```trm
  // # Sample declaration shape
  @knowledge example
  @viewpoints specification
  @description {
    Slot stock and Product kinds in a vending machine.
  }
  @scope {
    Describe the sample Slot's stock and Product association.
  }

  concept _Slot_ = {
    A stock position for one _Product_ kind.
  .relations
    contains _Product_
  .contract
    - A _Slot_ must contain at most one _Product_ kind.
    ### Stock
    - A _Slot_ stock count must not be negative.
  .remarks
    Chosen: the Slot owns its stock count.
  }

  concept _Product_ = {
    A kind of item identified by name.
  }
  ```
}


domain.undecided _Aterm_ in language = {
  _Aterm_ is a text format for modeling current knowledge through named Terms, their Term Declarations and Relations, stored in .trm files.
.relations
  applies _Schema_
  comprises _Term_Declaration_
  follows _Syntax_
  references _Contract_
  references _Definition_
  references _Reference_
  references _Relation_
  references _Remarks_
  references _Term_
  references _Vocabulary_
.invariants
  - Internal section structure and fixed filetypes must follow _Schema_.
  - Names and source blocks must follow _Term_ and _Term_Declaration_.
  - Cross-references must follow _Reference_ and semantic connections _Relation_, both resolving within one _Vocabulary_.
.rules
  - Every Term Declaration must begin with _Definition_. Its Term Kind selects the remaining sections from the declared _Schema_; Contract and Remarks are sections only where that Schema provides them.
  - Guidance may describe concepts, rules or procedures; no fixed section projection must be inferred from the .trm extension.
.boundary
  - A Term Declaration must declare its Term Kind explicitly. Classification text inside a section must not change its declared Term Kind or Schema.
  - _Aterm_ must give no meaning to code it contains, must not execute it, and must claim no semantic proof from structural validity.
.remarks
  _Definition_ supplies a stable vocabulary. _Contract_ attaches obligations
  to that vocabulary without hiding them in _Remarks_.
  The language Group owns notation; operations and graph own public tool behavior.
  Chosen: the Term Kind is deferred. A notation is not an entity, a value, an event or a
  policy; it is the subject the other Terms decompose.
}


entity _Vocabulary_ in language = {
  _Vocabulary_ is the set of Term Declarations across which one qualified name resolves to exactly one _Term_.
.relations
  has_member _Term_Declaration_
  references _Aterm_
  references _Reference_
  references _Relation_
  references _Term_
.invariants
  - A name must resolve to one Term with exactly one Term Declaration in one _Vocabulary_. Each (Knowledge ID, local Term name) pair must be unique; a different Term Kind, Viewpoint or Group must not permit another declaration of that Term.
  - A _Reference_, and both endpoints of a _Relation_, must resolve in the _Vocabulary_ holding the _Term_Declaration_ that makes them.
  - Membership changes as Term Declarations are added and removed; the _Vocabulary_ persists as the same one throughout.
.rules
  - Knowledge IDs must be flat, without hierarchy. A qualified name selects a Knowledge explicitly; underscores within a local name do not create nested scopes.
  - No Term Declaration must be reachable only through another Term Declaration; a qualified Term can be selected directly.
.boundary
  - What gathers the Term Declarations of a _Vocabulary_ is outside _Aterm_. The notation requires only that the set be decided and that (Knowledge ID, local Term name) pairs be unique in it.
.remarks
  Chosen: each Knowledge supplies one namespace. Local names are interpreted in that
  namespace; qualified identities remain stable across filename and path changes.
  Knowledge Space, hierarchical IDs and imports are deferred.
}


policy _Syntax_ in language = {
  _Syntax_ is the line-oriented grammar encoding Terms and Schema-keyed sections.
.relations
  applies _Schema_
  encodes _Term_Declaration_
  encodes _Group_
  encodes _Relation_
  references _Aterm_
  references _Definition_
  references _Term_Kind_
  references _Reference_
.invariants
  - Content section order and filetype validation must derive from _Schema_; the reserved structural .relations section must follow the shared notation grammar.
  - An omitted section must remain distinct from a present section with empty content.
  - Section meaning must depend on its key, never on inferred prose or headings.
.rules
  ### Term Declaration
  - A keyed opener at column zero must contain a Schema-permitted _Term_Kind_, an exact Term, an optional `in group.path` clause, `=`, and `{`. The Term Kind may have a Viewpoint qualifier, as in `skill.procedure \_Work_ = { ... }`; a separate Viewpoint field and a keyed filetype suffix are not permitted.
  - The optional in clause must follow the Term and precede = in keyed block, one-line and legacy openers. It must occur at most once.
  - A Group path must match [a-z][a-z0-9]*(?:_[a-z0-9]+)* for each dot-separated segment. Empty segments, whitespace, slashes and uppercase segments must be errors.
  - A one-line Term Declaration must place a nonempty _Definition_ and the closing `}` on the opener line after `{`; it must declare no other section.
  - Names must match `_[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*(?:_[A-Za-z0-9]+)*_` case-sensitively.
  - A block Term Declaration must close with column-zero `}`. Indented braces must remain content.
  - Nonblank body lines must have two leading spaces, which parsing removes.
  - Before Term Declarations, a Knowledge must declare `@knowledge <id>`, then `@viewpoints <name> [<name>…]` and may then declare one `@description {` block and one `@scope {` block, in either order. Both optional blocks must contain nonempty multiline prose with two leading spaces per nonblank body line and a column-zero closing brace; duplicate, misplaced, inline and unclosed blocks must be errors. Outside Term Declarations and these directives, only blank lines and column-zero `//` comments are valid.
  - An optional `// # Title` comment supplies the Knowledge title; otherwise use the filename stem.

  ### Sections
  - The first Schema section, `definition`, has no header: the content between the opener and the first section header, or between the braces of a one-line Term Declaration, is the `definition` section, and it must be nonempty.
  - An optional .relations section must follow definition and precede every Schema-defined content section. Every content section header must be a column-zero dot followed by its exact Schema key, without a filetype suffix, in Schema order and without duplicates. Unknown keys, duplicate .relations and reordered keys must be errors.
  - Every section after `definition` may be omitted or empty; missing sections require no placeholder.
  - `md` means Markdown; `ts` means opaque TypeScript; `yaml` and `json` mean structured values. Unsupported language labels must be errors.
  - Legacy backtick Term Declarations and `---md`/`---ts` positional separators must remain readable only when their filetypes match _Schema_. The opener's backtick count must delimit that legacy Term Declaration.

  ### Parsed form
  - A reading must retain the authored Term Kind spelling in `termKind` and the resolved owner in `viewpoint`. Term Kind identity must use the qualified Term Kind, independent of whether the opener used its short or qualified spelling.
  - Qualified Term Kinds must work in block, one-line and legacy Term Declarations, with or without a Group path.
  - A reading must retain an authored Group path as the optional group string on its Term Declaration; omission must remain absent.
  - A reading must retain each present section as `key`, `filetype`, `content` and the physical opener or separator `line`, together with its _Schema_.
  - A parsed Knowledge must retain each declared @description and @scope separately as content and inclusive physical line/endLine bounds. Neither block declares Term Declarations, scans Term references or contributes graph edges; omission must remain absent.
  - A reading must retain a declared `@scope` as its Markdown content with the inclusive physical bounds of its block, alongside the Term Declarations and separate from every Term Declaration's own source bounds.
  - A Knowledge must declare exactly one @knowledge <id> before @viewpoints. IDs must match [a-z][a-z0-9]*(?:_[a-z0-9]+)*; hierarchy and @import are not supported.
  - Declarations must use local Term names. References may use local spelling or qualified spelling such as `\_skill_foundations:Skill_`; an adjacent dagger marks either form required.
  - Structured sections must reject invalid syntax, duplicate mapping keys and YAML aliases.
  - Literal Term tokens in scalar values participate in the same declaration, resolution, rename, move and graph mechanisms as Markdown references; comments do not supply references.
  - Encoded escapes must not hide a Term token from source-addressed rewriting.
  - Structured section references may select an existing section; they do not perform dagger expansion or execute actions.
.boundary
  - TypeScript sections carry no meaning in _Aterm_. They must remain searchable and render as code, and must never be compiled or scanned for _Reference_.
.remarks
  Chosen: section selectors follow the closing underscore so Term spelling and rename positions remain unchanged. The selector uses an existing Schema key; body is not an alias for all Term Declaration content. Markdown expansion does not rewrite the source Knowledge.
  Column-zero section headers and braces are structural; indented versions are body text.
  Chosen: two leading spaces rather than a tab, and braces rather than indentation, so a
  Term Declaration survives copying into a chat window.
}


policy _Schema_ in language = {
  _Schema_ is the description and ordered section contract of one _Term_Kind_, governing the content sections of every _Term_Declaration_ in it.
.relations
  constrains _Term_Declaration_
  references _Aterm_
  references _Term_Kind_
  references _Relation_
.invariants
  - A _Schema_ must contain a nonempty description and a nonempty ordered section list.
  - Each section must carry a name, exactly one filetype from `md`, `ts`, `yaml` or `json`, and an optional description of what that section is for.
  - Each section name must be unique within its _Schema_.
  - Exactly one _Schema_ governs a _Term_Declaration_, chosen by the _Term_Kind_ on its opener. Two Term Kinds may declare different sections, the same section differently, or the same section identically.
  - A configuration violating any of these does not denote a _Schema_; there is no partially valid one.
.rules
  ### Schema sections
  - Section names must match `[a-z][a-z0-9_]*`.
  - A Term Declaration body must not override a section's filetype.
  - Every _Schema_ must begin with `definition`. The keys `contract` and `remarks` are the public projection: a Schema that declares them keeps their public meaning; a Schema for another _Term_Kind_ may declare its own ordered keys after `definition`.

  ### Named sections
  - The key `relations` must be reserved for structural _Relation_ declarations and must not occur in a Schema content section list.
  - Every Term Kind must permit the same optional .relations section independently of its content Schema.
  - Schema configuration must not designate a content section as a Relation-bearing section.
.boundary
  - Where a _Schema_ comes from, and which Term Declarations it governs, is outside _Aterm_.
.remarks
  Fields belong to a Term Kind, not to a Knowledge. A Term Kind whose Term Declarations have no
  lifecycle has no section for one, and a reader is never handed a box that is always empty.
}


value _Term_ in language = {
  _Term_ is the exact, case-sensitive name identifying one concept in a _Vocabulary_.
.relations
  identifies _Term_Declaration_
  references _Line_Address_
  references _Syntax_
  references _Vocabulary_
.invariants
  - Two Terms are identical only when both their Knowledge IDs and local spellings are identical.
  - A _Term_ must use the name grammar of _Syntax_ and identify exactly one _Term_Declaration_ declaration in its Knowledge.
  - Term identity is the pair (Knowledge ID, local Term name); identical local spellings in different Knowledges identify different Terms.
  - Term Declaration identity is the same (Knowledge ID, local Term name) pair as Term identity; Term Kind is an attribute, not an identity component.
.boundary
  - A _Term_ must not include its section key, a _Line_Address_ or a required-context marker. None of those belong to identity.
.remarks
  A section selector addresses content and a _Line_Address_ describes source lines.
  Neither changes the concept's identity.
}


entity _Term_Declaration_ in language = {
  _Term_Declaration_ is the single complete source declaration describing one _Term_ under one Viewpoint-qualified _Term_Kind_ and its Schema.
.relations
  has_part _Definition_
  declares _Term_Kind_
  belongs to _Group_
  references _Reference_
  references _Syntax_
  references _Term_
  references _Vocabulary_
.invariants
  - _Definition_ must denote the identity section, not the entire Term Declaration or its name.
  - A _Term_Declaration_ is identified by its _Term_. Its Term Kind, Group, content and source location are attributes. Changing Term Kind through a complete Term Declaration edit preserves identity and must validate the entire replacement against the selected Term Kind Schema.
  - A _Term_Declaration_ may declare a Group path without changing its identity.
  - A _Term_Declaration_ must retain its opener, section delimiters and closing delimiter under _Syntax_.
  - A name occurring inside a _Term_Declaration_ must follow _Reference_.
.remarks
  Membership in a _Group_ organizes reading independently of semantic Relations.
  A Term Declaration can move between Groups while its Term and Term Kind stay unchanged.
}


value _Term_Kind_ in language = {
  _Term_Kind_ is a Viewpoint-owned classification for a _Term_, together with the _Schema_ its _Term_Declaration_ follows.
.relations
  carries _Schema_
  classifies _Term_
  constrains _Term_Declaration_
.invariants
  - Every _Term_Kind_ must carry exactly one _Schema_; the label and the sections it permits are one thing, never two.
  - A local Term Kind name must match `[a-z][a-z0-9-]*`; its canonical identity is the supplying Viewpoint name followed by a dot and that local name.
  - Different Viewpoints own distinct Term Kinds even when local names and Schemas are identical.
  - Qualified spelling such as `skill.procedure` must select that exact Term Kind from the Knowledge's declared Viewpoints.
  - A local spelling such as `procedure` must resolve to exactly one declared Term Kind; ambiguity must fail with qualified candidates. Qualifying a Term Kind must not import an undeclared Viewpoint.
.rules
  - The default vocabulary permits `concept`, `procedure` and `undecided`, each following the identity, Contract and Remarks Schema; Term Kind names are editable configuration data.
  - `concept` denotes a thing, data shape, state or rule set; `procedure` denotes an operation with inputs, decisions, effects and outcomes; `undecided` defers classification.
  - Changing the available Term Kinds must change validation without silently remapping existing Term Declarations.
.boundary
  - A Term Kind classifies a Term and names the sections its single Term Declaration may carry. It must not create a separate Term namespace, permit a second Term Declaration for the Term or imply execution behavior.
.remarks
  Chosen: Viewpoint-qualified identity resolves vocabulary collisions without copying Viewpoints or renaming their local Term Kinds.
  A noun such as Validation may name data or an operation. Classify the actual meaning, not its grammatical form.
}


value _Definition_ in language = {
  _Definition_ is the nonempty normative identity statement naming what a concept denotes and what distinguishes it.
.relations
  references _Contract_
  references _Remarks_
.invariants
  - _Definition_ plus _Contract_, including referenced contracts, must remain sufficient without _Remarks_.
.rules
  - The identity must state applicable scope, not merely usefulness or an expansion of the name.
  - Each Term Declaration must cover one independently nameable concept, interface, procedure or rule.
.boundary
  - Detailed characteristics, constraints and procedures are not _Definition_; they belong to _Contract_.
}


value _Contract_ in language = {
  _Contract_ is the normative section specifying the characteristics and obligations of the concept identified by _Definition_.
.relations
  specifies _Definition_
  references _Relation_
  references _Remarks_
.invariants
.rules
  - _Contract_ is optional; absence leaves unspecified details to implementation. An explicitly empty Contract contains no additional rules.
  - _Contract_ must describe only the owning Term: concept invariants or procedure behavior, not another service's obligations by proxy.
  - When needed, it must specify consequential types, field meanings, constraints, ownership, accepted inputs, effects, ordering, transitions and failure behavior.
  - Independently checkable obligations should use separate bullets; ordered procedures should use numbered steps and meaningful headings.
  - Rationale and examples must belong to _Remarks_; essential behavior must not exist only there.
.boundary
  - Applying another named contract reuses its obligations. Merely mentioning a Term, or asserting a _Relation_ to it, implies nothing further.
}


value _Remarks_ in language = {
  _Remarks_ is the optional informative section containing rationale, background, examples and supplementary explanation.
.relations
  references _Contract_
  references _Definition_
.invariants
  - Removing it must leave _Definition_ and _Contract_ sufficient.
.rules
  - It must not introduce, alter or contradict normative obligations.
  - It must not replace an empty _Definition_.
.boundary
  - Whether a read includes _Remarks_ is the reader's choice. The notation only marks the section informative.
}


value _Reference_ in language = {
  _Reference_ is an exact standalone defined-name token in a Markdown section of an _Aterm_ Term Declaration.
.relations
  names _Term_
  references _Aterm_
.invariants
  - An occurrence is fixed by its section, file, physical line and the one-based column at which it begins in that line, together with the name it carries; every occurrence must retain all of them.
  - The column must address the physical line as written, so an occurrence in a one-line Term Declaration and one in an indented body line are addressed the same way.
  - A self-reference must not count as a reference from another Term Declaration and must need no Relation declaration.
  - Every other recognized reference in any section must target a Term declared in that Term Declaration's .relations section; a declaration in another Term Declaration must not satisfy it.
  - Declaration matching must use canonical qualified identity and be independent of textual order.
  - A declaration may stand alone without a body occurrence; references inside opaque code remain outside this validation.
  - Explicit edges may form cycles.
  - A local reference must resolve only within its owning Knowledge; no corpus-wide fallback is allowed.
  - A qualified reference must resolve in the named Knowledge, including an explicit reference to its own Knowledge.
  - Parsed references must retain authored spelling and physical position separately from canonical qualified identity.
.rules
  ### Section selection and expansion
  - A dot followed immediately by an exact Schema section key after the closing underscore selects that section. Parsed references must retain targetSection and contentOffset separately from their Term name, identity and physical position.
  - An adjacent dagger after a section selector requests expansion at that occurrence. Such an occurrence must occupy its own body line; surrounding whitespace is allowed, but inline prose, list markers and inline-code wrappers must fail.
  - Expansion inserts only the selected section's content, recursively at every occurrence; it must not add a Term Declaration or section heading. Nested references resolve within the original owning Knowledge of each section.
  - Section expansion cycles must fail with a diagnostic naming the cycle; ordinary reference and whole-Term required-context cycles remain valid.
  - The existing Markdown code opacity and escaping rules apply to section references. Plus is not a syntax alias for dagger.
  - Relation declarations still target whole Terms; a section selector is not permitted in .relations.

  ### Recognition
  - Inline-code tokens must count; escaped and identifier-embedded tokens must not.
  - Markdown fenced and indented code must be opaque to scanning, and scanning state must reset for each section.

  ### Required marker
  - An immediately adjacent dagger (`†`, U+2020) after an exact name's closing underscore or its section selector must mark that occurrence as required: its target is essential context for understanding or applying the containing Term Declaration.
  - Parsed occurrences must retain the marker, and a grouped explicit edge must keep it if any occurrence in that section is marked.
  - A whitespace-separated dagger must not mark a required reference.
.boundary
  - A reference without a section selector addresses the whole Term and its single complete Term Declaration. A section selector keeps the same Term identity and must resolve to a present section of that Term Declaration; a missing section must fail naming the target. Present empty sections remain valid.
  - The marker must not become part of the name or change the target's identity, Term Kind or Contract.
.remarks
  Use `†` only when omitting the referenced context risks misunderstanding the current
  Contract, not for every related concept.
}


value _Relation_ in language = {
  _Relation_ is a labeled directed assertion connecting its owning _Term_ to a declared target and permitting references to that target in the same Term Declaration.
.relations
  connects _Term_
  references _Reference_
  references _Remarks_
  references _Schema_
  references _Vocabulary_
.invariants
  - The source must be implicit from the containing Term; the target must resolve to a Term in _Vocabulary_, through its single Term Declaration.
  - An occurrence is fixed by source, phrase, target, file and physical line; repeated triples must retain every location.
  - Self-relations and cycles remain structurally permitted; a successful check does not prove the semantic consistency of a built-in assertion. They do not establish incoming use from another Term.
.rules
  - Each nonblank .relations line must use the following grammar without a heading, bullet, source token or trailing punctuation. An optional `†` immediately after the target marks that occurrence as a required _Reference_.
    ```text
    <built-in name or one to five English words> _Target_[†]
    ```
  - General phrase words must contain ASCII letters with optional internal hyphens. Whitespace must normalize to one space without changing phrase spelling or case.
  - Built-in names must match their exact lowercase spelling. Underscores are reserved for registered built-in names; an unknown underscore name must fail with the accepted names. Ordinary phrases remain valid and must not acquire built-in meaning through synonym or case matching.
  - One shared built-in vocabulary must assign each accepted name its type and structural flag. Parsed declarations and structured graph edges must retain these fields without changing authored phrase, direction, required context or occurrence locations.

  | Name | Type | Meaning | Structural |
  | --- | --- | --- | --- |
  | is_a | generalization | Every instance of the Source type is also an instance of the Target type | yes |
  | instance_of | instantiation | The Source is an instance of the Target type | no |
  | has_part | parthood | The Target is a constituent of the Source | yes |
  | has_member | membership | The Target belongs to the Source collection or organizational scope | yes |
  | has_state | state_membership | The Target is a permitted State in the Source's state space | yes |
  | has_property | property | The Source has a property whose value type is the Target; its role is specified in content | no |
  | depends_on | dependency | The Source requires the Target for a purpose stated in content | no |
  | realizes | realization | The Source implements the specification supplied by the Target | no |
  | references | reference | The Source declares a Target for reference without a stronger assertion | no |

  - Any other valid general phrase must have type association and structural false. Built-in classification is independent of Term Kind and Viewpoint.
  - Only lines in the reserved .relations section must declare Relations. Ordinary content sections retain reference scanning but must not infer Relations from sentences, headings or old Relation markers.
  - The .relations section must belong to the Term Declaration rather than any Viewpoint content Schema. Every nonblank line must be a declaration; prose, Markdown lists and code fences must be rejected there.
  - derived_from is a non-structural derivation from an explanation to its authoritative source; it does not expand reading or prescribe execution.
  - A self-derivation or a cycle of derived_from Relations is invalid.
.boundary
  - A Relation asserts only its stated connection. Built-ins add their declared meaning, but never automatic code inheritance, Contract copying, storage, ownership, lifecycle or execution effects.
  - is_a and instance_of must remain distinct. has_part must not imply Composition, and has_member must not imply Parthood or Generalization. Type-level connections must not silently require every instance to participate.
  - has_state declares a permitted State, not the Source's current runtime State. It does not imply Parthood, an initial State, a transition, or that exactly one declared State must be active; those conditions belong in the owning content sections.
  - Role, cardinality, participation, identity and lifecycle conditions remain in the owning content sections. This version must not infer edges, merge Contracts or enforce instance constraints from built-in names.
  - Relation declaration order must not imply execution order. Content headings and lists must not imply additional edges or Relation grouping.
  - Declaration does not load another file, imply transitive imports or make a local name resolve in a different Knowledge.
  - A neutral references phrase must state only the declared connection; it must not invent use, inheritance or execution meaning.
.remarks
  Chosen: every Term Kind shares .relations; body references must declare their dependencies.
  Put the assertion on the active, concretizing side: Schema contains Field,
  rather than Field is contained by Schema. Incoming views still discover it.

  Chosen: built-in names extend ordinary phrases through the same declaration mechanism.
  Chosen: has_state is structural for state-space discovery and filtering; structural classification alone does not derive Graph enclosure.
  Structural classification initially supports discovery; semantic cycle checks, role syntax
  and inferred assertions remain deferred. The original annotated-type draft is not syntax.
  - Chosen: separate Skill entrypoints, body reading and short reanchoring; validate derived guidance through explicit impact review.
}


value _Line_Address_ in language = {
  _Line_Address_ is a Term Declaration-relative address, a Term Kind and exact Term followed by `:start-end`, as in `procedure \_Order_:1-4`, describing inclusive source lines of one _Term_Declaration_.
.relations
  identifies _Term_Declaration_
.invariants
  - Line 1 must be the Term Declaration opener.
.boundary
  - A _Line_Address_ describes lines for inspection. It is not a patch selector, and it confers no ability to change what it addresses.
}


entity _Group_ in language = {
  An organizational collection of Term Declarations within one Knowledge, identified by its Group path while its membership changes.
.relations
  has_member _Term_Declaration_
  references _Vocabulary_
  references _Term_
  references _Relation_
.invariants
  - A _Group_ must be identified by its Knowledge and complete Group path.
  - A _Group_ must have at most one parent, identified by its path without the final segment.
  - A _Group_ must contain the Term Declarations naming its exact path and the Groups extending it by one segment.
  - A _Group_ must preserve Term Declaration and Term identities when membership changes.
  - A _Group_ must not create a namespace or change name resolution in _Vocabulary_.
.states
  - A _Group_ must exist while at least one Term Declaration names its path or a descendant path.
  - A _Group_ must disappear when no Term Declaration requires it or a descendant.
.rules
  - Group paths must use dot-separated lowercase_snake_case segments without empty segments.
  - Each Term Declaration may name one Group path; omission places it directly under Knowledge.
  - A Term has one authored Group path on its single Term Declaration.
  - Group prefixes must be implicit; separate Group declarations are not required.
.boundary
  - Group membership must not imply a _Relation_ between member Terms.
  - A Group instance is not a _Term_ and cannot be addressed by Term reference syntax.
  - Group membership does not determine rendering geometry or expansion state.
.remarks
  Example: test.procedures creates test and its procedures child without declaring either as a Term.
  Example: concept Test can belong to test.concepts while procedure Test belongs to test.procedures.
}


concept _Application_ in operations = {
  _Application_ is the program that gathers one _Vocabulary_ from files and answers questions about it.
.relations
  reads _Aterm_
  references _Vocabulary_
  references _Compare_Corpus_Revisions_
  references _Command_
  references _Concept_Map_
  references _Configuration_
  references _Corpus_
  references _Knowledge_
  references _Edit_Term_Declarations_
  references _External_Files_
  references _Format_Relations_
  references _Home_
  references _Operation_Request_
  references _Rename_Corpus_Identities_
  references _Search_Space_
  references _Search_Corpus_
  references _Query_Service_
  references _Validate_Corpus_
  references _Viewpoint_
.contract
  - A Term Declaration's Term Kind must come from a _Viewpoint_ its owning _Knowledge_ declares; nothing else supplies one.
  - Concept discovery must follow _Concept_Map_.
  - Inspection must use _Query_Service_, _Validate_Corpus_ and _Compare_Corpus_Revisions_; authoring must use _Edit_Term_Declarations_, _Rename_Corpus_Identities_ and _Format_Relations_.
  - Knowledge selection must follow _Home_, _Configuration_ and _Corpus_; explicitly requested external text selection must follow _External_Files_. No undeclared source may be discovered.
  - The command line must follow _Command_, and every request it makes must follow _Operation_Request_.
  - _Application_ must add no meaning to _Aterm_; a Term Declaration means the same read by anything else.
  - Every refusal must be one failure that names the rejected input and the accepted form, in the operator's own terms rather than an internal path; no refusal may be reported as a partial success.
  - Every text comparison used to order results, scans or authored Relation lines must use the same code-unit comparison. One corpus must order the same way on every machine, so no ordering may depend on the runtime's locale, and no two operations may order the same names differently.
.remarks
  The notation is specified separately. This Term and everything below it exist only
  because something has to find the files, hold an index and answer a question.

  Chosen: code-unit comparison for every presented ordering. The initial correction aligned
  Concept Map ordering with term list; formatting later adopted the same comparison.
  Naming migration preserves this comparison rule; the relative order of renamed Terms
  follows their new canonical names.
}


concept _Home_ in operations = {
  _Home_ is the `.aterm` directory holding _Configuration_, and _Workspace_ is its parent.
.relations
  has_member _Configuration_
  references _Edit_Term_Declarations_
  references _Workspace_
.contract
  ### Selection
  - _Home_ must be selected by the first of: an explicit `--home` path, the `ATERM_HOME` variable, the nearest `.aterm` directory from the working directory upward, then `~/.aterm`.
  - An explicitly selected path that is not a directory must fail; an empty `ATERM_HOME` must fail. No candidate may be skipped silently.
  - Selection with no candidate must fail naming the working directory, the user candidate and `aterm init`.
  - Selection must read the filesystem only; it must never create or modify a directory.
  - _Home_ must hold its configuration as `aterm.yaml`; no other filename may be read.
  - _Home_ must hold the exclusive write lock of _Edit_Term_Declarations_.
.remarks
  `*_HOME` names the tool's own directory in common use, as with `CARGO_HOME`; the
  workspace is derived from it rather than configured separately.
}


concept _Workspace_ in operations = {
  _Workspace_ is the parent directory of _Home_ and the base every relative source path and reported source file path resolves against.
.relations
  has_member _Home_
  references _Compare_Corpus_Revisions_
.contract
  - _Workspace_ must be derived from _Home_; it must never be configured or supplied separately.
  - Every source file path reported to an operator must be relative to _Workspace_ and use forward slashes.
  - _Workspace_ must be the working directory of the Git comparison in _Compare_Corpus_Revisions_.
  - Every Aterm authoring operation must reserve paths containing a .git segment, compared case-insensitively, including a .git directory pointer file. Creation, replacement and deletion must fail before saving; dry-run must report the same refusal. Raw reads of existing regular text metadata remain allowed.
.remarks
  Selecting `~/.aterm` makes the user home directory the workspace, so its sources are
  normally written as absolute paths.
}


concept _Configuration_ in operations = {
  _Configuration_ is the `aterm.yaml` declaration of the source directories and the bound _Viewpoint_ set of one _Home_.
.relations
  binds _Viewpoint_
  references _External_Files_
  references _Home_
  references _Skill_
  references _Workspace_
.contract
  ### Sources
  - Optional top-level useDefaultKnowledge must be boolean and default to true. It includes the shipped aterm, vending_machine and aterm_skills Knowledges in the ordinary corpus without adding package paths to sources. false removes only implicit inclusion; Skill guidance remains available independently.
  - Init must write useDefaultKnowledge: true and explain that false does not disable guidance. The retired defaultTerms key must fail with a migration message naming useDefaultKnowledge; non-boolean values must fail.
  - Optional allowDefaultWrites must be boolean and default to false. Packaged Knowledge and Viewpoint files must be read-only unless it is true, including explicit source paths, explicit Viewpoint bindings and raw file writes or deletion. This permission does not lift Workspace, source, version or validation guards. Init must write false.
  - Optional top-level skills accepts sync and uninstall only. Supply both nonempty Bash command strings or neither. Unknown keys, the removed source selector, unmatched pairs and blank commands fail. Commands run only during explicit local Skill lifecycle actions, with the environment specified by _Skill_.
  - Optional `server` must require `port`, an integer from 1 through 65535, and accept `host`, `debounceMs`, `watchExternal`, `publicOrigin` and `controlToken` only. Omitting server must keep filesystem-only operation; supplying server without port or with invalid values must fail. Server startup requires server.port or an explicit --port for recovery.
  - `server.host` must be localhost or an IPv4 or IPv6 address without brackets, port or zone identifier, default 127.0.0.1. Init must write that default explicitly. 0.0.0.0 selects all IPv4 interfaces; :: selects the unspecified IPv6 address, with IPv4 acceptance determined by the platform. A concrete address selects that local interface. Host and port changes require restart.
  - Optional server.controlToken must be a string of at least thirty-two characters and authorize external UI controller requests. It must never be returned in health, Session or command results. Omitting it must restrict UI control to local CLI requests; ordinary browser reading and registration follow the configured listen address. Token changes require restart.
  - `server.debounceMs` must be an integer from 0 through 60000, default 200. `server.watchExternal` must be boolean, default true. watchExternal controls background observation only and must not disable external search.
  - Optional top-level `externalSources` must map source names to file globs, independently of server configuration, following _External_Files_. Search and grep must use it even when server is omitted. Legacy server.externalDirectories and server.externalExtensions must be rejected as unknown configuration keys.
  - Term uniqueness must not be configurable. The retired `allowMultipleEntriesPerTerm` key must fail as an unknown key regardless of its value; remove it from existing configuration.
  - _Configuration_ must declare a nonempty `sources` list; a relative entry must resolve against _Workspace_ and an absolute entry must be used as given.
  - Repeated sources must collapse to one; a missing or unreadable source must be a warning, not a failure.

  ### Viewpoint bindings and validation
  - Optional useViewpoints must select built-in names from specification, generic, domain and skill only. Omission enables all four in that order; an empty list enables none. Repeated names collapse to one and unknown names fail.
  - Built-in bindings must read package files directly without copying them into Home. An optional viewpoints mapping adds custom documents and overrides a selected built-in binding of the same name. A project Knowledge may declare only effective bound names; internally loaded guidance does not add Home bindings.
  - A viewpoint path beginning with `~` must resolve against the user's home directory, and a relative path against _Home_; every other path must be used as given.
  - An unknown key and an invalid Schema must each fail naming the configuration file; a missing `aterm.yaml` must fail naming its path and `aterm init`.
  - _Configuration_ must not classify Knowledges. Each Knowledge declares its own Viewpoints.
  - Optional server.publicOrigin must be an HTTP(S) origin without path, credentials or query. It permits that explicit proxy Host/Origin; arbitrary forwarded headers do not grant access. Changes require restart.
.remarks
  ```yaml
  sources:
    - docs
  useDefaultKnowledge: true
  useViewpoints: [specification, generic]
  allowDefaultWrites: false
  viewpoints:
    uml: ~/aterm-lib/aterm-viewpoint-uml.md
  ```
  Filenames carry no semantic identity; each Knowledge declares its ID and Viewpoints.
}


concept _Viewpoint_ in language = {
  _Viewpoint_ is one reusable vocabulary for describing a subject: the Term Kinds it offers, the sections each of them expects, and the guidance for writing in it.
.relations
  references _Query_Service_
  has_member _Term_Kind_
  references _Configuration_
.contract
  - CLI viewpoint list must list bindings without selecting a default name. CLI viewpoint view must require one or more exact names or whole-name globs; omitting the operation must only print group help.

  - A _Viewpoint_ must declare each Term Kind with a name, a description of what that Term Kind is for, its ordered content sections. Every Term Kind shares the reserved .relations section independently of those sections.
  - A Term Kind description must be machine-readable, because it is what steers whoever writes in the _Viewpoint_.
  - A Term Kind naming one section twice, or whose sections do not begin with `definition`, must fail the _Viewpoint_ naming the Term Kind.
  - A _Viewpoint_ must not name a Knowledge or a source; it describes a vocabulary, never which files use it.
  - `viewpoint list` must show one table row per bound Viewpoint: name and description, without nested Term Kind rows or a reading hint. JSON/YAML must retain Term Kind metadata.
  - `viewpoint view` text and Markdown must output each selected original Markdown file, including frontmatter, comments, whitespace and line endings, concatenated without wrappers in binding order. An empty selection must report no Viewpoints. JSON/YAML must retain full source and parsed metadata; lists need not carry full source.
  - Source must come from the loaded configuration, including the published server snapshot. Presentation must not reread files.
  - `viewpoint view <pattern...>` must select bound Viewpoints by exact names or whole-name globs under the _Query_Service_ case policy, reject unknown exact names naming what the _Configuration_ binds, and require at least one selector. An unmatched glob must select nothing; overlapping selections must not duplicate a Viewpoint. The CLI positional selectors must populate the `viewpoints` query field, the same field used by filters. The CLI must not require or accept a guidance flag; original source already contains the explanatory body. The programmatic guidance field remains available for structured body reads.

  ### Generic vocabulary
  - The shipped generic Viewpoint must declare exactly one Term Kind, term, with Markdown sections definition, classification and description in that order. Definition must be the default body section; classification and description may be omitted.
  - Generic classification must be free-form authored text with no fixed vocabulary or single-value limit. Labels such as concept, procedure and policy must not select a Schema, imply inheritance, declare another Term Declaration or act as a Term Kind filter. Description must accept unrestricted explanatory content rather than a prescribed topic outline.
  - Generic must use the same .relations declarations and non-self reference validation as every other Viewpoint.

  ### Skill vocabulary
  - The shipped skill Viewpoint must offer Term Kinds skill, explanation, principle, rule, constraint, procedure and resource. A Skill must support reusable knowledge and guidance without requiring an execution sequence. Supporting content must retain meaning independently of a Skill that uses it.
  - Step must be a meaningful instruction unit's role within a Procedure, not a separate Term Kind. A Procedure serving as a Step must retain its identity; an inline action need not become a Term.
  - Supporting Term Kinds use definition as the default body and offer applicable explanatory sections without requiring invented content. Skill uses definition, description, body and optional reminder.
  - Skill offers description for activation and Markdown body/reminder for guidance. Procedure retains inputs, procedure and outputs; resource retains location and usage.
  - Guidance must distinguish a Principle's judgment criterion, a Rule's behavioral direction, a Constraint's condition, an Explanation's content, a Procedure's method and a Resource's supporting material. Different roles must not by themselves require different Term identities.
  - Guidance must keep shared instructions on the owning Term, caller-specific bindings on the caller, and control flow in the caller's procedure. Required-context order must not imply execution order. Applicable caller and callee obligations must remain in force; reference order must not invent precedence.
  - Examples must use short contextual GOOD/BAD comparisons. Validation must identify evidence for the authored claim; a completed review must not imply the reviewed result is valid. Skill Evaluation must remain distinct from per-application validation.

  ### Questions
  - A section of a Term Kind may ask questions, `question` on the section, one per line; the text up to the first `?` is the question and the rest is its frame, whose blank is the answer. None or one per section is the usual number.
  - The answer to a section's question is written into that section; connections found by any question must be declared in .relations on their active side, while descriptive answers and obligations remain in the section that asked the question.
  - A section may carry one `example`: an answer from the Viewpoint's worked example and how its shape is read.
  - A Term Kind is asked its sections' questions in section order.

  ### Source file
  - A _Viewpoint_ must be one Markdown document whose frontmatter carries the machine-readable declaration and whose body carries the explanation and the instructions for writing in it.
  - Common Skills must defer to the selected Viewpoint Markdown for vocabulary-specific authoring guidance. Where it conflicts with general Skill guidance, the Viewpoint takes precedence within its vocabulary; user instructions, Knowledge Scope and required structural validation and repository review remain in force.
  - Viewpoint names must match `[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9-]*` case-sensitively. Term Kind names in Viewpoint frontmatter remain local; qualification belongs to a Term Declaration or selector.
  - Its frontmatter name must equal the name the _Configuration_ binds it to; a mismatch must fail naming both.
  - A _Viewpoint_ that does not parse must fail the _Configuration_ that names it, and must never leave a Knowledge silently reading with a partial vocabulary.
.remarks
  Chosen: compose Viewpoints by qualified Term Kind identity; equal local names do not merge or impose precedence. The consolidated Knowledge uses the original domain, skill and specification Viewpoints.
  Verification covers identical local names with equal and different Schemas, explicit qualification, ambiguous short names and undeclared owners.
  One _Viewpoint_ may serve many Knowledges, and one Knowledge may declare several. The
  package is what makes a vocabulary portable between projects; without the body the
  Term Kinds are names whose meaning lives only in whoever last wrote a prompt.
  Declaring sections per Term Kind is what keeps a section from being a box that one Term Kind
  always fills and another never does.

  Chosen: every Viewpoint selection uses the same exact-name and whole-name glob
  mechanism, whether selecting vocabulary, filtering Term Declarations.
  Unknown exact names remain errors; only unmatched globs produce an empty selection.
  Selection is validated against configuration, including bound Viewpoints with no Term Declarations.
  Chosen: role-specific Skill Term Kinds apply the generic Skill model without making every
  Skill a workflow. Step is a role, not a second declaration for a reused Procedure.
  Verification reads the worked example without a procedure and installs its Skill
  document while leaving supporting Term Kinds in the corpus.
  Chosen: resource command migration preserves existing query semantics and adds Knowledge and Skill discovery. CLI examples, generated Skill documents, local/HTTP reads and refusal behavior are verified together.
}


concept _Skill_ in operations = {
  Reusable knowledge or task guidance represented by a Skill Term Declaration, read through its entrypoint and body and optionally installed as SKILL.md.
.relations
  references _Configuration_
  references _Knowledge_
  references _Skill_Reading_
  uses _Guidance_Catalog_
.contract
  - Discover every skill Term Declaration in the combined package and Home catalog, whether its Term Kind is written short or qualified. There is one Skill system and no registration protocol, catalog-source switch or Workflow command family.
  - Reading follows _Skill_Reading_; installation and canonical MCP tools share the same generated TOC and activation metadata.
  - Skill content teaches reusable knowledge or application guidance through short explanations and adjacent examples. Rewritten guidance declares derived_from its authoritative sources.
  - Install IDs are the complete Knowledge ID and local Term name joined with a hyphen, with underscores replaced by hyphens and letters lowercased. IDs must satisfy the installation alphabet and 64-character limit and be unique; descriptions must be nonempty and at most 1024 characters. Refuse invalid or colliding metadata before changing managed files.
  - Installed SKILL.md adds name and description frontmatter to the exact skill toc Markdown. It must not embed the body or Reminder. Read actual guidance using qualified Term selectors with skill view.
  - Validate the combined corpus, Skill declarations, prerequisites and install metadata before managed writes or Agent sync. Reading-size targets are advisory and never truncate output.
  - install, update and uninstall are distinct local operations managing only the selected Home's skills directory; they never dispatch through HTTP or execute Skill guidance.
  - Install requires no existing manifest; otherwise it directs the caller to update. Update and uninstall require a valid manifest bound to the selected Home.
  - Update migrates previous managed catalogs through the same manifest, ownership guards, stale-name removal, synchronization and recovery mechanism.
  - A managed file path must match its Skill name; installation must refuse unmanaged or modified files rather than overwrite them.
  - Preview all ownership conflicts before saving any managed file. Preserve guarded paths and concurrency checks during writes.
  - Update synchronizes current names and content and removes stale managed names. Uninstall removes only manifest-owned files and uses recorded uninstall commands.
  - Configured sync receives the selected Home, workspace, Skill directory, operation, current names and removed names. Removed names stay tracked until synchronization succeeds.
  - Maintain the write-ahead manifest across partial writes or failed sync, report saved paths/pending work, and support a later update or uninstall without claiming incomplete effects succeeded.
  - No configured external synchronization means only local managed-file state is claimed.
  - Skill update is required to refresh installed content after corpus changes; source reads do not themselves synchronize Agent files.
.remarks
  Chosen: promote prerequisite-based Skills as the sole system. Remove legacy Workflow commands, registration YAML and Markdown method/Guide composition; preserve useful source principles and procedures as plain Knowledge.
  Verification covers ordered prerequisites, distinct TOC/body/reminder readings, hidden packaged guidance, CLI/HTTP/MCP parity and managed installation migration.
}


concept _Knowledge_ in language = {
  _Knowledge_ is our current model of a subject: how its concepts are defined, used and related. Its Term Declarations (_Term_Declaration_) and their Relations (_Relation_) are stored together in one `.trm` file with a stable Knowledge ID.
.relations
  declares _Viewpoint_
  has_part _Term_Declaration_
  references _Relation_
  references _Schema_
  references _Configuration_
  references _Workspace_
.contract
  ### Viewpoint selection
  - A _Knowledge_ must declare, after @knowledge and any `//` comment lines, one line `@viewpoints <name> [<name>…]` naming Viewpoints the _Configuration_ binds, in the order they compose.
  - A Knowledge without that line or naming an unbound Viewpoint must produce an error diagnostic and must not be parsed; its Term Declarations have no vocabulary.
  - A second `@viewpoints` line, or one after the first Term Declaration, must be a diagnostic of that Knowledge.
  - The Term Kinds a Knowledge's Term Declarations may open with must be the union of its declared Viewpoints' Term Kinds, in the order declared, each keeping the _Schema_ its own _Viewpoint_ gave it; local names shared by different Viewpoints must coexist as distinct qualified Term Kinds. Repeating the same Viewpoint must not create a second candidate.
  - Reported Viewpoint of a Term Declaration must be the resolved owner of its Term Kind; an explicit qualifier must select a declared owner, while a short name must have one possible owner. The filename must not select a Viewpoint.

  ### Description and Scope
  - Description is reader-facing discovery text: what this Knowledge contains and why a reader would open it. It must not substitute for authoring coverage obligations.
  - A Knowledge may declare one @description block and one @scope block after @viewpoints and before its first Term Declaration, in either order. Both use the same nonempty multiline block syntax; either may be omitted, and neither may be inferred from the other.
  - Description must remain distinct from a Term Declaration definition or Skill .description. Description belongs to the Knowledge, including when it has no Term Declarations.
  - A Knowledge may declare one `@scope {` block after `@viewpoints` and before its first Term Declaration. Its nonempty Markdown body must use two leading spaces and end at a column-zero `}`; inline, duplicate, misplaced and unclosed blocks must fail validation.
  - Scope is writer-facing authoring guidance: what must be covered in this Knowledge, what belongs elsewhere and what freedom remains. Authors must read it before creating or changing Term Declarations; Description is not a substitute. Absence of scope must remain distinguishable from empty scope and must not claim completeness.
  - Reads must retain scope content and its physical bounds as Knowledge context on every returned Term Declaration, including required-context expansion from other Knowledges. Scope must not change Term Declaration source bounds or become a Term Declaration or Relation.
  - An Agent implementing behavior covered by scope must treat a missing obligation as a specification gap: add supported requirements to the owning Term Declarations, record newly chosen behavior as `Chosen:` in remarks, and resolve the gap before completing implementation. It must not silently widen scope or turn incidental implementation behavior into a requirement.
  - Structural validation must check scope syntax without claiming that Term Declarations satisfy its semantic coverage.

  ### Identity and source file
  - A _Knowledge_ must retain its source file's path relative to _Workspace_, absolute path, owning source, text and a content version.
  - Its content version must be a hash of its bytes, and an authoring write must refuse when the stored version no longer matches the file.
  - A file that is not valid UTF-8 must produce an error diagnostic and must not be parsed.
  - Knowledge records the currently adopted definitions, uses and relationships within its scope. History records Memory; it does not define current Knowledge.
  - Every discovered Knowledge must declare one @knowledge ID before @viewpoints; missing, duplicate, malformed or misplaced declarations are errors.
  - Knowledge IDs must be unique across the full discovered corpus, including empty Knowledges. One Knowledge is stored in one .trm file; it cannot span several files.
  - Knowledge identity is independent of file path; moving or renaming a file must preserve Term identities. Configured sources discover Knowledges without @import.
  - Reader labels, authoring guidance and diagnostics must name the modeled subject Knowledge. When only its physical storage matters, name the .trm file.
.remarks
  Chosen: Knowledge is the subject formerly named Document; a .trm file is its storage, not a second modeled subject. Existing identity, header, Scope and authoring rules are preserved.
  Knowledge may change as our understanding or its surrounding conditions change. Its meaning comes from the Term Declarations and their connections together.
  The filename is free; `SPEC-todo.trm` and `todo.trm` are read alike. One Knowledge may
  declare `@viewpoints specification ui` and hold the specification and the screen of one
  feature together.
  Chosen: Knowledge is the domain name for one current subject model; it is used as a countable noun within Aterm. It is not a separate storage container.
}


concept _Corpus_ in operations = {
  _Corpus_ is the collection of admitted _Knowledge_ instances from the configured and enabled default sources of one _Configuration_.
.relations
  derives from _Configuration_
  has_member _Knowledge_
.contract
  ### Discovery
  - _Corpus_ must read every .trm file under each configured source, recursively. Unless useDefaultKnowledge is false, it must also include the package's complete shipped Knowledges in Term, Knowledge, search, Graph, checking and reference resolution, consistently across CLI, Web UI and MCP.
  - Implicit package Knowledges must be parsed and validated with their own Viewpoint bindings and the shared one-declaration-per-Term invariant; Home vocabulary selection must not replace package bindings. Query filters and search section selection must recognize the vocabularies of admitted Knowledges as well as configured Home bindings.
  - A physical source path must appear once. Explicitly configured source content and candidate overlays take precedence over implicit package inclusion; a candidate deletion must not reappear from the package scan. Different files claiming one Knowledge ID must fail through the shared index, never shadow one another.
  - Packaged Knowledge writes require allowDefaultWrites under _Configuration_, including when its path is explicitly configured as a source. Authoring must refuse a protected change before saving any file. term format with no selector must select writable Knowledges while validating references against the full corpus.
  - useDefaultKnowledge: false excludes implicit package Knowledges from ordinary corpus operations. Explicit sources remain active, and project references to excluded Knowledge become unresolved; Skill guidance retain their independent complete catalog.
  - Scanning must skip `.git`, `node_modules` and names beginning with `.aterm`, and must not follow symbolic links; a skipped link must be a warning.
  - Knowledges must be ordered by workspace-relative path and diagnostics by absolute path, so repeated scans of unchanged sources produce identical output.
  - A Knowledge is admitted when its source file's `@viewpoints` line resolved and it parsed; only admitted Knowledges enter the index.

  ### Shared index
  - All admitted Knowledges must share one symbol table; a name filter must not hide corpus errors.
  - Terms of every Viewpoint must share that symbol table; _Corpus_ must not introduce Viewpoint-qualified names or per-Viewpoint lookup precedence.
.remarks
  Terminology migration: scan failures use trm.encoding, trm.viewpoints, trm.viewpoint and trm.termKinds diagnostic codes for physical .trm files. Validation and admission rules are unchanged.
  A directory outside the configured sources is not active syntax merely because it
  contains an old specification. File selection and named-reference lookup are separate operations.
}


concept _Term_Selector_ in operations = {
  _Term_Selector_ is a Term pattern in a query or authoring request: one exact _Term_ or one whole-name glob.
.relations
  references _Query_Service_
  selects _Term_
  references _Aterm_
  references _Line_Address_
  references _Edit_Term_Declarations_
.contract
  - Name selectors must be exact names or whole-name globs, combined as a deduplicated union. Read matching follows _Query_Service_; authoring selectors remain case-sensitive.
  - Unmatched globs must return no matches; unknown exact names must fail.
  - An exact selector must not include a section key, a _Line_Address_ or a required-context marker; `Term.key` is an _Edit_Term_Declarations_ selector only.
  - Exact unqualified names must use the common Knowledge resolution: one distinct Term succeeds, several return candidates even when one spelling matches exactly under the default case-insensitive policy. Use --case-sensitive to distinguish case-only identities. --knowledge restricts targets before filters; qualified names explicitly select their Knowledge.
.remarks
  `_Aterm_*` selects every Term whose name starts that way; an exact selector such as
  _Term_ must exist. Quote a glob on the command line so the shell does not expand it.
}


concept _Term_Selection_By_File_ in operations = {
  _Term_Selection_By_File_ is the selection of every _Term_ declared in admitted _Corpus_ Knowledges chosen by workspace-relative source file path.
.relations
  reads _Corpus_
  selects _Term_
  references _Term_Selector_
.contract
  - Selectors must be workspace-relative source file paths, such as `docs/SPEC-core.trm`, or quoted globs following the _Term_Selector_ glob rules.
  - An unknown exact path must fail and an unmatched glob must select nothing.
  - Every Term declared in a matched source file must be selected, independently of its edges, without reading Contracts.
}


concept _Remarks_Inclusion_Option_ in operations = {
  _Remarks_Inclusion_Option_ is the query option deciding whether _Remarks_ text and its reference edges take part in a read.
.relations
  scopes _Remarks_
  references _Contract_
  references _Definition_
  references _Relation_
.contract
  - Queries must include _Remarks_ by default alongside _Definition_ and _Contract_.
  - `--no-remark` must exclude _Remarks_ text and reference edges from results and traversal, not from validation.
  - Default graph queries must include the reference edges of every Markdown section and every _Relation_ edge.
  - The sole Remarks selector must be `--no-remark`.
}


concept _Diagnostic_ in operations = {
  _Diagnostic_ is one message attached to a physical source location: an error that invalidates the corpus or a warning that does not.
.contract
  - A _Diagnostic_ must retain file, one-based line and message.
  - Warnings must remain separate from errors, preserve exit status 0 and permit ordinary queries.
  - Every scan message must be one _Diagnostic_ in one list; no second per-file list may repeat it.
}


concept _Corpus_Index_ in operations = {
  _Corpus_Index_ is the Term Declarations, _Reference_ occurrences, _Relation_ edges and _Diagnostic_ values built from one _Corpus_.
.relations
  derives from _Corpus_
  carries _Diagnostic_
  references _Reference_
  references _Relation_
.contract
  - A _Corpus_Index_ must retain every occurrence location of a reference or Relation; repeated triples retain every location.
  - The index must reject every repeated Term within its Knowledge, regardless of Term Kind, Viewpoint or Group. Diagnostics must name the qualified Term and both Term Kinds and source locations; no duplicate Term Declaration may be chosen or merged.
  - Duplicate (Knowledge ID, local Term name) diagnostics must explicitly demand resolution, name the Term Kind and identify both Term Declaration locations; queries must not silently choose a Term Declaration.
  - Index keys must be canonical qualified Term identities. Each valid key has exactly one Term Declaration; equal local names in different Knowledges are independent.
  - Term Declaration uniqueness must use Term identity alone. Qualified Term Kind identity selects a Schema and never permits a repeated Term declaration.
}


concept _Search_Space_ in operations = {
  _Search_Space_ is the query-local set of eligible _Term_ nodes and connections selected from a _Corpus_Index_ by a Viewpoint filter and a phrase filter.
.relations
  references _Query_Service_
  derives from _Corpus_Index_
  selects _Term_
  references _Concept_Map_
  references _Corpus_
.contract
  - _Search_Space_ must validate the whole _Corpus_ before projection; it must neither hide corpus errors nor create unresolved-reference errors by excluding a valid Term.
  - Viewpoint inclusions and exclusions must resolve against configured Home bindings and admitted Knowledges' vocabularies, including Home bindings with no Term Declarations. Unknown exact names must fail and unmatched globs must select nothing, as in `viewpoint view`.
  - `--viewpoint` and `--exclude-viewpoint` must select Term Declaration Viewpoints; `--relation` and `--exclude-relation` must select authored edge phrases. Each accepts one or more patterns.
  - Filters must match exact names or whole-string globs under the _Query_Service_ case policy; an absent or empty inclusion list must include all, and exclusion must win over inclusion.
  - A Viewpoint filter must select each Term Declaration by the Viewpoint supplying its Term Kind. A Term is eligible when its single Term Declaration is eligible. An edge must retain only occurrences owned by eligible Term Declarations and require both endpoint Terms to be eligible; traversal must not pass through excluded Terms.
  - A phrase filter must match the phrase as authored; no synonym or reverse wording is inferred.
  - The exclusion and phrase filters must apply to `term list`, `corpus search`, `graph overview`, `graph connect`, `graph relations` and `graph path`; every other operation must reject a supplied nonempty filter.
  - The Viewpoint inclusion selection means one thing everywhere: restrict the answer to the named Viewpoints. It applies only to operations accepting the Viewpoint selection field; Skill reading uses its own declared vocabulary and rejects this filter.
  - Directly selected exact Terms outside the space must be rejected, including path endpoints; globs and file selections must silently intersect the eligible Terms.
  - Term Declaration eligibility must filter occurrence locations before edge grouping. Phrase filtering must apply after the explicit-Relation precedence of _Concept_Map_; excluding an explicit phrase must not resurrect its replaced generic reference.
}


concept _Concept_Map_ in operations = {
  _Concept_Map_ is a compact discovery view of Term identities and labeled connections, used to locate relevant Contracts before reading their detail.
.relations
  projects _Relation_
  projects _Reference_
  summarizes _Definition_
  references _Find_Occurrences_
  references _Rename_Corpus_Identities_
  references _Validate_Corpus_
.contract
  ### Connections
  - _Concept_Map_ must retain edge origin (`relation`, `reference`), phrase, direction and locations; equal body-reference triples must combine section occurrences without duplicate displayed edges.
  - Structured Relations and raw reference edges must expose type and structural from the shared built-in vocabulary. Raw References have type reference and structural false. The existing --relation phrase filters must match built-in names exactly or by glob without aliases; text must retain canonical authored names.
  - _Concept_Map_ must default to both origins; `--edges <origins>` on `graph overview`, `graph connect` and `graph relations` must accept a comma-separated nonempty selection of `relation` and `reference`, and reject other values.
  - _Concept_Map_ must order declared Relations before body references, then by source, phrase and target.

  ### Term summaries
  - _Concept_Map_ must represent each Term once with an `termDeclarations` collection containing its one eligible Term Kind, Viewpoint, Definition and location summary. It must omit Contracts, Remarks and required-context expansion. Text must distinguish the Term Kind of each summary.
  - Text must identify selected Terms, their Viewpoints and Definitions; external and intermediate Terms must be identifiable as edge endpoints without expanding their Contracts.
  - Text must show physical locations only with `--with-fileline`; structured results must retain locations and intermediate path Term identities.

  ### Explicit Relation precedence
  - _Concept_Map_ must omit the generic `references` edge for an ordered source-target pair when at least one _Relation_ with that source and target exists; every explicit phrase must remain visible.
  - That omission must apply before origin filtering, so a `reference`-only projection shows exactly the ordered pairs still lacking a _Relation_.
  - A reverse-direction _Relation_ must not be treated as a substitute; the generic edge for the opposite ordered pair must remain.
  - The omission must apply to _Concept_Map_ only; reference occurrences, required context, _Find_Occurrences_, _Rename_Corpus_Identities_, _Validate_Corpus_ and detailed view edges must remain complete.
}


concept _Query_Service_ in operations = {
  _Query_Service_ is the read-only query boundary over a _Corpus_Index_ for a selected _Corpus_.
.relations
  has_member _Jq_Query_
  has_member _GraphQL_Query_
  has_member _Discover_Terms_
  has_member _List_Term_Declarations_
  has_member _Search_Corpus_
  has_member _Show_Term_Declarations_
  has_member _Read_With_Context_
  has_member _Find_Occurrences_
  has_member _Find_Paths_
  has_member _Show_Graph_Overview_
  has_member _Find_Connecting_Paths_
  has_member _List_Connections_
  queries _Corpus_Index_
  references _Schema_
  references _Corpus_
  references _Diagnostic_
  references _Search_Space_
  references _Validate_Corpus_
.contract
  - Corpus JSON projection and program evaluation follow _Jq_Query_; native jq matching is not reinterpreted by the Aterm case setting.
  ### Read matching
  - Read selectors and literal text must match case-insensitively by default. CLI --case-sensitive and query caseSensitive: true must request exact case; omission or false selects the default.
  - This policy applies to Term, Knowledge, Viewpoint, Skill selection, section-key selection, Relation phrase/type filters and filters over admitted source file paths. Lists without a matching input retain their ordinary content and ordering.
  - Matching must be locale-independent and retain canonical identity, authored text and original token spelling in results. Case-insensitive lookup must not merge distinct Terms or change reference resolution.
  - Authored names, corpus references and guarded authoring remain case-sensitive. Configured source globs, raw filesystem paths and Git revisions keep their existing semantics; the query option must not redefine them.
  - GraphQL matching follows _GraphQL_Query_; lexical and semantic discovery distinctions follow _Discover_Terms_.

  ### Read projection
  - Field selection and nested traversal must follow _GraphQL_Query_, using the same corpus validation and canonical identity resolution as other reads.
  - Ranked discovery results follow _Discover_Terms_; canonical Term Declaration ordering for ordinary reads must not replace retrieval rank.
  - Show and view results must retain selected source Term Declarations and carry sectionContext separately: the Term Declaration resources needed by recursive section expansion, with authored content and reference metadata. Renderers must not perform extra corpus reads to expand sections. The context may contain a selected Term Declaration and does not add it again to the selected list.
  ### Boundary
  - _Query_Service_ must supply the same validation and domain behavior to every adapter.
  - Every accepted corpus selector and filter must follow _Search_Space_ semantics; the request declaration owns which fields each operation accepts.
  - Invalid corpora must fail ordinary queries rather than return partial success, according to _Validate_Corpus_.
  - Finite results must support text, Markdown, JSON and YAML.
  - Every query must run _Validate_Corpus_ first; `corpus check` must return its _Diagnostic_ values, where any other operation must fail on them.

  ### Results
  - JSON and YAML must use termDeclarations for Term Declaration collections, termDeclaration for an owned single declaration, termDeclarationCount for counts, and termKind or termKinds for Term Kind metadata. Compound field names must use the same subjects. CLI, HTTP, MCP and browser consumers must share these spellings; former Entry and Kind field spellings are not aliases.
  - Every result must name the operation that produced it, so that a consumer and the presentation read it from the result rather than from the invocation.
  - Term selection must return each eligible Term's single Term Declaration. Whole-Term required context must expand the complete Term Declaration of each required Term once. Semantic query graph nodes and paths must remain Term-based.
  - Structured Term Declaration results and ConceptMap Term Declaration summaries must retain any authored Group path in the optional group field. Group metadata must not add Relations or alter Term selection, ordering or identity.
  - Results must carry the Viewpoint of each Term Declaration, including search matches and required-context expansion.
  - Text and Markdown `term list` must show Term Kind and Viewpoint. Default text `corpus search` must show Term Kind and Viewpoint; Markdown search and Term Declaration readings omit Viewpoint annotations. Copyable Term Declaration openers must retain Term Kind and any authored in Group path.
  - Structured Term Declaration arrays and flat text lists must order Term Declarations by ascending canonical qualified Term ID, independently of selector order and of Knowledge order; only the required context of _Read_With_Context_ may follow outside that order. Hierarchical text must apply that order among sibling Term Declarations; its container order belongs to the operation contract.
  - Text `term view` and `term show` must identify each Term Declaration's Viewpoint but omit Knowledge scope by default. Plain-text `term view --detail` must include scope when declared; JSON/YAML must retain its content and physical bounds. Context annotations must remain separate from Term Declaration source and preserve copyability and source-line numbering; `--section` must continue to return only section content.
  - A Knowledge that could not be read must appear as a diagnostic, never as unclassified Term Declarations.
  - Every keyed section must be exposed in _Schema_ order, additionally projecting `definition`, `contract` and `remarks` when declared.
  - Text must expose paths when they identify source content or a managed cache: external search matches, discovery evidence, grep occurrences, detailed view source bounds, unified-diff paths, Index cache locations and the File headers of `term list --tree`. Other result paths must be hidden unless `--with-fileline` is supplied.
  - Every returned Term Declaration must retain local name, Knowledge ID and canonical qualified id. Index, graph, edge, path and external reference identities must use canonical IDs.
  - Candidate results must be distinguishable from completed query results in text and structured output. Text reads must identify each Term Declaration Knowledge while preserving local declaration spelling.
  - Term Declaration results must include knowledges, a Knowledge catalog of declared IDs, source file paths and optional Description content with source bounds independent of selected Term Declarations, including Knowledges with no Term Declarations. Invalid source files without a parsed Knowledge ID must remain diagnostics rather than invented catalog identities.

  ### Knowledge discovery and reading
  - knowledge list must report every declared Knowledge, including empty Knowledges, ordered by ID. Text and Markdown tables must show Knowledge, distinct Term count, Viewpoints and reader-facing Description, in that order. Structured results must additionally retain source file and Term Declaration count. Missing Description must stay absent and display as an empty table cell; Scope must not fill that cell.
  - knowledge view must require IDs or whole-name globs and include the same metadata with Description as the reader introduction, authored Scope when present, and the Term Declaration inventory with qualified identity, Term Kind and Group. Text and Markdown must put Scope and Declarations in distinct headed sections separated by blank lines. Unknown exact IDs must fail; unmatched globs must return an empty result.
  - Knowledge results must validate the complete corpus before selection. They must read one published server snapshot when dispatched and the same corpus locally otherwise. Structured results must retain optional Description and Scope, each with content and physical bounds, and Term Declaration inventory in both list and view; presentation selects summary or detail.
  - Knowledge reading is a whole-Knowledge inspection; default Scope omission for term readings does not apply to an explicitly requested Knowledge view.

.remarks
  Chosen: resource command migration preserves existing query semantics and adds Knowledge and Skill discovery. CLI examples, generated Skill documents, local/HTTP reads and refusal behavior are verified together.
}


specification.procedure _List_Term_Declarations_ in operations = {
  _List_Term_Declarations_ lists the Term Declarations of Terms matched by a _Term_Selector_, with Term Kind and Viewpoint.
.relations
  accepts _Term_Selector_
  presents _Group_
.contract
  - `term list [pattern...]` must default to every eligible Term. `--unreferenced` must retain only Terms with no incoming reference from another Term, ignoring self-references.
  - `--unreachable-from <pattern...>` must retain only Terms unreachable from the union of the selected roots through directed references and Relations; roots must themselves count as reachable. Name selection and both graph filters must intersect. Unknown exact roots and an empty root selection must fail.
  - `term list` selection must remain unchanged by required-context expansion.
  - `term list --tree` must present matching Term Declarations as File → Group → Term Declaration, using only authored in Group paths and their implicit prefixes.
  - Each File header must identify its source path and Knowledge ID. Term Declarations without a Group must appear directly under their File.
  - Each Term Declaration leaf must show its local Term name, Term Kind and Viewpoint. Each Term must have one leaf at its authored Group location.
  - Every selection and graph filter must run before tree construction. Only Files and Groups containing matching Term Declarations may appear; an empty selection must report no matches.
  - File and Group counts must count matching descendant Terms, each represented by its one Term Declaration, including direct members.
  - Files must sort by source path. Sibling Groups must sort by Group path and precede direct Term Declaration leaves; sibling Term Declarations must follow the canonical Term ID and Term Kind order.
  - `--with-fileline` must add source locations to Term Declaration leaves without changing tree membership.
  - Tree presentation must not alter the query or structured result. JSON and YAML must retain the complete ordinary result, including each Term Declaration's Group metadata.
.remarks
  Chosen: a tree leaf represents the single Term Declaration of a Term at its authored Group location.
  Chosen: File headers always expose their paths to distinguish equal filenames; leaf locations remain opt-in.
  Chosen: Relation-derived display Groups belong to the Graph and do not appear in the authored CLI tree.
}


specification.procedure _Discover_Terms_ in operations = {
  _Discover_Terms_ finds a ranked, bounded set of Terms from a natural-language question, with source evidence for reading the candidates.
.relations
  references _Query_Service_
  reads _Semantic_Index_
  reads _Corpus_Index_
  references _Search_Corpus_
  references _Read_With_Context_
.contract
  - Selection, section keys, lexical word tokens and exact-name priority must follow the _Query_Service_ case policy. Matching section keys form a deduplicated union of canonical keys. Semantic vector similarity is not a case-sensitive literal filter; the option must not alter Embedding input or vector distance.
  - `corpus discover <question>` must accept nonblank text, `--mode lexical|semantic|hybrid` with default hybrid, and `--limit` from 1 through 100 with default 10.
  - `--knowledge`, `--match`, `--viewpoint`, `--exclude-viewpoint` and `--no-remark` must select eligible Term Declarations before ranking. Default Remarks inclusion must match other corpus reads.
  - `--search-in <section...>` must restrict eligible source chunks to the specified exact section keys before lexical scoring, vector distance and rank fusion. Omission must retain all sections; repeated keys must collapse to one. Keys not declared by any configured Term Kind must fail. Remarks exclusion must win over section inclusion.
  - An explicit section selection must limit lexical matching to the selected section content, excluding standalone Term-name matches and exact-name priority. Semantic inputs must retain the owning Term identity and Term Kind as context; the Definition prefix must be included only when Definition is inside the search selection. Unselected section bodies must not enter Embedding input. Source chunks and the eligible Definition must retain the owning Term Kind.
  - Discovery must validate the complete corpus and exact selectors before ranking; selector ambiguity must use the existing candidate result.
  - Without an explicit section selection, lexical ranking must match Unicode word tokens and exact canonical or local Term names. An exact Term name must precede non-exact candidates in lexical and hybrid modes.
  - Semantic ranking must compare the question Embedding with eligible source chunks; hybrid ranking must combine lexical and semantic rank through reciprocal rank fusion. Ties must resolve by canonical Term ID.
  - Results must return one candidate per eligible Term and at most the requested number of candidates.
  - Each candidate must expose rank, canonical ID, local name, Knowledge, the single eligible Term Kind and full Definition in the existing metadata collections, ranking score and up to three source evidence items with Term Kind, section, excerpt and physical section bounds. The Definition must come from the owning Term Declaration, independently of the matched section.
  - Default text and Markdown discovery output must show a rank-ordered table with only Term and Score columns, retaining headers for empty results. Scores must use four decimal places. With --detail, candidates must use Term and Score headings followed by the eligible Definition, with a blank line between candidates; Term Kind labels, source evidence and locations must also appear. Warnings must remain visible in either presentation. Structured output must retain full metadata, Definitions, evidence and the selected searchSections.
  - Each result must expose operation, question, mode, limit, eligible Term count, candidate count before limiting, truncation, corpus fingerprint and warnings. Scores must be finite retrieval scores, never probabilities or claims of truth.
  - The corpus fingerprint must identify the selected source Term Declarations, section contents and physical bounds; filters may change the selected snapshot fingerprint. It is not a server revision number.
  - Evidence must come from the same validated source snapshot as its identity and location. Similarity must not invent Relations, expand required context or rewrite authored content.
  - Lexical mode must work without a Model or saved Index. Semantic and hybrid modes must refuse an unprepared Model with `corpus index build` guidance; they must not silently fall back to lexical ranking.
  - Discovery must index corpus Term Declaration sections only. External source files remain searchable through _Search_Corpus_; configured externalSources must not enter Embedding input.
  - Local and HTTP reads must run the same discovery operation. Queries must use the current captured corpus and reuse unchanged Embeddings; stale or deleted Term Declarations must not leak from a cache into results.
.remarks
  Chosen: keep literal search stable and expose ranked discovery as a separate operation.
  Chosen: use BM25 for lexical ranking and reciprocal rank fusion with constant 60.
  Candidates are leads. Read their actual contracts with _Read_With_Context_ before acting.
}


concept _Semantic_Index_ in operations = {
  _Semantic_Index_ is the rebuildable local cache of source-chunk Embeddings used to discover Terms by meaning.
.relations
  references _Home_
  references _Term_Declaration_
.contract
  - The Index must be derived from validated Term Declaration identity, Term Kind, Definition and authored section content; Knowledges remain authoritative.
  - Source sections must be split into bounded chunks. Each chunk must retain its owning Term Declaration and section; section expansion must not copy another Term's content into that owner.
  - Inputs exceeding the Model token limit must be split into complete inference windows and combined; no source or question suffix may be silently truncated.
  - The cache identity must include Model identity and revision, Embedding recipe and exact input text. Source position changes alone must not require re-embedding.
  - Model files and cached vectors must remain local under the selected Home's cache directory. Corpus text and questions must never be sent to an inference service.
  - Embedding inference and vector distance computation must run through embedded native libraries; no separate service may be required.
  - Incompatible or malformed cached vectors must never be used. Cache writes must publish complete records transactionally; a failed update must not invalidate previously complete records.
  - Unused cached chunks must not become candidates. Query eligibility and source bounds must come from the current corpus, independently of retained cache records.
  - Model loading must be lazy, and ordinary corpus commands must not import or initialize the inference runtime.
.remarks
  Chosen: multilingual-e5-small, quantized ONNX, mean pooling and normalized 384-dimensional vectors.
  Chosen: pin the Model revision and prefix questions with `query: ` and documents with `passage: `.
  Chosen: 384-code-point source chunks include canonical identity, qualified Term Kind and a 192-character Definition prefix. Long inference inputs use token-checked windows with a normalized, token-weighted mean.
  Chosen: ready Model files are loaded by absolute local path. Transformers.js Hub metadata lookup otherwise contacts the network even with local_files_only; offline verification must block every fetch.
  Chosen: native SQLite cosine distance performs exact search over eligible chunks; approximate indexing is unnecessary for the current corpus size.
}


specification.procedure _Prepare_Semantic_Index_ in operations = {
  _Prepare_Semantic_Index_ prepares the local Model and cached Embeddings for the current corpus, or reports what remains to prepare.
.relations
  changes _Semantic_Index_
  references _Discover_Terms_
.contract
  - `corpus index build` must validate the corpus, download missing pinned Model files, and embed only missing source inputs. It must never upload corpus content.
  - `corpus index status` must report Model identity, cache path, total chunks, cached chunks, missing chunks and whether the current corpus is ready, without downloading or embedding.
  - Build must run locally even when an Aterm Server is running. Status may use the server's captured corpus.
  - Subsequent semantic queries must reuse cached Model files without network access and fill missing Embeddings from their captured corpus before ranking.
  - Build and status must identify their operation, corpus fingerprint and source warnings. Failure must name the unavailable Model or cache and provide recovery guidance.
  - Index preparation must not change source Knowledges, Relations or external files.
.remarks
  Chosen: refresh lazily against each query snapshot. Filesystem publication stays independent of Embedding speed and availability.
}


specification.procedure _Search_Corpus_ in operations = {
  _Search_Corpus_ is the operation that finds Terms whose name, sections or incident Relations contain a literal phrase.
.relations
  references _Query_Service_
  reads _Corpus_Index_
  reads _External_Files_
  references _Relation_
  references _Remarks_
.contract
  - Structured corpus matches must carry the complete matching Term Declaration under termDeclaration, plus section and text. Definition content belongs to termDeclaration.definition; the complete declaration must not be labeled definition.
  - `corpus search` must perform literal matching under the _Query_Service_ case policy across names, every section and incident _Relation_ declarations by default, labeling the first matched section or relation for each Term.
  - `--no-remark` must exclude _Remarks_ from search.
  - Search must inspect section content, including TypeScript sections.
  - With external selection, search must additionally return one match per external physical line containing the same literal phrase under the same case policy. Each match must retain source name, workspace-relative file, one-based physical line, one-based UTF-16 column of the first match, and full line text, ordered by source name, file, line and column.
  - External matches must be carried as `externalMatches` in structured results and label text locations as `ext.<source>: <file>:<line>:<column>` even without `--with-fileline`. They must remain visible when there are no corpus matches.
  - Corpus name, Viewpoint, Relation and Remarks filters must affect corpus matches only; named globs alone must select external files. External reads must not bypass corpus validation or change the corpus result.
  - `corpus search <text>` must reject missing or whitespace-only text. `--match <pattern...>` must restrict matching Term Declaration names using Selector rules; omitted patterns must search all eligible Term Declarations. Results must identify each matching Term, its Term Kind, Viewpoint, matched section or Relation and matching text.
}


specification.procedure _Show_Term_Declarations_ in operations = {
  _Show_Term_Declarations_ is the operation that prints the exact Term Declarations a _Term_Selector_ names, without whole-Term required-context expansion.
.relations
  accepts _Term_Selector_
.contract
  - `term show <pattern...>` must require at least one Selector and return copyable Term Declarations without whole-Term required-context expansion or line-number prefixes. Section expansion resources must be available separately for Markdown presentation; default text and structured Term Declaration content remain authored source.
  - Structured `term show` must retain incident Relations and reference edges without expanding endpoint Contracts; text must contain the selected Term Declarations with Knowledge and Viewpoint annotations, without Scope by default.
  - Default text `term show --section <key>` must print only that section of each selected Term as plain text, in the result's Term order, with no header, fence or annotation; a Term without the section must be omitted, and no Term having it must fail naming the sections there are. Structured output must be unaffected.
}


specification.procedure _Read_With_Context_ in operations = {
  _Read_With_Context_ is the operation that reads Term Declarations with their references in both directions and supplies required Term and section context from _Reference_ occurrences.
.relations
  accepts _Term_Selector_
  expands _Reference_
  references _Relation_
  references _Validate_Corpus_
.contract
  - A dagger-marked section reference must supply that section as expansion context without appending its whole target Term to the Term Declaration list. Required whole-Term references encountered inside the selected section must still contribute view context; other sections of that resource must not cause traversal. Explicitly selected Terms retain their ordinary complete Term Declaration reading.

  ### Selection and presentation
  - Structured results must retain original source with inclusive physical file and line bounds, line numbers and both reference directions.
  - `term view <pattern...>` must require at least one Selector. Default text must provide copyable Term Declarations and their Knowledge and Viewpoint annotations, including required Term Declarations, without Scope; structured results must retain reference information independently of text presentation options.
  - `term view --detail` must print original source with Term Declaration-relative line numbers starting at 1 on the opener; its header must retain inclusive physical bounds.
  - Detailed text must identify every incoming and outgoing referenced Term without duplicates, retaining Relation precedence and exposing reference locations when `--with-fileline` is supplied.
  - `term view --section <key>` must print only that section, as `term show --section` does, of every Term it outputs, selected or pulled in.

  ### Required context
  - `term view` must output its selected Term Declarations first, then recursively include whole-Term required targets in breadth-first order, following each Term Declaration's references in the order they first occur in its text, so that a table of contents reads as written.
  - Each exact name must be output once across all selectors and required targets, including self-references, cycles and shared dependencies.
  - Included targets must receive the same content and presentation as explicitly selected Term Declarations.
  - Ordinary references and _Relation_ edges must not cause this expansion.
  - Missing or ambiguous targets must fail under _Validate_Corpus_, never produce a successful partial view.
}


specification.procedure _Show_Graph_Overview_ in operations = {
  _Show_Graph_Overview_ is the operation that returns selected Terms with their identities and incident edges as a _Concept_Map_.
.relations
  accepts _Term_Selector_
  accepts _Term_Selection_By_File_
  produces _Concept_Map_
.contract
  - `graph overview` must accept Term selectors, _Term_Selection_By_File_ or both, and return selected Term identities and their incident edges in either direction; neighbors' Definitions and further connections must not be expanded.
  - `graph overview` must include every Term declared in a selected file, including Terms without any edge.
  - `graph overview` with Term selectors must fail with narrowing guidance, not silent truncation, when the result exceeds 100 Terms; a selection made only through _Term_Selection_By_File_ is bounded by its files and must not fail on size.
  - `graph overview` must fail when neither a Term selector nor a file selection is supplied.
  - Text must distinguish selected identities from incident connections and retain the authored direction of each connection.
}


specification.procedure _Find_Connecting_Paths_ in operations = {
  _Find_Connecting_Paths_ is the operation that joins two to ten exact Terms through their shortest cycle-free paths and returns the union as a _Concept_Map_.
.relations
  accepts _Term_Selector_
  produces _Concept_Map_
.contract
  ### Path selection
  - `graph connect` must require two to ten distinct exact Terms and choose up to `--limit` cycle-free undirected paths per endpoint pair, shortest first; the limit must default to 5 and reject values that are not positive integers.
  - `graph connect` must distinguish paths by their Term sequences, not parallel Relation labels. Equal-length alternatives must follow deterministic discovery order.

  ### Result
  - `graph connect` must union the chosen edges and Terms, retain canonical directions and explicitly report disconnected pairs. It must not claim globally minimal size or invent a directed data flow.
  - Text must retain requested Term order and identify every successful path with its traversal steps.
  - Text must orient every step from the current to the next Term, using the authored phrase or a reverse arrow; the authored structured edge must remain unchanged.
  - Text must identify the endpoint pair of each path when more than two Terms are selected, and explicitly identify every disconnected pair.
}


specification.procedure _List_Connections_ in operations = {
  _List_Connections_ is the operation that returns the canonical edge list of a _Search_Space_ without Term Definitions or detailed Contracts.
.relations
  accepts _Term_Selector_
  applies _Search_Space_
  references _Concept_Map_
.contract
  - `graph relations` must accept optional selectors; absent selectors must include all edges in the search space. Supplied selectors must select incident edges in either direction.
  - `graph relations` must apply the same canonical grouping, provenance retention, explicit-Relation precedence, origin and Remarks filtering as _Concept_Map_.
  - `graph relations` must return one canonical source-phrase-target triple per retained edge and no Term Declaration content; an empty result must be reported explicitly as no relations.
}


specification.procedure _Find_Occurrences_ in operations = {
  _Find_Occurrences_ is a source-context lookup for exact Term occurrences and matching Term Declaration headers.
.relations
  references _Query_Service_
  accepts _Term_Selector_
  returns _Line_Address_
  references _Remarks_Inclusion_Option_
.contract
  - With external selection, grep must return every exact Term-token occurrence matching its referencePattern among declared Terms, with term, source name, file, physical line, UTF-16 column and full line text in `externalOccurrences`, ordered by source name, file, line and column. Text locations must use `ext.<source>: <file>:<line>:<column>`. Corpus containing-Term and Remarks filters must not filter external text. Matching must use the same token boundary rule as external rename, with the read case policy from _Query_Service_. Returned tokens retain their source spelling; rename still requires exact canonical spelling.
  - `term occurrences <text>` must require an exact Term or quoted Term glob. `--match <pattern...>` must restrict containing Term Declarations using Selector rules; omitted patterns must inspect all Term Declarations.
  - `term occurrences` must include Term Declaration names and scanned references, respecting _Remarks_Inclusion_Option_.
  - `term occurrences` must return each matching line once with up to one preceding and following line, bounded by its Term Declaration.
  - `term occurrences` must label snippets with the containing Term and Term Declaration-relative line range, without requiring physical paths.
}


specification.procedure _Find_Paths_ in operations = {
  _Find_Paths_ is directed graph navigation between two exact Terms in a _Corpus_Index_.
.relations
  reads _Corpus_Index_
.contract
  - `graph path` must validate both endpoints and return at most ten cycle-free paths in breadth-first order.
  - `graph path` must use explicit references and declared Relation edges, including Remarks by default; repeated edge kinds must not duplicate a Term sequence.
  - `graph path` must return no paths for disconnected endpoints and a one-Term path for identical endpoints.
}


specification.procedure _Validate_Corpus_ in operations = {
  _Validate_Corpus_ is the structural and reference checking of Term Declarations and their _Corpus_, producing _Diagnostic_ values.
.relations
  checks _Corpus_
  produces _Diagnostic_
  validates _Reference_
  validates _Relation_
  validates _Term_
  references _Definition_
  references _Remarks_
.contract
  ### Diagnostics
  - It must diagnose malformed Term Declarations, fences, indentation, excess separators and empty _Definition_.
  - It must diagnose duplicate (Knowledge ID, local Term name) pairs, unresolved references and disallowed Term Kinds. Every repeated Term declaration is an error, including declarations with different Term Kinds or Groups. Corpus reads must fail and semantic authoring must stop before writes when a duplicate exists; check must print the diagnostic and exit 1. Guarded raw-file operations remain available for repair. Operations that do not read the corpus must not gain an implicit scan.
  - It must diagnose malformed .relations lines, unresolved declared targets and non-self references absent from that Term Declaration's declarations; declaration ownership must come from the containing Term.
  - It must validate all sections even when output excludes _Remarks_.

  ### Check results
  - `corpus check` must return errors with exit 1; absence of errors must return exit 0.
  - `corpus check --root <pattern...>` must require reachability from the union of matched roots and report each unreachable Term Declaration; unknown exact roots and an empty root selection must fail. `--no-remark` must exclude Remarks edges from reachability only, never from structural validation.
  - Structural success must not claim semantic completeness or correct section separation.
.remarks
  Verification: shared live/candidate checking preserves reachability roots, Knowledge selection and Remarks options; remote command tests cover reachable, unreachable and unknown-root cases.
}


specification.procedure _Edit_Term_Declarations_ in operations = {
  _Edit_Term_Declarations_ is Term-addressed addition, replacement and deletion using Codex-style context patches over the writable sources of one _Home_.
.relations
  patches _Term_Declaration_
  selects _Term_
  references _Home_
  references _Rename_Corpus_Identities_
  references _Validate_Corpus_
  references _Corpus_
.contract
  ### Input and matching
  - `term edit --file <path>` must read its patch as UTF-8 from that path relative to the working directory; omitted `--file` or `--file -` must read standard input. Empty input and an unreadable patch file must fail.
  - _Edit_Term_Declarations_ must require `*** Begin Patch` and `*** End Patch`, with one or more `*** Add Term: <selector>`, `*** Update Term: <selector>` or `*** Delete Term: <selector>` blocks. Update blocks require @@ hunks.
  - A selector must be `[term-kind ]Term[.key]`, using an exact Term and optional Term Kind identifier, as in `procedure \_Order_.contract`. A Term Kind selector may be Viewpoint-qualified, as in `skill.procedure \_Order_.procedure`. An optional Term Kind must match the selected Term's single Term Declaration and acts as a classification guard. An ambiguous unqualified Term must fail listing the candidate Knowledge-qualified identities. Update and Delete require an existing Term. Mismatched Term Kinds, globs and absent section keys must fail. Add and Delete must refuse section selectors.
  - Add must require an explicit destination Knowledge through a qualified Term or --knowledge. Conflicting qualifications, an unknown Knowledge or an existing target identity must fail; no Knowledge is created implicitly.
  - Add must take plus-prefixed lines containing exactly one complete Term Declaration whose local name and optional Term Kind guard match the selector. It must validate against the destination vocabulary, retain an authored Group and append after existing content using the file's line endings. Knowledge directives or additional declarations must fail.
  - Delete must take only its selector, without a body. It removes that complete Term Declaration while retaining Knowledge headers and unrelated content, even when no Terms remain.
  - A complete-Term selector must target the whole Term Declaration; a keyed selector must target literal section source excluding delimiters, preserving author-supplied indentation.
  - A keyed selector on a one-line Term Declaration must fail; the complete-Term selector edits it.
  - A keyed patch must preserve Term Declaration identity, Term Kind, Group path, syntax form and section key and filetype structure. Structural changes require a complete-Term selector.
  - Hunks must use space-prefixed context, minus-prefixed removal and plus-prefixed addition lines. Multiple hunks and Terms must be accepted in one request, in order against the evolving candidate.
  - Matching must try exact lines, then trailing-whitespace normalization, then surrounding-whitespace normalization; an ambiguous match at the first matching level must fail rather than choose a location.
  - `@@ <context>` must advance past that uniquely matching context line; `*** End of File` must constrain the hunk to the selected target's end.
  - A complete-Term Declaration patch may add, change or remove its in Group path without renaming the Term.
  - Update blocks must preserve Term identities and order; Add appends and Delete removes only their selected declarations. Identity changes must use _Rename_Corpus_Identities_.

  ### Validation and storage
  - _Edit_Term_Declarations_ must hold one exclusive write lock in _Home_ for the whole operation and must release it whether or not the operation succeeds.
  - _Edit_Term_Declarations_ must refuse a target outside the configured sources and must not follow a symbolic link into one.
  - Term edit, rename, Knowledge rename and move must honor the implicit package read-only boundary of _Corpus_, including dry-run; a refused multi-file plan must save nothing.
  - _Edit_Term_Declarations_ must apply and parse every candidate before the first source write, rejecting malformed candidate syntax, identity changes through Update, new duplicate (Knowledge ID, local Term name) pairs and undeclared non-self references. The reserved .relations section must be editable through the same section-addressed patch mechanism.
  - Final candidate validation must allow references between Terms added in the same patch, independent of block order. A deleted identity absent from the final candidate must have no surviving corpus references; otherwise the complete patch must fail before saving. References may be repaired or their owners deleted in the same patch.
  - References to never-defined draft targets must retain the existing unresolved-warning behavior for Add and Update; this does not permit breaking references through Delete. These warnings remain errors in subsequent _Validate_Corpus_ queries.
  - `--dry-run` must perform the same candidate checks without saving source changes.
  - _Edit_Term_Declarations_ must check every touched file version before the first write and recheck it at replacement; unrelated bytes and file permissions must be preserved.
  - Multi-file saving must not claim crash atomicity. A write failure must identify the already saved paths; no automatic rollback may overwrite concurrent external changes.
  - Preview and saved authoring results include derivedImpacts: affected derived Terms and their changed source identities, following incoming derived_from Relations transitively.
  - Impact calculation preserves identity mapping across rename and move, considers baseline and candidate Relations, deduplicates and orders results, and does not rewrite explanations automatically.
.remarks
  Chosen: Add and Delete extend the existing patch operation rather than introducing a second batch command. Blocks use the evolving candidate; final reference integrity is checked before any source write.
  ```diff
  *** Begin Patch
  *** Update Term: _Term_.invariants
  @@
  -  - Old contract.
  +  - Revised contract.
  *** End Patch
  ```
  A file may move without changing its Term address.
  - Chosen: separate Skill entrypoints, body reading and short reanchoring; validate derived guidance through explicit impact review.
}


specification.procedure _Rename_Corpus_Identities_ in operations = {
  _Rename_Corpus_Identities_ is the coordinated operation that renames declared Terms or a Knowledge identity together with their scanned references.
.relations
  references _Move_Terms_
  reads _External_Files_
  updates _External_Occurrence_
  updates _Reference_
  updates _Relation_
.contract
  - _Rename_Corpus_Identities_ must use the common guarded authoring save procedure for every selected file: source containment, symlink refusal, content-version checks, Home lock ownership, dry-run and partial-save reporting. Corpus targets are bounded by configured sources; external targets are bounded by the explicitly selected roots.
  - _Rename_Corpus_Identities_ must accept exact from and to names, or one `*` capture in both patterns, substituting the captured middle text into the destination.
  - Rename must update the single Term Declaration of a selected Term and all its references together. Distinct Terms must not merge even when their Term Kinds differ; wildcard mappings apply simultaneously.
  - _Rename_Corpus_Identities_ must reject empty selections, invalid destination names, duplicate source (Knowledge ID, local Term name) pairs and destination Term collisions before writing.
  - _Rename_Corpus_Identities_ must change Term Declarations and exact reference occurrences in every section, preserving dagger annotations.
  - It must replace each occurrence at the position _Reference_ records, and must refuse naming the missing position rather than search the line for a token to replace.
  - _Rename_Corpus_Identities_ must update an owned _Relation_ source through its Term Declaration and rewrite explicit targets, preserving phrases and whitespace.
  - Within Aterm Knowledges, _Rename_Corpus_Identities_ must not replace ordinary strings, escaped tokens, identifier substrings or opaque code.
  - With external selection, rename must replace only occurrences whose exact names appear in its validated corpus rename map, using that map simultaneously over the original text; a replacement must never cascade into another replacement.
  - Rename must plan and validate all corpus and external changes before its first write, preflight every changed file version and path, and check again at each replacement. Invalid external selection, unreadable content or a version conflict detected during preflight must fail before any write. A conflict detected during saving must stop further writes and report files already saved.
  - `--dry-run` must return the same complete corpus and external diff without saving. Successful writes must preserve unrelated text bytes, BOM, line endings and permissions; a failed multi-file save must name already saved files without claiming crash atomicity.
  - External-only rename without a declared source Term, filename rename and code identifier inference must not be performed.
  - A Term rename must preserve its Knowledge. It must update local and qualified occurrences according to authored spelling, including its declaration and all references; cross-Knowledge Term moves must use _Move_Terms_.
  - rename-knowledge must change the selected @knowledge ID and all scanned qualified references to its Terms, including self-qualified references. Local references and filenames must remain unchanged.
  - Knowledge rename must refuse an unknown source or invalid or occupied destination ID. It must share staged validation, guarded save and dry-run with Term rename.
  - Configured externalSources participate by default in both rename operations; an explicit selection replaces them.
}


specification.procedure _Format_Relations_ in operations = {
  _Format_Relations_ is the authoring operation that orders declarations within each Term Declaration's .relations section without changing their owner or other section content.
.relations
  accepts _Term_Selector_
  places _Relation_
  reuses _Edit_Term_Declarations_
  references _Application_
.contract
  ### Selection
  - _Format_Relations_ must select authored source Terms by exact names or globs; omitted patterns must select all Terms.
  - _Format_Relations_ must reject unknown exact names; unmatched globs must produce no changes.

  ### Ordering
  - _Format_Relations_ must sort contiguous declaration lines in .relations by phrase then target using the common code-unit comparison of _Application_.
  - _Format_Relations_ must preserve blank-line boundaries between declaration runs and leave content sections unchanged. Keyed and legacy Term Declarations must use the same declaration ordering; supporting prose and headings belong outside .relations.
  - _Format_Relations_ must retain duplicates and stable order for equal sort keys, and must preserve authored direction, phrase and ownership.
  - _Format_Relations_ must preserve file byte order marks, untouched line bytes and line-ending style, and must be idempotent.

  ### Validation and storage
  - _Format_Relations_ must reject corpus diagnostics before planning writes and validate the complete resulting corpus before saving.
  - _Format_Relations_ must follow the _Edit_Term_Declarations_ write guards, lock ownership, version checks and multi-file failure reporting.
.remarks
  Chosen: declarations are ordered only inside .relations. Contract structure and prose
  order remain untouched, so formatting cannot detach an explanation from its heading.

  Chosen: formatting and reading share the same text comparator. Verification compares
  formatted Relation targets with `graph relations` output where English collation and code-unit
  ordering differ, and checks that a second format changes nothing.
}


specification.procedure _Compare_Corpus_Revisions_ in operations = {
  _Compare_Corpus_Revisions_ is the operation that compares the working tree with a base commit and returns a unified diff of the `.trm` files under the configured sources.
.relations
  reads _Corpus_
  references _Workspace_
.contract
  - `corpus diff` must accept an optional base commit, defaulting to HEAD, and optional file globs matching workspace-relative paths.
  - The comparison must include staged, unstaged, deleted and untracked `.trm` changes without staging them.
  - The comparison must run in the Git repository at _Workspace_; a source outside that repository must be excluded and a path escaping it must be refused.
  - An invalid commit and a workspace that is not a Git repository must fail explicitly.
  - Inspection must not stage, check out or modify the source repository. Private temporary control metadata must be removed after success or failure.
  - Comparisons must use raw stored and working-tree content without repository-configured conversion filters, textconv, external diff, fsmonitor, hooks, pagers or network access. Repository configuration, included config and inherited Git environment must not select commands, another working tree or write destinations.
  - Ordinary repositories and linked worktrees must use the same isolated read mechanism. Git directory/common-directory pointers must be validated as metadata; source metadata symlinks, object alternates and unsupported repository formats/extensions must fail explicitly instead of falling back to unsafe reads. Repository-local ignore files remain applicable; global configuration does not.
  - Private control metadata copying must be bounded to 128 MiB and 100000 files; each Git subprocess must time out after 10 seconds and bound captured output to 32 MiB. Object data must remain read-only and must not be copied in full.
  - An empty comparison must explicitly report no changes; unified diff headers must remain visible.
  - Invalid drafts must remain diffable.
}


concept _Command_ in operations = {
  _Command_ is one named operation of the `aterm` command line, owning its syntax, help and deferred input.
.relations
  references _Jq_Query_
  references _GraphQL_Query_
  references _Workspace_File_Access_
  references _Remote_Command_Execution_
  references _Command_Execution_
  references _MCP_Server_
  references _Move_Terms_
  projects _Query_Service_
  references _Compare_Corpus_Revisions_
  references _Dispatch_Query_
  references _Edit_Term_Declarations_
  references _Format_Relations_
  references _Home_
  references _Operation_Request_
  references _Rename_Corpus_Identities_
  references _HTTP_Server_
  references _Control_UI_
  references _Skill_
  references _Viewpoint_
.contract
  - corpus jq must follow _Jq_Query_ for program input, Knowledge selection, output and failures.
  - CLI commands must use resource then operation: term list/show/view/occurrences/edit/rename/move/format; corpus query/jq/search/check/diff; graph overview/relations/connect/path; knowledge list/view/create/edit/delete/rename; viewpoint list/view/create/edit/delete; file list/read/write/delete; skill list/toc/view/remind/install/update/uninstall; server run; mcp run; ui session list/view; ui command view; ui open; ui explore set/add/remove/clear; ui note send. The bootstrap command init must remain top-level.
  - corpus query must follow _GraphQL_Query_ for its document inputs, projected response formats and error exit status.
  ### Markdown output
  - `--output markdown` must be accepted through the same global format selector as JSON and YAML, including identical-repeat acceptance and conflicting-format refusal.
  - Markdown Term Declaration readings must begin with `## <term-kind> <Term>`, then Definition content without a heading, then `### <section>` and content in source Schema order. Each Term is presented through its single Term Declaration.
  - Markdown Term Declaration readings must omit Knowledge, Viewpoint, Scope and Group annotations. A reading containing several Knowledges must use qualified Term names in its Term Declaration headings; a single-Knowledge reading uses local names.
  - Body Markdown, Term spelling and authored headings must remain intact; no automatic hyperlinks or anchors may be added. Relation declaration rows must become bullets, and TypeScript, YAML and JSON sections must be fenced with their declared language.
  - Markdown must expand dagger-marked section references at their authored positions using the supplied sectionContext. Unmarked references and whole-Term daggers remain authored text. `--no-remark` must exclude Remarks content and expansion through Remarks, including an explicit Remarks section target.
  - Markdown `--section` must return only selected section content, with expansion and language-appropriate code fencing but no generated heading. Empty present sections succeed; no selected Term Declaration having that section fails.
  - Markdown must honor --with-fileline, --detail and --tree. Detail adds source and both reference directions; tree retains filtered File / Group / Term Declaration hierarchy and counts. Term listing uses Term, Term Kind and Viewpoint columns; search and grep use excerpts; Relations use Source / Phrase / Target rows; changes use a diff fence; check uses a summary and diagnostic list. Formats must not change query selection or exit status.
  - Generated Markdown output must end with two empty lines after its final content line, represented by three trailing newline characters. Adjacent Term Declaration blocks must have the same two-empty-line separation. Original-file passthrough must preserve the authored ending instead of applying generated-output spacing.

  ### List and reading layout
  - Every flat list must use an aligned table with one item per row, shared between text and Markdown. Table cells must collapse embedded whitespace to one space without truncating content; Unicode display width must determine padding. Empty lists must show headers and no data rows.
  - Term lists must show Term, Term Kind and Viewpoint, with Source only on request. Knowledge list tables must show exactly Knowledge, Terms, Viewpoints and Description, in that order. File and Term Declaration count remain available in Knowledge view and structured results. Viewpoint lists must show Viewpoint and Description. Skill lists must show qualified Term and Description. Installation state belongs to lifecycle results and its manifest, not the reading catalog.
  - The explicit term list --tree mode must keep its authored hierarchy. JSON/YAML must retain structured results rather than text tables.
  - Generated readings must distinguish metadata, Scope, declarations and reference or Relation sections using explicit headings and blank lines. Knowledge view must place reader-facing Description after the Knowledge heading and separate writer-facing Scope from Declarations. Copyable Aterm text must use comments for context headings; original Viewpoint files and selected section bodies must remain verbatim.

  ### Command behavior
  - CLI commands must use resource then operation: term list/show/view/occurrences/edit/rename/move/format; corpus query/jq/search/check/diff; graph overview/relations/connect/path; knowledge list/view/create/edit/delete/rename; viewpoint list/view/create/edit/delete; file list/read/write/delete; skill list/toc/view/remind/install/update/uninstall; skill list/view/remind/export/review; server run; mcp run; ui session list/view; ui command view; ui open; ui explore set/add/remove/clear; ui note send. The bootstrap command init must remain top-level.
  - A _Command_ must declare its full command path, removed flat spelling when applicable, its argument syntax, its options and its summary, behavior and example help in one place; group and leaf registration must be mechanical from that declaration. One shared invocation path must execute all leaves.
  - A resource group without an operation must print its help and execute nothing. A read requiring names must refuse their omission; extra positional arguments and unsupported options must fail.
  - Recovery hints for missing Terms and identity changes must name the canonical resource command path.
  - Removed flat commands must fail with their new command paths rather than remain aliases. Removed Skill lifecycle flags and old implicit Skill/Viewpoint reads must fail with subcommand guidance.
  - CLI paths and programmatic operation IDs must be distinct declarations; commands map to explicit query operations. Skill list/toc/view/remind maps to skill-list/skill-toc/skill-view/skill-remind. The result uses skills and selectors use skillTerms; the former skill/newskill/Workflow shapes are removed under protocol 21.
  - A query command must produce an _Operation_Request_ and must not read the filesystem, select the home or render results itself.
  - `--server`, `--home`, `--output` and `--knowledge` must be global options; every other option belongs to its command.
  - Read and authoring queries must select an existing Home. Skill list/toc/view/remind may use their shipped fallback when discovery finds none; initialization must use its explicit destination; Skill installation must use the source-selection and destination rules of _Skill_.
  - `init [directory]` must default to the working directory and return the created Home, configuration and source paths. Its target must not be redirected by the global `--home` option.
  - Successful commands must exit 0; invalid input, failed reads or writes, and refused semantic validation must fail without claiming success. Raw writes report storage success separately from corpus validity, following _Workspace_File_Access_. `--help` must describe command syntax and behavior without executing the operation; `--version` must report the tool version.
  - Command output must follow its reading contract: ordinary reads default to text, Skill readings to shared Markdown and corpus query to the GraphQL JSON response and corpus jq to its output values array. `--output json` and `--output yaml` must serialize the complete structured result; invalid or conflicting formats must fail, while repeated identical formats must be accepted.
  - `--with-fileline` on `term list`, `corpus search`, `term show`, `term view`, `graph overview`, `graph connect`, `graph relations`, `term occurrences` and `corpus check` must expose source locations without changing selection. `term view --detail` must expose source bounds and both reference directions. `term list --tree` must expose the authored File / Group / Term Declaration hierarchy. These text options must not change structured results.
  - `--section` on `term show` and `term view` must override detailed text presentation and context annotations; it must not filter the structured result.
  - UI commands must follow _Control_UI_, declare their own input projection and use the shared invocation and output adapters. --session is required for control; optional --command-id supplies a UUID for deduplicated retries.
  - `mcp run` must follow _MCP_Server_ for stdio startup and optional HTTP hosting.
  - `server run` must start _HTTP_Server_ locally; ordinary queries must route through _Dispatch_Query_.
  - `init` must include server.port set to 43127 in newly created configuration.
  - `init` must include skills.sync and skills.uninstall Bash commands using npx skills for project-scoped Claude Code and Codex installation. Sync must remove stale managed names from every Agent in project scope then add only current managed names from ATERM_SKILLS_DIR to Claude Code and Codex; uninstall must remove supplied managed names from every Agent in project scope. These removals include same-name connections made by other Agents, but never other names or global installations. Init must write configuration without running these commands or installing Agent Skills.
  - init must create the configuration, first source directory and empty viewpoints directory inside _Home_. It must select specification, generic, domain and skill through useViewpoints, reading the packaged documents directly. Existing unbound local Viewpoint files must remain untouched.
  - `init` must refuse a _Home_ that already holds a configuration or a _Viewpoint_ file of a name it ships, naming the path it refused, and must overwrite nothing.
  - Skill commands are list/toc/view/remind/install/update/uninstall and follow _Skill_. The former newskill and workflow command families are removed.
.remarks
  Terminology correction: Knowledge help, search examples and initialization guidance follow the canonical Knowledge name; whole Knowledge/Viewpoint writes accept complete source.
  Chosen: corpus discover and corpus index build/status expose ranked retrieval and explicit local Model preparation through the shared command declaration mechanism.
  Initialization guidance correction: the generated configuration example declares @knowledge before @viewpoints, matching Knowledge syntax. Only explanatory comments changed; default values and initialization behavior remain unchanged. Verified by initialization tests and a fresh installed-package Home.
  Chosen: resource-first CLI paths replace the inherited flat command surface. Init remains the bootstrap operation. Existing query IDs stay stable for HTTP and library consumers; added read operations require the new shared protocol.

  Chosen: initialization is not transactional. A refusal partway through leaves the files
  already created and deletes nothing it did not write; a rerun refuses again, naming what
  exists. The alternative, rolling back, would let a failed `init` remove a file a user had
  put there. Verified by an initialization over a pre-existing Viewpoint file, which keeps
  the user's bytes and names that path.
  Chosen: resource command migration preserves existing query semantics and adds Knowledge and Skill discovery. CLI examples, generated Skill documents, local/HTTP reads and refusal behavior are verified together.
  Version correction: --version reads the installed CLI package manifest, shared with MCP implementation metadata, so a published release cannot retain the previous hardcoded development version.
}


concept _Operation_Request_ in operations = {
  The validated request produced by a command, naming one operation and its accepted selectors, filters and write options.
.relations
  references _Jq_Query_
  references _Query_Service_
  references _GraphQL_Query_
  selects _Term_
  references _Command_
.contract
  - jq is a read operation accepting the required jq envelope {program, knowledge?} defined by _Jq_Query_ and optional caseSensitive. Selection belongs to the envelope; unrelated operation fields must be refused.
  - Optional boolean caseSensitive selects the _Query_Service_ read matching policy. Selector-bearing corpus reads, validation, changes, discovery, Knowledge/Viewpoint/Skill reads and GraphQL accept it. Authoring, Index preparation/status and lifecycle actions reject it.
  - graphql is a read operation accepting the required graphql envelope defined by _GraphQL_Query_ and the optional caseSensitive read setting. Its variables and filters belong inside that envelope; unrelated top-level fields must fail.
  - Optional `searchSections` must be a nonempty array of exact section keys on discover only. It selects searched content, independently of a CLI output-section selector. Unknown keys must fail against the configured vocabulary before retrieval.
  - Discovery requests must use `question`, `searchMode` and `limit`; question must be nonblank and at most 4000 characters, mode must be lexical, semantic or hybrid, and the discovery limit must be at most 100. `index-build` and `index-status` must accept no query fields.
  - An _Operation_Request_ must name exactly one read, validation, authoring, changes, Viewpoint, Skill or Index preparation operation. Read operations include discover, index-status, knowledge-list, knowledge-view and skill-list; knowledge-view requires nonempty knowledgeIds. index-build prepares the selected server or local Home and must not be accepted by the read-only HTTP query endpoint. Skill read operations are skill-list, skill-toc, skill-view and skill-remind; toc/view/remind require nonempty skillTerms. No implicit modeling default is selected. Initialization and Skill installation lifecycle operations must be separate local actions, not corpus queries.
  - An _Operation_Request_ must be validated before execution; an unknown field and a field the named operation does not accept must both fail, naming the field and the operations that do accept it.
  - Which fields each operation accepts must be declared in one place, and that declaration must be the only validation of a request's shape. An operation must not restate it, so no second list can drift from the first.
  - A field the named operation does not use must be rejected, never accepted and ignored. This holds for every field alike; no field may be exempt from the rule because it is harmless to ignore.
  - Every query field must have one meaning: `termPatterns` selects Terms by exact name or whole-name glob; `files` selects workspace-relative source file paths; `skillTerms` selects exact local or qualified Skill Terms; `knowledgeIds` selects Knowledge IDs by exact name or whole-name glob; `viewpoints` selects bound Viewpoints by exact name or whole-name glob.
  - `searchText` must be a literal phrase for search under the _Query_Service_ case policy; `referencePattern` must be an exact Term or whole-name glob identifying grep targets. Grep's `termPatterns` must independently select the containing Term Declarations.
  - Removed ambiguous fields `patterns` and `text` must be rejected, not retained as alternate spellings. CLI resource paths must map to these semantic fields; existing programmatic operation IDs remain stable.
  - `externalSources` must represent a map of source names to file globs on search, grep, move and both rename operations only; all other operations must refuse it. Legacy externalDirectories and externalExtensions request fields must be rejected.
  - An _Operation_Request_ must not override the Home or configured corpus sources. Its `files` selectors must operate only within the configured corpus or its Git comparison, never select a different Home or source.
  - knowledge must select one declared Knowledge for Term queries, edit, rename, move, format and Home Guide or corpus Skill reads. Unsupported operations must reject it.
  - An exact unqualified selector must resolve across Knowledges before Term Kind or Viewpoint filtering. One matching Term is selected; several produce candidate identities, Term Kinds, source locations and --knowledge guidance.
  - Read ambiguity must retain candidate identities without guessing a selection. Term Declaration-reading commands return a candidate result with no partially executed request and CLI exit 0; field-projected response errors follow _GraphQL_Query_. Authoring ambiguity must refuse without writes and exit 1.
  - An unqualified glob matches local names across Knowledges; a qualified glob matches canonical IDs. knowledge restricts selected targets; a conflicting explicit qualifier must fail. Required reading context can cross that boundary.
.remarks
  Chosen: change the programmatic request shape instead of preserving overloaded names.
  API callers use semantic field names; the resource-first CLI projects the same requests.
  Operation-field validation remains the single declaration of accepted request fields.
  Verification: an unsupported field reports its own request path; the limit refusal
  regression check prevents an unrelated Knowledge prefix from entering diagnostics.
  Chosen: resource command migration preserves existing query semantics and adds Knowledge and Skill discovery. CLI examples, generated Skill documents, local/HTTP reads and refusal behavior are verified together.
}


concept _External_Files_ in operations = {
  _External_Files_ is the named collection of UTF-8 text files selected alongside the corpus by file globs.
.relations
  references _Configuration_
.contract
  - Top-level externalSources in _Configuration_ must map source names to one nonempty file glob each. Names must begin with an ASCII letter and contain only ASCII letters, digits, underscores and hyphens. An empty map must select no files.
  - Search and grep must include configured sources regardless of whether server is configured or running, and regardless of server.watchExternal. Explicit `--external <name=glob...>` options must replace the configured map for that request. CLI names must be unique and each argument must contain both name and glob. The externalSources query field must carry the same map; an explicit empty map must disable configured sources for that request. Legacy --external-ext must be rejected.
  - Term move, Term rename and Knowledge rename must use configured externalSources unless an explicit selection replaces it. Without configuration or explicit selection, no external files may be read or written.
  - Relative globs must resolve against the Workspace; absolute globs may select files outside it. Matching must use case-sensitive Node path.matchesGlob semantics, including *, **, ?, character classes and brace alternatives. Patterns must use forward slashes, with no NUL, newline, backslash or leading negation. A glob matching nothing, including a missing literal directory prefix, must select nothing.
  - Selection must recurse under each glob's literal directory prefix, skip .git, node_modules and names beginning with .aterm, and never select corpus .trm files as external text. Symlinks in a literal prefix must fail; descendant symlinks must be skipped with warnings. Overlapping prefixes must not duplicate file reads or writes.
  - A file matching several named sources must retain every source name. Search and grep must report each match under each matching source, ordered by source name, workspace-relative file, line and column using the common code-unit comparison. Rename must change that file only once. Paths outside the Workspace must retain relative parent components.
  - Every selected file must decode losslessly as UTF-8 without NUL. An unreadable root or selected file, or invalid encoding, must fail before any write; it must not silently omit possible matches.
  - Each selected file must retain its path, absolute path, owning literal directory prefix, matching source names, text and content version for the common guarded write procedure.
  - External files must not contribute Term Declarations, Relations, reference validation or graph traversal to the corpus.
.remarks
  Chosen: source selection belongs to configuration independently of execution transport.
  One named glob mechanism serves configuration and explicit query overrides; no legacy
  directory-plus-extension selection remains. Result source names expose provenance, while
  physical file identity deduplicates writes. server.watchExternal only controls caching
  and background observation; disabling it uses on-demand reads of the same selection.
}


concept _External_Occurrence_ in operations = {
  _External_Occurrence_ is a fully bounded Term token in an external text file, independent of the file's programming language.
.relations
  occurs in _External_Files_
.contract
  - An occurrence must use the Term name grammar and must not adjoin a Unicode identifier-continuation character or a dollar sign on either side; an identifier substring must not count.
  - Occurrences in comments, strings, escaped text and code must be treated alike. No code identifier, naming-style variant or language-level symbol may be inferred from a Term.
  - An occurrence must remain external text rather than a corpus Reference; an unknown Term spelling in external text must not invalidate the corpus.
  - Only qualified Term tokens have semantic identity in external source text; unqualified tokens remain searchable literal text and must not be tracked or renamed.
.remarks
  Given: rename targets exact Term spelling only. Chosen: external matching is lexical,
  not AST-based; the file's language and quoting do not change the token boundary rule.
}


concept _HTTP_Server_ in operations = {
  _HTTP_Server_ is the resident HTTP endpoint for the application behavior of one selected Home, available to CLI clients and the same-origin _Explorer_.
.relations
  references _Usage_Log_
  exposes _GraphQL_Query_
  references _Command_Execution_
  exposes _MCP_Server_
  maintains _Corpus_Snapshot_
  exposes _Query_Service_
  references _Explorer_
  tracks _UI_Session_
  exposes _Control_UI_
.contract
  - Execution evidence must follow _Usage_Log_.
  - The public read endpoint POST /graphql must follow _GraphQL_Query_ on the same listener and published snapshot. Its public envelope is independent of the private /api/query protocol.
  ### HTTP service
  - `aterm server run` must run in the foreground, use configured `server.port` and `server.host` unless --port or --host overrides them, report its listen address and refuse an occupied address or port without changing the existing listener. Omitting host must retain loopback-only listening; wildcard listening must permit connections through the machine's reachable network interfaces.
  - The server must expose `GET /api/health` with the current shared protocol version, canonical Home path, revision and refresh readiness, and `POST /api/query` with a JSON object containing `home` and `query`. Successful query responses must be the same structured application result as local execution; failures must carry `error.code` and `error.message`.
  - /api/query requests must require `Content-Type: application/json` and the matching `X-Aterm-Protocol` header, match the server's canonical Home, and use the existing query schema. Request bodies above 8 MiB must fail. Unknown routes and unsupported methods must return 404 and 405 respectively; rejected inputs must return 400 and a wrong Home 409.
  - Every HTTP request must supply a valid Host authority on the listening port or the explicitly configured server.publicOrigin. A concrete listener must accept its configured address; a loopback listener must also accept localhost, 127.0.0.1 and ::1 as Host aliases. A wildcard listener must accept IP addresses and hostnames supplied by remote clients. Invalid Host or Origin must return 403 before serving assets, health or queries.
  - A supplied Origin must equal the HTTP origin of the request's Host, including its port, or the explicitly configured server.publicOrigin. An absent Origin must remain valid for direct navigation and CLI requests. The server must not grant cross-origin access or trust forwarded headers. MCP authoring follows _MCP_Server_; initialization, installation and arbitrary process execution remain local-only.
  - The corpus query endpoint must accept read queries only and reject edit, move, rename, rename-knowledge and format, including dry-run, before execution. Read queries must execute against the latest complete published snapshot without triggering reconciliation or waiting for background refreshes or other read requests. Automatic local CLI dispatch keeps writes on the filesystem; explicit remote execution follows _Command_Execution_ and waits for publication.
  - SIGINT and SIGTERM must stop the listener, browser connections, filesystem watchers and pending debounce or watcher-retry timers, settle pending UI commands as unknown, finish active requests and exit. Host or port configuration changes require restart; the running listener must not silently migrate.
  - HTTP error responses must not stop the server; repaired files must become usable without restarting.
  - The shared protocol must expose Knowledge Descriptions, original Viewpoint sources, Knowledge identities, candidate results, Relation type and structural metadata, section-reference selectors and section expansion context consistently with filesystem execution. An incompatible running server must be refused.
  - Knowledge directive changes and file moves must invalidate the snapshot and cached source references just like Term Declaration changes.
  - POST /api/ui must accept a JSON envelope with home and request, require the shared protocol header and dispatch only the closed UI control vocabulary. UI control authorization must precede execution.
  - /api/ui/events must accept same-origin WebSocket upgrades. Registration must match protocol and canonical Home; messages must validate the closed registration, state and acknowledgement schemas.
  - Browser connections must receive health/revision changes on publication and after registration. Heartbeat must detect abandoned connections without treating a hidden tab as disconnected merely for being hidden.
  - --port must allow cold recovery startup even when configuration cannot load; --host defaults to loopback. Raw file commands and help must remain reachable throughout repair.
.remarks
  Chosen: protocol 24 adds field-local jq bindings and the graphql UI Session view. Refuse incompatible peers before dispatch. GET /queries serves the packaged browser workspace; POST /graphql keeps the public query envelope.
  Chosen: protocol 23 includes the jq request and values result and the unified GraphQL collections with predicates and ordering. Current clients refuse older peers so unsupported reads cannot silently use different behavior. The public GraphQL envelope is unchanged; Viewpoint and Term Kind collection selections now use nodes.
  Chosen: protocol 21 unifies the Skill operations and removes legacy Workflow query shapes. It also includes selected built-in vocabularies, private guidance availability and default resource write protection, in addition to case-insensitive read defaults. Refuse older peers rather than execute requests under different configuration semantics.
  Chosen: protocol 18 migrates Term Declaration and Term Kind names throughout structured results, Viewpoint metadata and UI state. Term Declarations use the /term-declarations route and UI view value; browser Term Kind filtering uses termKind. Older clients and servers must not exchange these incompatible shapes.
  Chosen: protocol 17 makes one Term Declaration per Term unconditional and uses the canonical Term ID as Term Declaration identity. Older servers must be restarted before current clients connect.
  Chosen: protocol 13 adds discovery section selection and full candidate Definitions; clients must not consume an older result as the new shape.
  Chosen: discovery captures the published corpus before asynchronous Embedding work; slow or unavailable inference must not block filesystem publication or other reads. Protocol 12 introduces ranked discovery and Index status.
  Verification: the packaged Explore and shared Graph state modules use the same fixed-asset allowlist and isolation headers; the HTTP protocol and query behavior are unchanged.
  Verification: protocol 8 distinguishes Viewpoint-qualified Term Kind semantics from older server snapshots.
  Chosen: one HTTP service and versioned JSON boundary serve CLI and browser clients; configured listening controls network reachability.
  Chosen: the packaged graph-geometry.js module uses the same fixed-asset allowlist and isolation headers as the other Explorer modules.
  Chosen: packaged markdown.js and mermaid.js use the same fixed-asset allowlist. Image policy permits HTTP(S) images and embedded image data while scripts and application modules remain same-origin.
  Chosen: external access is opt-in through server.host; wildcard listening permits any valid Host on this port while Origin remains same-origin. The service has no authentication and exposes its configured corpus and source snippets to clients that can reach it. Authentication and TLS termination belong to a proxy; server.publicOrigin permits an explicit proxy origin. A daemon manager remains outside scope.
  Chosen: resource command migration preserves existing query semantics and adds Knowledge and Skill discovery. CLI examples, generated Skill documents, local/HTTP reads and refusal behavior are verified together.
}


concept _Corpus_Snapshot_ in operations = {
  _Corpus_Snapshot_ is the current configuration, scanned Knowledges and diagnostics held by one running server.
.relations
  reflects _Corpus_
  reads _Configuration_
.contract
  - The snapshot must observe Home configuration, selected source directories, enabled packaged sources and bound Viewpoint files through native file monitoring. Creation, content edits, removal and rename must invalidate the selected state. Configured source and Viewpoint changes must update the watched set. Paths outside the selected roots and bound files, and excluded source subdirectories, must not request a refresh.
  - Missing roots and replaced directories must remain observable through their ancestors. Viewpoint bindings must be observed before their contents load, so creating or repairing a newly bound file can recover an invalid configuration without another configuration edit.
  - Native file events must reset a trailing debounce timer configured by server.debounceMs. A burst must publish one settled update rather than a partial update for each event; scans overtaken by events must not publish. There must be no periodic corpus scan or configuration and external-content reread while idle. Requests must read the current published snapshot immediately, including during pending debounce or in-flight reconciliation. Filesystem writes become visible eventually after a complete refresh is published.
  - A native watcher setup failure must make readiness false and prevent stale successful reads. Retrying failed observation must not repeatedly scan the corpus; restored observation must request a settled refresh. Configuration edits must remain usable to remove failed watch targets.
  - Changed configuration, content versions or diagnostics must advance a revision and replace the complete published state. An unchanged scan must reuse the read application and index.
  - Once detected by background reconciliation, invalid configuration or failed refresh must prevent stale successful query responses and be visible through health readiness. A valid configuration with an invalid corpus must retain diagnostics so check can report them normally.
  - When server.watchExternal is true and top-level externalSources is configured, the snapshot must observe the literal directory prefixes of those globs and retain selected external source text alongside corpus state, refreshing exact Term occurrences and source names after changes. Ancestor monitoring must detect creation of missing prefixes. When false it must neither watch nor pre-read those external files; search and grep must still read the configured sources on demand. External files must never become corpus Knowledges or graph edges. An external read error must fail queries using that selection without blocking unrelated corpus queries; repair must recover without restart. Explicit per-request external selection and Git changes must remain fresh on demand.
  - Background refreshes must serialize with each other and publish complete scans by replacing the read application. Each request must retain the application captured at its start; it must not claim atomicity with concurrent writes by external processes.
  - Knowledge header changes, duplicate Knowledge declarations and file moves must invalidate the same snapshot and query caches as Term Declaration edits; repaired Knowledge identity must recover without restart.
  - Explicit server command writes must request reconciliation before completing. Their subsequent reads see saved content or diagnostics; ordinary external file events retain trailing debounce.
.remarks
  Chosen: native file monitoring replaces the one-second full scan. Ancestor observation
  detects new roots and directory replacement; only failed watcher setup is retried.
  This avoids reparsing an unchanged corpus continuously. Silent event loss by an underlying
  filesystem is not repaired by periodic polling; native watch support is required.
  Reads reuse memory without waiting for freshness; pending changes leave the last
  published snapshot readable.
  Explicit external selections and Git comparisons remain on-demand operations. Verified
  that repeated reads do not scan and reads complete during a blocked background refresh.
  Revision is local to one process.
  Verification: idle and unrelated-file tests observe no configuration, corpus or external
  rereads. Native-event tests cover missing roots, directory replacement, atomic saves,
  invalid Viewpoint recovery, failed-watch recovery and shutdown during debounce.
  Verification: useDefaultKnowledge and useViewpoints changes replace the published corpus without a listener restart; packaged guidance stays readable in either mode. Observe packaged Knowledge and Viewpoint directories even when they are hidden from ordinary queries. Package upgrades still require restarting the process that loaded the old implementation.
}


specification.procedure _Dispatch_Query_ in operations = {
  _Dispatch_Query_ performs automatic local discovery for a CLI query to the matching local HTTP server when present, otherwise to the same filesystem-backed application.
.relations
  references _Remote_Command_Execution_
  sends _Operation_Request_
  discovers _HTTP_Server_
.contract
  - Discovery and Index status must dispatch as reads. Index build must remain a local cache-preparation action. Discovery has a 120-second request deadline to admit incremental local Embedding work; other reads retain their existing deadline.
  - Without explicit --server, Dispatch must resolve Home and configuration locally and prepare the same query before choosing transport. Presentation and exit status must remain CLI responsibilities and must not change with transport.
  - Without server configuration dispatch must execute locally. With configuration it must probe health at server.host and server.port and verify protocol version and canonical Home before sending the query. Discovery must use 127.0.0.1 for an IPv4 wildcard and ::1 for an IPv6 wildcard, including equivalent IPv6 spellings; concrete addresses and localhost must use their configured destination. IPv6 URLs must use brackets.
  - Health discovery must time out after 1500 ms; a query must allow 30000 ms plus the configured debounceMs before timing out. Timeout must be an error rather than a fallback.
  - Only a refused health connection may indicate an absent server and permit local fallback. A timeout, incompatible response, wrong Home or server rejection must fail explicitly.
  - Once query submission begins, any failure must be reported without a hidden local retry.
  - Edit, move, rename, rename-knowledge and format, including dry-run, must run locally during automatic discovery without probing the server. Initialization, Skill installation lifecycle operations and the foreground server command must remain local lifecycle operations.
.remarks
  Chosen: automatic fallback is limited to absence, not arbitrary network failure.
  Automatic local discovery does not send writes over HTTP. Explicit --server uses _Remote_Command_Execution_ instead; it needs no local Home.
  Behavior preservation: application dispatch separates operations that do not use a read snapshot, snapshot reads, authoring, selection validation, GraphQL handling and external-source augmentation. Snapshot choice, scan timing, GraphQL cache/error conversion and query validation order remain unchanged; differential requests and integration tests verify these boundaries.
}


concept _Working_Set_ in operations = {
  An ordered set of canonical Terms deliberately placed together for reading and comparison in the Main View.
.relations
  belongs to _Explorer_
  contains _Term_
  presents _Term_Declaration_
  differs from _Graph_Selection_
  accompanies _Context_Pane_
.contract
  - Term Declarations, References and Graph Explore must use one Working Set.
  - Membership must use canonical qualified Term identities and retain insertion order without duplicates.
  - A Term must contribute its complete single Term Declaration regardless of its matching library occurrence.
  - Tree Term-name activation must replace the Working Set with that Term.
  - Every Tree Term row must expose a checkbox that adds or removes that Term without changing the reading target, active Main View, filters or library scroll.
  - All Tree occurrences of a Term must reflect its membership immediately.
  - Every Knowledge and Group branch must expose a checkbox for its descendant Terms, including collapsed descendants. It must operate on the matching subtree after current search and filters, deduplicate canonical Terms and apply one membership change in Tree order without changing members outside that subtree.
  - A branch checkbox must be checked when all matching descendants belong to the Working Set, unchecked when none do, and indeterminate when only some do. Checking an unchecked or indeterminate branch must add all matching descendants; unchecking a checked branch must remove them.
  - Branch membership must remain separate from expansion and Knowledge browsing, preserve the reading target, Main View, filters and library scroll, and update all related Term and branch checkboxes immediately.
  - Other Term hyperlinks must read Context without changing membership.
  - Every displayed Term label or hyperlink must support dragging to the Main View from navigation, reading content, Notes, Graph and the Main View itself.
  - A drop must add the Term once, preserve the active Main View and reading target, and suppress the source click. Dropping outside the Main View or cancelling must leave membership unchanged.
  - Literal source code must remain code rather than becoming a Term link or drag target.
  - Graph Term-label dragging must remain separate from Graph Selection and Node-position dragging.
  - Term Declarations and References must present one collapsible card per Term containing its single Term Declaration.
  - A collapsed card must show Term name, Knowledge, Term Kind attributes and each Term Declaration's Definition.
  - The first card must initially expand; other new cards must initially collapse. Explicit expansion must survive tab changes, additions and corpus refresh within the session.
  - Empty membership must remain explicit and must not repopulate itself during refresh.
  - Corpus refresh must remove unavailable members and retain surviving order.
  - Structure must retain the whole corpus independently of membership. Membership changes must not select or hide Structure Nodes.
.remarks
  Chosen: Working Set is shared comparison context; the Context reading target and Graph Selection remain independent.
  Chosen: Select Working Set and hiding Structure surroundings are deferred.
  Verification: ordered identity, duplicate prevention, shared membership and Undo are covered by model tests. Browser checks cover Tree replacement and toggles, card expansion, HTML and SVG dragging, empty-set reload and history.
}


concept _Explorer_ in operations = {
  _Explorer_ is the read-only browser interface for finding and reading Terms from one running server.
.relations
  references _Term_
  reads _HTTP_Server_
  registers _UI_Session_
  supports _Control_UI_
  presents _Term_
  presents _Group_
  has_part _GraphQL_Workspace_
  has_part _Graph_View_
  has_part _Context_Pane_
  uses _Working_Set_
  presents _Markdown_Note_
.contract
  - The application menu must provide Explorer and GraphQL destinations. The GraphQL destination follows _GraphQL_Workspace_; returning to Explorer preserves the Working Set, navigation and Context.
  - The server root must serve the Explorer and its fixed packaged assets on the same origin; no arbitrary filesystem path may be served. The interface must use the shared corpus read API and UI connection protocol and expose no corpus write controls.
  - The library must organize Term Declarations by their authored Group hierarchy, support name and body search, and offer Term Kind and Viewpoint filters. A Term must appear when its single Term Declaration matches; detail must show that complete Term Declaration in its Schema order.
  - The library must expose Knowledge roots and Group prefixes as independently collapsible outline branches, initially collapsed in both All Knowledge and a selected Knowledge. Their state must survive tab changes, Knowledge selection and refresh within the session. Newly discovered branches must start collapsed. Search must expose matching branches while preserving the prior expansion state; clearing search must restore it.
  - Knowledge and Group name activation in the Tree must toggle only that branch's expansion, preserving selected Knowledge, filters, reading target and Working Set membership. Knowledge browsing must use the Knowledge chooser. Tree rows must omit trailing navigation arrows; branch disclosure indicators must communicate expansion.
  - The library and Graph must derive the same authored hierarchy; Group paths must sort canonically and member Term Declarations must sort by Term. Term Declarations without a Group must remain directly under Knowledge.
  - The selected Term must expose Term Kind, Viewpoint, Group path when declared, Knowledge, scope, sections, incoming and outgoing authored Relations, corpus reference occurrences and external source snippets with source names and physical locations labeled ext.<source>. Selecting a Term reference outside the Tree must read it in Context without changing the Main View or Working Set.
  - The .relations section must render each declaration on a separate row with a clickable target in both Term Declarations and _Context_Pane_.
  - Working Set membership, Context target, search and filters must be represented in route state; browser history must restore them. Asynchronous results for older reading targets must not replace newer ones.
  - The Explorer must observe server health and revision, refresh changed data, preserve selection and reading position, and distinguish disconnection, refresh errors and corpus diagnostics from successful current data. Invalid state must not be presented as up to date.
  - Empty search, removed Terms and unavailable data must have explicit states and a way to recover. Navigation and actions must be keyboard accessible with visible focus; narrow screens must retain access to the library and context.
  - Term Declarations, References and Graph must share the Working Set, app shell and left navigation. The header must show the first Term's local name, followed by N more for remaining members, with Term and Term Declaration counts below. Empty membership must show No Terms. The first name must support Context reading and dragging; full qualified identity and remaining names must remain available. The browser title must use the same first-Term summary.
  - A single tab row must place Term Declarations, References and Graph on the left and Context and Note on the right as independent tab groups. The common header and tabs must remain above the independently scrolling Main View and reader.
  - Switching Main Views must preserve membership, Context, navigation visibility, width, filters and library scroll. Graph controls must be visually separate below the tab row and confined to the Main View.
  - Collapsed navigation must expose a chevron that opens the library on pointer hover. A pin icon inside the library must toggle its pinned state and visibly indicate whether it is pinned. The interface must not require a mode selector or menu for these actions.
  - Pinned navigation must remain visible and dock beside the Main View on wide screens. Unpinning must keep the library open while the pointer remains over it and collapse it when the pointer is outside. Subsequent hover over the chevron must reopen it; pointer departure must collapse an unpinned library after a short delay to avoid flicker.
  - Temporary navigation must overlay the Main View without changing its width, Graph geometry or camera. Chevron activation and keyboard search must provide access without hover. Keyboard focus inside navigation, active search, resizing, dragging and an owned dialog must prevent automatic dismissal during interaction. Explicit unpinning outside pointer hover, Escape and outside activation must still permit dismissal.
  - Navigation must retain width resizing by pointer and keyboard on wide screens. Pinned state and width must survive tab changes, Term selection, corpus refresh and browser reload when preference storage is available; unavailable storage must leave in-memory controls usable. With no stored preference, wide screens must default to pinned and narrow screens to unpinned and collapsed.
  - Narrow screens must present navigation as an overlay with explicit dismissal. Navigation and _Context_Pane_ must have independent controls. Hiding navigation must leave the main shell visible and occupying the released width; hiding the divider must not change shell placement.
  - The Graph tab must follow _Graph_View_ while retaining the common app shell and independent library navigation.
  - Term Declarations, Graph details, Knowledge Description and Scope must share one Markdown renderer. Support heading levels, paragraphs, emphasis, strikethrough, nested ordered and unordered lists, read-only task lists, blockquotes, horizontal rules, tables, inline code, indented code and backtick or tilde code fences. Each .relations declaration remains its own row.
  - Code fences must use the declared language for syntax highlighting, including common programming and configuration languages and Aterm. Unknown or missing languages must remain readable literal code. TypeScript sections must use the same code rendering. Code and diagram source must never create Term links, and Copy must preserve the original source text.
  - The trm and aterm fence labels must select the same Aterm source highlighting. Distinguish directives, qualified or short Term Kinds, Term spellings, Group clauses, section headers, Relation phrases, section selectors, daggers, delimiters and comments. Reuse the shared Term, Term Kind and Group grammar. Embedded code and comments must not be interpreted as surrounding Aterm notation; highlighting must not validate, execute or expand the sample.
  - Mermaid source must highlight diagram keywords, directions, arrows and cardinalities, labels, comments and configuration. Highlighting must remain available for invalid diagrams and use the same code renderer in every reading surface without loading the diagram implementation merely to color source. Highlight spans must preserve the source consumed by diagram rendering and Copy.
  - A mermaid fence must render a diagram asynchronously, with its source available for reading and copying. Invalid syntax or unavailable rendering must show a local error and retain the source without preventing other content or diagrams from rendering. Replacing a view must not receive an older diagram result; repeated reading must reuse a bounded rendering cache.
  - Markdown and diagram implementations must ship with the application and require no runtime CDN. Mermaid must load only when needed. Wide tables, code and diagrams must fit or scroll inside the reading surface on narrow screens. Diagrams must offer Fit width and Actual size so a narrow pane can show the whole diagram or retain readable text with scrolling.
  - Authored HTML must remain literal and never execute. Term links must retain the shared core grammar and the containing Knowledge; inline and fenced code and explicit external link labels must not become nested Term links. HTTP(S) links must open separately without opener access. Images may use HTTP(S) or embedded raster data, without sending a referrer; arbitrary filesystem paths must not become server endpoints.
  - Mermaid must use strict rendering with HTML labels and callbacks disabled, and its image presentation must isolate diagram styling from the app. A diagram must not override these site security settings. Resource-limit errors must retain the authored source like syntax errors.
  - Term selection, URL state and semantic Relation endpoints must use canonical qualified IDs. Graph Nodes, focus and expansion must use their distinct display identities under _Graph_View_. Equal local names from different Knowledges must remain distinct.
  - Term Declaration hyperlinks must resolve local tokens using that Term Declaration Knowledge, including content displayed from several Knowledges. Candidate results must offer qualified navigation instead of selecting an arbitrary Term.
  - Browser reference recognition must share the core Term grammar. Display local names with Knowledge context; preserve independent pane and navigation resizing.
  - A read-only Knowledge and Viewpoint browser must be reachable from navigation and contextual Knowledge and Viewpoint labels, independently of the Working Set and library filters. Its catalogs must include empty Knowledges and configured Viewpoints with no Term Declarations; search must match names, descriptions and source paths without case sensitivity.
  - Knowledge reading must show identity, source path, Term count, declared Viewpoints and Term links. Description and Scope must be separately labeled reader-facing and writer-facing Markdown; an absent block must be reported as absent without substituting the other block. Viewpoint and Knowledge readings must link to each other through declared usage.
  - Viewpoint reading must show its description, source path, Markdown guidance and ordered Term Kinds with section names, types, descriptions, questions, answer frames and examples. The original source must remain available as literal readable code. Unqualified example Terms in Viewpoint guidance must not acquire an arbitrary Knowledge namespace.
  - Opening, navigating and closing these readings must preserve the Working Set, main tab and Graph camera, positions and selection. Term links must open Context; Term drag must retain the shared add-to-Working-Set behavior. Browsing a Knowledge's Terms is an explicit library-filter action. Keyboard and narrow-screen users must have access to the catalogs and content, with dismissal returning focus to the opener. Corpus refresh must refresh open readings; unavailable or failed reads must be visible without an older response replacing a newer selection.
  - Term search must be the first control below the application menu in navigation. Knowledge, Viewpoint and Term Kind must form one visually grouped filter region below search. A searchable Knowledge chooser must list all declared Knowledges, including empty Knowledges, with IDs, reader-facing Descriptions when declared, source file paths and Term counts, and offer All Knowledge. Chooser search must match ID, Description and filename; Description must render as safe text. Scope must not substitute for Description.
  - The selected Knowledge must restrict library Terms and search; Viewpoint and Term Kind choices must derive from that Knowledge. Changing Knowledge must clear those two filters, retain the search phrase and current reading or graph session, and create a browser-history entry. All Knowledge must group matching Terms by Knowledge.
  - Knowledge selection must persist in the URL across all tabs and browser history. A missing Knowledge must remain explicit with recovery to All Knowledge; it must not silently broaden the selection.
  - Library scope must not hide cross-Knowledge Relations, References or graph neighbors. Context must show the read Term's Knowledge and offer an explicit action to browse there, without automatically changing library scope or membership.
  - Term cards and Context must distinguish local Term names from Knowledge. The shared header must offer copying of qualified Working Set identities and keep library scope and Knowledge selection reachable with navigation collapsed. The chooser must support keyboard navigation, contained focus, Escape dismissal and focus return.
  - Library Term Kind choices must use Viewpoint-qualified identities and distinguish overlapping local names. A short-name filter value must match the local name across owners; the Viewpoint filter must still apply to that same Term Declaration.
  - Term Declaration selection, search matches and Graph identity must use the resolved Term Kind owner, so changing only a Term Kind spelling must not lose selection or merge different Term Declarations.
  - Tree Term-name clicks, checkboxes and Term dragging must follow _Working_Set_ in every Main View. Library scope and filters must not remove members or restrict graph neighbors.
  - Tree Term rows must place local Term names and Term Kind labels on one line, with the Term Kind aligned to the right and truncated when needed. Hover over a Term Kind must reveal its full Viewpoint-qualified name; truncation must not discard that identity.
  - Term labels in navigation, Graph and reading surfaces must share a delayed Hover Preview showing the qualified Term name, a copy action and each Term Kind's Definition separately. Preview must use the current in-memory corpus and the shared Markdown renderer without a read request or changes to Working Set, Context, route or Graph state.
  - Pointer movement into the preview must keep it available for copying and reading. Departure must dismiss it after a short grace period; Escape, outside activation, scrolling the source, dragging, source removal and corpus refresh must dismiss or cancel it. Keyboard focus must offer the same preview and access to copying, with Escape returning focus to its source.
  - The preview must remain within the viewport and show a copy failure without claiming success. Copy must use the canonical qualified Term identity. Literal code and Term Kind-only hover must retain their existing behavior.
  - Reading a Term while Graph is active must keep the mounted Canvas, geometry and camera available; older read responses must not replace newer targets.
  - Term Declarations and References must have /term-declarations and /references routes. Graph modes must retain /graph/structure and /graph/explore. Repeated set query parameters must retain ordered Working Set membership, including an explicitly empty set; term must identify the independent Context target. Library filters must remain independent.
  - Existing /terms/<knowledge>/<local-name> and its /references route must open a singleton Working Set. Large collections must use browser history state instead of exceeding the UI location limit.
  - Direct navigation, refresh and browser history must support those routes. Existing hash links must migrate to the corresponding route without losing their selection and filters. Known UI routes must serve the packaged shell; API errors and missing assets must not become shell responses.
  - Route transitions must retain mounted shell/navigation state and retained Graph models. Reading a Term from Graph must preserve its geometry, camera and selection while updating the reading target and URL.
  - The shell must display the connected Session prefix and make its full UUID available for copying.
  - The common shell must provide the reader and cached history governed by _Markdown_Note_ without replacing the mounted Graph. All reading surfaces, including Notes, must use the same Markdown renderer and diagram lifecycle.
.remarks
  Verification: reads for an already selected canonical Term ID request case-sensitive matching, preserving browser identity when distinct Terms differ only by case. Free-text discovery retains its case-insensitive behavior.
  Terminology correction: Knowledge chooser, Scope labels and corpus summaries use Knowledge; file-only locations use source file or file. Structure and Explore retain the same catalog and interaction behavior.
  Correction: cancel an active Graph pointer gesture as soon as corpus refresh begins, before asynchronous reads; the delay before remounting must not allow an interrupted move to commit.
  Verification: the brand navigation retains its existing preferred Aterm Term under the migrated _Term_ identity; Knowledge discovery now presents the four consolidated Knowledges.
  Chosen: Graph behavior belongs to _Graph_View_; the shared shell stays here.
  Chosen: Dark Gold styling remains shared across the application.
  Chosen: Knowledge and Viewpoint reading uses one modal catalog-and-document surface, keeping the three Working Set tabs mounted. Knowledge Scope is visible alongside Description, while Term Kind schemas and original source can be expanded independently. Existing structured query operations supply the data; no raw-file endpoint is introduced.
  Chosen: library Term rows omit decorative glyphs so names have more horizontal space. Working Set checkboxes and textual Term Kind labels retain their existing roles for every Term.
  Chosen: Knowledge, Group and Term rows use compact membership checkboxes. Smaller navigation Term names preserve horizontal space; branch checkboxes retain a visible indeterminate state.
  Chosen: compact book and folder icons distinguish Knowledge and Group names in the Tree. Icons are part of the branch disclosure target; checkboxes retain their separate membership action.
  Chosen: Hover Preview opens after 450 ms and allows a 220 ms pointer crossing gap. It keeps temporary navigation open during preview interaction; Tab from its focused source reaches Copy.
  Chosen: Navigation preferences are browser-origin-local. Hover reveal uses a short entry delay and a slightly longer departure delay; pointer and keyboard access share the same library and state.
  Chosen: pinning is a binary preference; an unpinned library reveals on chevron hover. Previous Hidden and Auto-hide preferences migrate to unpinned, and Pinned migrates to pinned.
  Verification: browser checks cover chevron hover, pinning beyond pointer departure, unpinning inside and outside hover, keyboard activation and focus return, reload persistence, and unchanged Graph camera and Main View width during hover reveal.
  Chosen: Markdown uses a packaged standard parser with the Aterm token grammar taking precedence over underscore emphasis. Highlighting and diagram presentation follow the same Dark Gold reader styling.
  Chosen: packaged Mermaid and Aterm lexical grammars share the existing syntax color palette. Aterm samples remain literal code; Mermaid source coloring is independent of asynchronous diagram rendering.
  Source snippets continue to come from queries, never a file endpoint.
  Chosen: React Router Data Mode owns browser navigation; a persistent React shell hosts the existing reader, library and Graph DOM adapters. Browser WebSocket events replace periodic health polling; reconnect starts with a fresh health read.
  Chosen: CLI tree, library and Graph use one core Group-tree derivation. Browser packaging bundles the shared implementation into library-model.js; regression tests preserve Knowledge boundaries, implicit prefixes and Term Declaration-level membership.
  Verification: brand navigation follows the renamed Term identity; display and query behavior remain unchanged.
  Behavior preservation: refresh separates checked-corpus acceptance, Working Set reconciliation, Graph context reconciliation and visible readings. Refresh coalescing, error handling, selected-Term preservation and graph preparation order remain unchanged.
}


specification.procedure _Move_Terms_ in operations = {
  _Move_Terms_ relocates complete Terms into another existing Knowledge while preserving the identities of their referenced targets.
.relations
  uses _Edit_Term_Declarations_
  reads _External_Files_
  references _Viewpoint_
  references _Term_
  references _Reference_
.contract
  - `term move <selector...> --to <knowledge>` must accept exact Terms and quoted whole-name globs. Optional --knowledge selects the source Knowledge. Every selected Term must move with its single Term Declaration; repeated selectors must not duplicate a move.
  - The destination must be an existing discovered Knowledge. Missing selectors, empty selections, ambiguous exact names, unknown destinations and Terms already in the destination must fail before writing.
  - Distinct Terms must never merge, even across different Term Kinds. A destination name collision or two selected Terms mapping to the same destination name must fail.
  - The destination must declare each moved Term Kind under the same Viewpoint-qualified identity and compatible ordered section definitions. An identical Schema under another Viewpoint must not substitute for the original Term Kind. Missing or incompatible Term Kinds must fail; move must not choose a vocabulary or rewrite section meanings.
  - A move must preserve local declaration names, Term Kinds, Groups, section content, Relation phrases, section selectors and dagger annotations. An unqualified Term Kind opener must gain its existing Viewpoint qualifier when the destination makes the short name ambiguous; an authored qualifier must remain. It must append complete Term Declaration blocks in source-file and declaration order, using destination line endings. .trm file headers, comments outside Term Declarations and empty source Knowledges must remain in place.
  - Every scanned _Reference_ must retain its target identity after the move mapping. Incoming references must follow the moved Term. An originally local reference must stay local when its owner and target share the resulting Knowledge, and become qualified otherwise. An authored qualified reference must remain qualified.
  - References between simultaneously moved Terms and self-references must follow the same mapping. Opaque code, escaped tokens and ordinary strings in Knowledges must remain literal text.
  - Configured externalSources must participate by default; explicit --external selection must replace them. Only exact qualified Term tokens in external UTF-8 text must change; code identifier inference is excluded.
  - The complete source and candidate corpus must pass validation. A move must not create unresolved-reference draft warnings or proceed through an invalid source corpus.
  - Move must use _Edit_Term_Declarations_'s Home lock, source containment, symlink refusal, candidate validation, version checks and partial-save reporting for corpus and external files. --dry-run must return the complete validated diff without writes. Filesystem saving must not claim multi-file crash atomicity.
  - Automatic CLI dispatch of move, including dry-run, must execute directly against the filesystem; the HTTP query endpoint must refuse it. Explicit remote command execution uses the same filesystem authoring service on the server.
.remarks
  - Chosen: creating a destination Knowledge and selecting its vocabulary precede moving Terms. This makes the coverage boundary and schema change explicit.
  - Verification covers multi-Term Kind moves, simultaneous references, section expansion, local and qualified self-references, destination collisions, schema differences, external text and BOM/CRLF preservation.
}


concept _Graph_View_ in graph.views = {
  The read-only graph exploration view within _Explorer_.
.relations
  belongs to _Explorer_
  has_part _Graph_Toolbar_
  has_part _Graph_Canvas_
  uses _Context_Pane_
  projects _Working_Set_
  uses _Graph_State_
  uses _Graph_Containment_
  uses _Graph_Projection_
  uses _Graph_Layout_Policy_
  uses _Graph_Viewport_
.contract
  - _Graph_View_ must retain the Explorer's shared header and library when its tab is selected.
  - _Graph_View_ must overlay _Graph_Toolbar_ inside the Canvas region of the Main View, beneath the common tab row. The Explorer owns the shared Context and Note reader beside the Main View.
  - _Graph_View_ must keep graph exploration independent of the library's selected Knowledge.
  - _Graph_View_ must preserve _Graph_State_ when switching between Explorer tabs.
  - _Graph_View_ must expose no corpus editing controls.
  - _Graph_View_ must apply the same behavior to every Knowledge.
  - Offer Structure and Explore modes in the same view; a new session starts in Explore using the Working Set.
  - Structure presents the whole corpus containment independently of Working Set membership. Explore presents the Working Set Terms and their eligible Relations and corpus References without containers.
  - Switching modes must preserve each mode's Nodes, geometry, camera, Selection, filters, layout choice and Undo history independently.
  - Explore selected must start Explore from the canonical Terms represented by the visible Structure Selection. A selected Term contributes itself; a selected Knowledge, authored Group, derived Group or Hidden Group contributes all of its member Terms, including collapsed members. Repeated display occurrences must produce one Node per canonical Term.
  - Explore selected must replace Working Set membership with exactly those Terms, select them, arrange and fit the resulting graph, and preserve the Structure session. It must retain Explore's Relation filters and layout choice; a plain mode switch must not perform this transfer.
  - Explore selected must do nothing when Selection contains no Terms. An effective replacement must record one Explore Undo snapshot, including previous membership, Selection and geometry; repeating an unchanged replacement must not create history or rearrange Nodes.
  - Both modes must share edge rendering, assertion provenance, reading, Selection feedback and keyboard behavior.
.remarks
  Verification: category selection is shared by both projections and neighbor operations; browser checks cover Reference visibility, preserved geometry and camera, independent mode state, keyboard controls and a 390-pixel viewport.
  Verification: Hover markers, synchronized Incoming/Outgoing choices, filtered Select all, Box selection, single and group movement, Undo, gesture and corpus-refresh cancellation, keyboard movement and narrow-screen dialogs are exercised alongside the shared Structure tests.
  Verification: model tests cover canonical membership, one-hop filtering, assertion provenance, stable placement and Undo. Browser checks cover Tree inclusion, staged dialogs, mode and tab state, a 634-Term corpus and narrow-screen controls.
  Chosen: this Knowledge owns Graph behavior; the Explorer owns the shared shell.
  Chosen: Explore selected is an explicit, undoable replacement; ordinary mode switching resumes the previous graph. A selected container contributes its represented Terms without selecting or expanding its Structure descendants.
  Verification: model tests cover canonical transfer from Knowledge, authored, derived and Hidden Groups, no-op replacement and Undo. Browser checks exercise E and the toolbar action, importing all 11 Vending Machine Terms, preserving Structure, undoing to the prior Explore graph, and accessible pane controls at desktop and narrow widths.
  Chosen: has_part describes visible regions; model dependencies use ordinary Relations without assigning visual membership.
  Chosen: Dark Gold styling remains shared with the Explorer.
  Behavior preservation: Explore membership separates transaction ownership, neighbor choice and collision-free placement; Structure rendering separates descriptive content and controls; keyboard input separates popup handling, movement, activation and shortcuts. Differential tests preserve 160 membership transactions, 36 SVG card variants and 400 keyboard combinations.
}


concept _Graph_Toolbar_ in graph.views = {
  The controls for navigating and arranging the current _Graph_Projection_.
.relations
  presents _Graph_State_
  controls _Graph_Projection_
  references _Control_UI_
  invokes _Hide_Other_Nodes_
  invokes _Unhide_Nodes_
  invokes _Find_Term_
  invokes _Collapse_Containers_
  invokes _Focus_Graph_
  invokes _Undo_Graph_Change_
  invokes _Arrange_Graph_
  invokes _Fit_Graph_
  selects _Graph_Layout_Policy_
  references _Context_Pane_
.contract
  - _Graph_Toolbar_ must use compact controls consistent with the shared app toolbar.
  - _Graph_Toolbar_ must float over the top of the Canvas as compact buttons and control groups. Its container must have a transparent background and no bottom border, and must not consume a separate layout row.
  - Empty space between floating controls must allow Canvas pointer interaction. Controls and open popovers must receive their own pointer input.
  - _Graph_Toolbar_ must wrap control sets within the available width so Selection and visibility actions remain reachable on narrow screens.
  - _Graph_Toolbar_ must provide the mode selector, Find Term, Undo, Arrange and Fit. Context and Note controls belong to the common tab row. Structure additionally provides Collapse all and Explore selected.
  - Explore selected must be disabled when the visible Structure Selection represents no Terms; E must invoke it only in Structure when not typing.
  - _Graph_Toolbar_ must show the Structure focus path with actions for ancestors and All Knowledge.
  - _Graph_Toolbar_ must offer Layered, fCoSE and CiSE through its Layout selector.
  - _Graph_Toolbar_ must offer a checkbox multi-select through its Relations selector: Reference, one category per structural built-in, and Other relations. Categories and authored-name labels must come from the shared vocabulary used by _Graph_Projection_. It must show the selected category count or All or None, and offer Select all as a toggle.
  - _Graph_Toolbar_ must offer Structure Selected only and Selected + neighbors as the Keep mode for Hide others.
  - _Graph_Toolbar_ must provide Structure Hide others, Unhide selected, Show all and a Selection count.
  - _Graph_Toolbar_ must disable Hide others without visible selected Nodes, Unhide selected without selected hidden Nodes and Show all without hidden state.
  - _Graph_Toolbar_ must disable Undo when the active mode has no change history.
  - _Graph_Toolbar_ must expose its actions to keyboard users with visible focus.
  - _Graph_Toolbar_ must not invoke shortcuts while the user is typing into a control.
  - Explore must provide Remove from graph for selected Nodes, disabled without Selection. Removal must affect only those Nodes and their incident Edges, never recursively remove neighbors.
  - Explore must provide Clear, disabled when no Nodes are included. Clear must use the same undoable membership operation as the clear command under _Control_UI_.
  - Explore must provide Undo for membership changes and manual moves; Delete or Backspace invokes Remove from graph when not typing. Container and Hidden Group commands belong to Structure.
  - Explore must expose Select and Pan tools and show concise gesture guidance. Tool changes must cancel a pending gesture without committing it.
  - Relation category choices must apply immediately and keep the selector open for further choices. Escape must close it and return focus to its trigger; outside activation or focus leaving it must close it without moving focus. The selector must remain within the viewport and support keyboard checkboxes without invoking Graph shortcuts.
.remarks
  Correction: preserve the open Relation selector throughout pointer activation inside it, including checkbox label presses that temporarily focus an ancestor. Dismiss on outside pointer activation or keyboard focus leaving it; do not hide its content during native label activation. Chromium 143 regression covers label text, held presses, Select all, keyboard activation and Escape in both Graph modes.
  Correction: Relation checkbox activation updates its state immediately and coalesces Graph redraw into the next animation frame. Leaving the Graph or rebuilding its view cancels pending redraw.
  Chosen: shortcuts follow the visible actions: / Find Term, C Collapse all,
  Ctrl/Command Z Undo, A Arrange, F Fit and T detail-pane toggle.
  Chosen: H invokes Hide others with the selected mode; U invokes Unhide selected; Shift+H invokes Show all.
  The compact controls use the shared app toolbar styling.
}


concept _Graph_Canvas_ in graph.views = {
  The interactive drawing region presenting _Graph_Projection_ through _Graph_Viewport_.
.relations
  presents _Graph_Projection_
  uses _Graph_Viewport_
  displays _Graph_Node_
  displays _Compound_Graph_Node_
  displays _Graph_Edge_
  supports _Highlight_Connections_
.contract
  - _Graph_Canvas_ must display the visible nodes, edges and expanded frames of _Graph_Projection_.
  - _Graph_Canvas_ must expose pointer panning and zooming through _Graph_Viewport_.
  - _Graph_Canvas_ must expose arrow-key panning and plus/minus zooming when appropriate.
  - _Graph_Canvas_ must show when placement is running or has failed.
  - _Graph_Canvas_ must keep visible content available when placement fails.
  - _Graph_Canvas_ must remain usable when the library or detail region is collapsed.
  - An empty Explore Canvas must explain adding Terms from the library and offer Add current Term when a valid reading target exists.
  - Explore must use compact Term cards and briefly emphasize newly added Nodes; reduced motion must retain static feedback.
.remarks
  Chosen: Explore supports manual node positioning; user-created Groups and Seed pinning remain outside this change.
  Chosen: displayed Node types are model dependencies; they do not declare organizational membership.
}


concept _Context_Pane_ in operations = {
  The shared right reading region of the Explorer, with Context for a Term, Knowledge, Group or original Graph assertions and Note for cached Markdown explanations.
.relations
  shows _Term_Declaration_
  shows _Knowledge_
  shows _Graph_Edge_
  shows _Graph_Group_
  invokes _Read_Details_
  presents _Markdown_Note_
  changes _Working_Set_
.contract
  - _Context_Pane_ must start collapsed.
  - _Context_Pane_ must collapse independently of the library.
  - _Context_Pane_ must provide a keyboard-accessible close icon at its upper-right corner, remaining available while reading scrolls. Closing must return focus to the active Context or Note tab; reopening must retain the reading target. T must continue to toggle the pane when not typing.
  - _Context_Pane_ must support width resizing by pointer and keyboard, with one retained width shared by Context and Note. Narrow screens may stack the same reader below the Main View.
  - Context and Note must be independent of the active Main View. Reading a Term from a Note must activate Context while retaining the selected Note and cached history. Closing the reader must leave both reading targets available for reopening.
  - _Context_Pane_ must show every Term Declaration of a read Term in its own Schema order.
  - _Context_Pane_ must show Term Kind, Viewpoint, authored Group path, Knowledge, source location, sections and scope.
  - _Context_Pane_ must show each .relations declaration on its own row with a clickable target.
  - _Context_Pane_ must show the reader-facing Description first, followed by the source file path, available authoring Scope under a separate Scope heading, and member links when reading a Knowledge. Missing Description must not fall back to Scope.
  - _Context_Pane_ must show an authored Group's path or a derived Group's root, with member Term Kinds and Term links, without querying the Group as a corpus Term.
  - _Context_Pane_ must identify a Hidden Group as temporary visibility state and show its member links.
  - _Context_Pane_ must show all original endpoints, phrases, types and locations when reading an edge.
  - _Context_Pane_ must offer Add to Working Set for a Term. An existing member must be indicated without adding a duplicate. This action must preserve the active Main View and Structure visibility.
  - _Context_Pane_ must render safe Markdown and literal code under the Explorer's content rules.
.remarks
  Chosen: renamed from Graph Detail Pane because one common reader now serves all Main Views.
  Hyperlinks change Context; adding or dragging changes Working Set membership.
  Verification: browser checks cover Note-to-Context links, retained Note history, Knowledge reading, unchanged Graph camera and shared reader layouts at desktop and narrow widths.
}


concept _Graph_State_ in graph.state = {
  The current-session exploration state controlling _Graph_Projection_ and _Graph_Layout_Policy_.
.relations
  holds _Graph_Selection_
  projects _Working_Set_
  controls _Graph_Projection_
  selects _Graph_Layout_Policy_
.contract
  - _Graph_State_ must distinguish canonical Term IDs, path-local display IDs and Knowledge container IDs.
  - _Graph_State_ must hold Structure expansion, focus, node selection, hidden subtree roots, Relation visibility and the selected layout.
  - _Graph_State_ must start Structure with no hidden Nodes or expanded containers and All Knowledge as its focus.
  - _Graph_State_ must start with every Relation category selected and Layered layout.
  - _Graph_State_ must retain expansion, focus, selection, hiding and layout selection across tab changes within the session.
  - _Graph_State_ must keep structural history separate from layout, camera, filter and reading changes.
  - _Graph_State_ must retain at most thirty change snapshots per mode from the current corpus revision.
  - Explore must project the Working Set's canonical Term IDs into Nodes, independently of selected Node IDs and the Context target. It must not own a second membership set.
  - Explore history must record ordered Working Set membership, Selection and Node geometry before an effective membership change, replacement or manual move. Undo must update membership in every Main View and the Tree. No-op changes must not create history.
  - Explore additions, removals and Undo must preserve the camera. Modes and tab changes must not discard either mode's state.
.remarks
  Correction: the active operation declares the effect; redundant changed by declarations have been removed.
  Chosen: focus is a graph boundary, not the library's Knowledge filter.
  Chosen: focus, expansion and layout selection are session state, not saved graph sessions.
  A Structure Undo snapshot holds expansion, focus, Selection and hidden roots; an Explore snapshot holds membership, Selection and geometry.
}


concept _Graph_Containment_ in graph.rendering.structure = {
  The hierarchy of Knowledge containers, authored Groups and path-local Term Declaration occurrences used for rendering.
.relations
  derives from _Group_
  derives from _Relation_
  organizes _Term_Declaration_
  organizes _Graph_Group_
.contract
  - _Graph_Containment_ must first place every Term Declaration at its authored Group path or directly under Knowledge when omitted.
  - _Graph_Containment_ must retain that authored occurrence even when another Term Declaration relates to it as a part.
  - _Graph_Containment_ must derive part Groups only from authored non-self parthood Relations within one Knowledge.
  - _Graph_Containment_ must determine the source Term Declaration of each assertion from its source location.
  - _Graph_Containment_ must represent a Term Declaration with eligible parts by a derived Group unless that Term Declaration repeats an ancestor.
  - _Graph_Containment_ must include the root Term Declaration leaf and its direct part Term Declaration occurrences inside each derived Group.
  - _Graph_Containment_ must create a separate display occurrence for each shared part's containing Term Declaration path.
  - _Graph_Containment_ must stop at a repeated ancestor without deleting its Term Declaration or Relation.
  - _Graph_Containment_ must order authored subgroups by path and Term Declarations by canonical Term then Term Kind.
  - _Graph_Containment_ must not derive part Groups from Membership, State membership, Generalization, ordinary phrases, Term Kind or Viewpoint.
.remarks
  Chosen: every Term Declaration has an authored location; parthood no longer suppresses that location.
  Chosen: Knowledge remains the boundary for deriving part Groups; cross-Knowledge parthood stays an Edge.
  A derived display occurrence does not move a Term Declaration into another authored Group.
  Correction: a Knowledge root has no Group segments; path and breadcrumb checks exclude a synthetic empty Group.
}


concept _Graph_Projection_ in graph.rendering.structure = {
  The visible graph obtained from the corpus under _Graph_State_, using _Graph_Containment_ in Structure mode.
.relations
  uses _Graph_Containment_
  uses _Graph_State_
  represents _Hidden_Node_Group_
  represents _Graph_Group_
  contains _Graph_Node_
  contains _Graph_Edge_
  selects endpoints for _Graph_Connection_Point_
.contract
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must include every declared Knowledge as a collapsed card initially, including empty Knowledges.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must restrict its nodes to the current focus subtree when focus is set.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must give each path-local Term Declaration occurrence a separate display identity while retaining its canonical Term identity.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must apply the hidden subtree state through temporary Hidden Groups while preserving original containment.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must represent collapsed members through their nearest visible collapsed Knowledge or Group.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must expose the root Term Declaration as a child Node when its derived Group is expanded.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must keep expanded Group headers separate from semantic Relation endpoints.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must represent each Term through its single Term Declaration; authored and derived Group paths may produce separate display occurrences.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must project each assertion only from occurrences of its owning source Term Declarations, retaining Term-based targets.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must connect mutually nearest visible source and target occurrences by containment-tree distance for each assertion.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must break equal-distance counterpart ties by display identity.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must omit connections with endpoints outside current graph focus.
  - _Graph_Projection_ must preserve all original assertion endpoints and locations.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must draw expanded parthood as edges; enclosure alone does not express that Relation.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must not turn internal connections of collapsed containers into container self-loops.
  - _Graph_Projection_ must aggregate assertions by visible source and target under the same rule for individual Term Declarations and collapsed containers.
  - _Graph_Projection_ must preserve the phrase, type, origin and locations of every assertion in that directed Edge.
  - _Graph_Projection_ must keep the opposite direction as a separate Edge without inferring inverse meaning from phrases.
  - _Graph_Projection_ must show all Relation categories and available corpus References by default, subject to Hidden Group boundary visibility.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must mark each Edge crossing a Hidden Group boundary with that Group identity, including when the Group is expanded.
  - In Structure, _Graph_Projection_ must show internal Edges normally when their endpoints become visible inside the same Hidden Group.
  - _Graph_Projection_ must classify connections into Reference, each built-in structural Relation, or Other relations using server-provided type and origin metadata and the shared built-in vocabulary. Reference includes authored references and raw corpus References; Other relations includes all remaining authored assertions.
  - _Graph_Projection_ must filter assertions before bundling Edges. Changing selected categories must preserve Nodes, Group membership, expansion, Selection, geometry, camera and Undo history in both modes; it must not automatically arrange the graph.
  - _Graph_Projection_ must never create or modify a corpus assertion.
  - _Graph_Projection_ must compute unshown connection counts from the same category-filtered assertions used for Edge projection, at each eligible visible source and target occurrence. An assertion remains unshown for an occurrence unless a normally visible incident Edge represents it in that direction.
  - Counts must include absent counterparts, focus exclusions, unmatched repeated occurrences, internal connections omitted from collapsed containers, and concealed Hidden Group boundary connections. Bundled assertions remain individually counted. Hover previews must not change these persistent counts.
  - Expanded container headers must not duplicate their descendants' connection counts; only their semantic endpoint representatives receive counts. Inclusion, hiding, expansion, focus, filters and refresh must recompute them without changing graph state.
  - Explore must contain one flat Node per included canonical Term, retaining its Term Kind and source provenance. Equal local names in different Knowledges must remain distinct.
  - Explore must draw all eligible Relations and corpus References whose two Term endpoints are included, including connections between separately explored branches and self-relations.
  - Explore must retain structural Relations as Edges without deriving enclosure, opening ancestors or adding any other Terms.
  - Explore exclusion must not create Hidden Groups; a removed Node becomes available for explicit inclusion again.
.remarks
  A Relation is an authored assertion; an Edge is its current visible representation.
  Chosen: nearest-counterpart projection avoids a Cartesian product of shared-part occurrences.
  Verification: unshown counts cover missing endpoints, bundled assertions, self-relations, filtered categories, focus, repeated occurrences and collapsed or Hidden Groups. Browser counts match corpus assertions and change with category selection.
  Chosen: Graph consumes the existing Relations result; an authored Relation still suppresses a raw Reference for the same ordered Term pair before category filtering. External source snippets remain Explorer reading features.
}


concept _Graph_Node_ in graph.rendering.nodes = {
  The visual representative of one Knowledge, one _Graph_Group_ or one occurrence of a _Term_Declaration_ or one canonical Term in Explore, with its own identity and outer bounds.
.relations
  represents _Term_Declaration_
  represents _Knowledge_
  represents _Graph_Group_
  has_part _Graph_Node_Header_
  has_part _Graph_Port_
  uses _Graph_Containment_
.contract
  - A Knowledge card must summarize its reader-facing Description and retain it in the tooltip. Missing Description must not fall back to authoring Scope; only a genuinely empty Knowledge may use the empty-Knowledge message.
  - _Graph_Node_ must keep equal local Term names from different Knowledges distinct.
  - _Graph_Node_ must distinguish display identity from Term Declaration and Term identities.
  - _Graph_Node_ must retain the Term Kind of its Term's single Term Declaration in every display instance.
  - _Graph_Node_ must retain the same display identity across compact and expanded presentation.
  - _Graph_Node_ must keep a derived Group and its root Term Declaration child as separate Nodes.
  - _Graph_Node_ must offer container expansion only for Structure Knowledge and Group containers.
  - _Graph_Node_ must have one _Graph_Node_Header_ while visible.
  - _Graph_Node_ must have one source _Graph_Port_ and one target _Graph_Port_.
  - _Graph_Node_ must have outer bounds covering its complete current presentation.
  - _Graph_Node_ must preserve display identity when those bounds change.
  - Both modes must show a compact +N when a Node has eligible connections not represented by normally visible incident Edges. The tooltip and accessible description must report Incoming and Outgoing counts; zero must omit the indicator.
  - N must count distinct unshown assertions, not neighboring Terms or bundled Edge labels. An assertion incident in both directions counts once in N and once in each direction. Relation categories excluded by the selector must not contribute.
  - _Graph_Node_ must derive Structure child membership from _Graph_Containment_, independently of expansion state.
  - Explore cards must show Viewpoint-qualified Term Kinds above the local Term name, without repeating Knowledge as a visible heading. The Knowledge-qualified identity and every untruncated Term Kind must remain accessible in the tooltip.
  - Explore must reveal Add neighbors, Move and Remove from graph markers on Hover or keyboard focus, independently of Selection and Read. Touch users must have reachable controls without Hover.
  - One neighbor dialog must present Incoming on the left and Outgoing on the right in equally sized columns, stacking on narrow screens. It must offer shared name search and Relation phrase, Viewpoint and Term Kind filters; Viewpoint and Term Kind must match the same Term Declaration. Incoming rows must preserve the authored source-to-target direction and phrase.
  - The dialog must show current inclusion through checkboxes and apply staged changes in one Undo step. Changing its filters must preserve staged choices; Cancel or Escape must discard them.
  - Expansion must consider only direct neighbors allowed by the current Relation category selection, including corpus References, across Knowledges and independently of library filters. Applying inclusion must never recursively expand new Nodes.
  - The dialog must show total neighbors and how many are already included, an explicit empty result, bounded scrolling, keyboard focus containment and focus return. A refresh must discard stale dialog choices.
  - Both columns must share staged inclusion by canonical Term identity; a Term present in both must stay synchronized. Counts denote distinct Terms, not assertions.
  - Select all must toggle the union of currently matching Terms; each column must offer the same toggle for its own matches. If all eligible checkboxes are checked, the next activation unchecks them. Empty matches disable the action. Filtered-out choices remain unchanged.
  - Remove from graph on a Node must remove only that Node and its incident Edges, preserve other Selection and positions, and be undoable without editing the corpus.
  - A Move handle must move its Node alone when unselected, or the whole current Selection when the Node is selected. Dragging a selected card must use the same movement behavior.
.remarks
  Chosen: Node denotes the whole visual object; a compact card and an expanded frame are presentations of that object.
  Position, size and expansion are properties, not new Node identities.
  Chosen: an empty Knowledge remains a readable node without expansion.
  Chosen: Explore's neighbor and Add Terms dialogs share the footer action order Select all matching, Cancel, Apply. The existing selection toggle, per-column controls and staged changes keep their behavior; the shared action remains reachable below the scrolling choices.
}


concept _Compound_Graph_Node_ in graph.rendering.nodes = {
  A _Graph_Node_ with direct children in _Graph_Containment_, whether those children are currently visible or collapsed.
.relations
  is_a _Graph_Node_†
  has_part _Graph_Node_Body_
  uses _Graph_Containment_
  represents _Graph_Group_
  references _Graph_Node_Header_
.contract
  - _Compound_Graph_Node_ must have at least one direct child in the current containment structure.
  - _Compound_Graph_Node_ must retain the same display identity when its child graph opens or closes.
  - _Compound_Graph_Node_ must retain its child relationships while collapsed.
  - _Compound_Graph_Node_ must enclose its visible children within its outer bounds when expanded.
  - _Compound_Graph_Node_ must include its _Graph_Node_Header_ and _Graph_Node_Body_ in those outer bounds.
  - _Compound_Graph_Node_ must permit children that are themselves compound nodes.
  - _Compound_Graph_Node_ must use the same attachment policy as a node without children.
.remarks
  Chosen: Compound Node is a visual subtype, not an Aterm Term Kind or a new corpus assertion.
  Knowledge containers and derived Groups follow the same rule when they have children.
  A derived Group includes its root Term Declaration as a separate child Node; an authored Group includes its assigned Term Declarations and subgroups.
  A closed compound still has children; an empty Knowledge does not qualify.
  The frame and collapsed card represent the same container, independently of its contained Term Nodes.
}


concept _Graph_Node_Header_ in graph.rendering.nodes = {
  The persistent identity, summary and control region of a _Graph_Node_.
.relations
  identifies _Graph_Node_
  identifies _Graph_Group_
  changes _Graph_Selection_
  invokes _Expand_Container_
  invokes _Collapse_Containers_
  invokes _Focus_Graph_
  invokes _Read_Details_
.contract
  - _Graph_Node_Header_ must remain visible while its owning node is visible, in both collapsed and expanded states.
  - _Graph_Node_Header_ must show Term identity, Term Kind and Viewpoint for every represented Term Declaration; Explore shows the Viewpoint-qualified Term Kind above the name in one compact canonical Term card without a duplicate lower classification row.
  - _Graph_Node_Header_ must show Knowledge identity for a Knowledge node.
  - Structure and Explore Term Nodes must share compact styling and information order: Viewpoint-qualified Term Kind, Term name, a bounded Definition preview and Read. Explore must preview the represented Term's Definition; the shared Hover Preview and Read must retain its complete content.
  - _Graph_Node_Header_ must show the reader-facing Description for a collapsed Knowledge node, consistently with _Graph_Node_.
  - _Graph_Node_Header_ must label an authored Group by its path segment and a derived Group by its root Term, with an explicit group suffix in both states.
  - _Graph_Node_Header_ must identify the root Term Kind on a derived Group.
  - _Graph_Node_Header_ must distinguish Group metadata from Term Kind and Viewpoint.
  - _Graph_Node_Header_ must select its Node by click, Enter or Space without expanding or reading it.
  - _Graph_Node_Header_ must expose a separate Chevron to toggle expansion by click, Enter or Space when its Node has direct children.
  - _Graph_Node_Header_ must label a Hidden Group with its hidden direct-member count.
  - _Graph_Node_Header_ must not offer expansion when its node has no direct children.
  - _Graph_Node_Header_ must offer an explicit Read action in both collapsed and expanded states.
  - _Graph_Node_Header_ must offer Focus when its original containment node is expanded; a temporary Hidden Group has no Focus action.
.remarks
  Chosen: Header includes the compact summary card when closed; it remains above the child graph when open.
  Chosen: both modes share Term title size, summary rendering, accent colors and card insets. Explore retains its move, neighbor and remove markers above a two-line Definition preview; its layout bounds include the preview and footer controls.
  Term Kind and Viewpoint classify the represented Term; they do not determine child membership.
  Term leaves always show Term Kind and Viewpoint; Group headers identify the collection instead.
  Correction: Knowledge summaries follow Description rather than authoring Scope; an empty Knowledge still has a readable card.
}


concept _Graph_Node_Body_ in graph.rendering.nodes = {
  The expandable child-graph region below the Header of a _Compound_Graph_Node_.
.relations
  belongs to _Compound_Graph_Node_
  contains _Graph_Node_
  presents _Graph_Projection_
  shares _Graph_Viewport_
  references _Graph_Node_Header_
.contract
  - _Graph_Node_Body_ must belong to exactly one compound node.
  - _Graph_Node_Body_ must display that node's visible direct children when expanded.
  - _Graph_Node_Body_ must include the full visible bounds of each child, including an expanded child's own Body.
  - _Graph_Node_Body_ must keep child nodes below _Graph_Node_Header_ and inside its owner's outer bounds.
  - _Graph_Node_Body_ must have no visible child content while its owner is collapsed.
  - _Graph_Node_Body_ must use the shared _Graph_Viewport_ rather than an independent child camera.
  - _Graph_Node_Body_ must not clip an edge merely because it crosses the compound boundary.
.remarks
  Chosen: Body denotes child-graph content; the collapsed definition preview belongs to the Header.
  Expanding reveals existing children. It does not create Terms or change their authored Relations.
  Child spacing and exact Header height remain layout details.
}


concept _Graph_Port_ in graph.rendering.nodes = {
  A node-owned attachment role through which visible edges leave or enter a _Graph_Node_.
.relations
  belongs to _Graph_Node_
  has_property _Graph_Anchor_
  accepts _Graph_Connection_Point_
.contract
  - _Graph_Port_ must belong to exactly one node.
  - _Graph_Port_ must have either the source role for outgoing edges or the target role for incoming edges.
  - _Graph_Port_ must be identified by its owning node and role independently of current coordinates.
  - _Graph_Port_ must retain its role when its owning node expands, collapses or moves.
  - _Graph_Port_ must permit multiple edge ends of the same role to share the attachment.
  - _Graph_Port_ must offer the boundary candidates defined by _Graph_Anchor_ for each incident edge end.
  - _Graph_Port_ must allow different incident edges to select different candidates without creating new Ports.
.remarks
  Chosen: one source and one target attachment per Node describe the current renderer.
  Chosen: has_property records the Anchor value role; no extra membership is asserted to control rendering.
  Example: three outgoing edges use one source Port and may choose three different Anchors.
  Chosen: Port identity denotes the connection role, not one fixed coordinate.
  These are model identities; no separate runtime object or visible Port marker is required.
  Non-goal: user-created Ports, Port dragging and drag-to-connect interaction.
}


concept _Graph_Anchor_ in graph.rendering.nodes = {
  A candidate geometric reference point for a _Graph_Port_ on the boundary of its owning _Graph_Node_.
.relations
  locates _Graph_Port_
  uses _Graph_Node_
.contract
  - _Graph_Anchor_ must be one of twelve candidates on the current outer bounds of the visible node.
  - _Graph_Anchor_ must use the 25%, 50% or 75% position on the top, right, bottom or left side.
  - _Graph_Anchor_ must be identified by node identity, boundary side and position fraction independently of its coordinates.
  - _Graph_Anchor_ must be available to both source and target Port roles.
  - _Graph_Anchor_ must use the full expanded bounds for a compound node, not only its Header bounds.
  - _Graph_Anchor_ must reflect the latest node positions and sizes after layout or expansion changes.
  - _Graph_Anchor_ must use the same candidate policy for every layout selection and containment depth.
  - _Graph_Anchor_ must keep graph-space coordinates independent of camera pan and zoom.
.remarks
  Chosen: twelve boundary candidates replace the fixed source-bottom/target-top policy.
  A Port retains its role; each Edge selects one candidate for its own connection end.
  An Anchor is not a Bézier control point; control points determine curve shape.
}


concept _Graph_Connection_Point_ in graph.rendering.edges = {
  One actual source or target endpoint of a visible _Graph_Edge_ curve, attached to a _Graph_Port_.
.relations
  belongs to _Graph_Edge_
  attaches to _Graph_Port_
  uses _Graph_Anchor_
  follows _Graph_Projection_
.contract
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must belong to exactly one end of one visible edge.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must attach to the corresponding role of the visible endpoint's Port.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must use the representative selected by _Graph_Projection_ when an original endpoint is hidden.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must coincide with one selected candidate _Graph_Anchor_ of its Port.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must be selected jointly with the other end of its edge from every candidate pair.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must exclude an identical source/target Anchor pair for a self-edge.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must prefer fewer penetrated solid cards and expanded Headers before all other costs.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must then prefer less total penetration length before all remaining costs.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must finally minimize curve length plus 0.75 times excess over chord length plus 48 times prior uses of its two Anchors.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must measure approximate length and penetration along sixteen equal-parameter segments of the candidate curve.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must treat an expanded Body as traversable while protecting its Header.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must use canonical visible endpoint and assertion ordering for deterministic greedy selection across edges.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must break equal scores by top, right, bottom, left side order and 50%, 25%, 75% position order.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must keep selection unchanged during pan, zoom or reading when projection and node geometry are unchanged.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must reflect the current projection and node geometry.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must leave the edge's original assertion endpoints unchanged.
  - _Graph_Connection_Point_ must not create extra attachments when its curve crosses a compound boundary.
.remarks
  Chosen: candidate-pair evaluation is a deterministic greedy heuristic, not a global graph optimum.
  Chosen: shared-anchor usage counts source and target ends together at the same physical boundary location.
  Chosen: boundary touches are not penetration; scoring uses a small numerical tolerance.
  Example: a hidden child's edge may end on its collapsed ancestor's Port; detail still names the child.
  Several edge-local Connection Points can coincide at one Node-owned Port.
  Known limit: direct candidate curves cannot guarantee no card penetration or edge crossings in every layout.
  No intermediate routing waypoints or boundary clipping are introduced.
}


concept _Graph_Edge_ in graph.rendering.edges = {
  A directed visible connection retaining one or more original _Relation_ assertions or _Reference_ connections.
.relations
  represents _Relation_
  represents _Reference_
  connects _Graph_Node_
  connects _Graph_Port_
  has_part _Graph_Connection_Point_
  references _Graph_Anchor_
  invokes _Read_Details_
.contract
  - _Graph_Edge_ must retain every original source, target, phrase, type, origin and source location.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must have one source and one target _Graph_Connection_Point_.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must draw a direct cubic Bézier curve between those Connection Points.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must use the selected boundary normals for its curve tangents.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must reverse a boundary tangent toward the Body when its opposite Connection Point lies inside that compound node.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must attach its source end to the source _Graph_Port_ of its visible source node.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must attach its target end to the target _Graph_Port_ of its visible target node.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must use the same connection rule across scopes and layout choices.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must not use obstacle routing or shared orthogonal trunks.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must display at most two distinct phrases of a visible Edge on separate lines.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must append a +N line when additional distinct phrases are omitted, with N counting those omitted phrases.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must order distinct phrases by case-sensitive code-unit order before choosing the displayed two.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must retain the complete phrase list in its accessible name and tooltip, and every original assertion in detail.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must append ×N only when more than one represented assertion shares that phrase.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must not add a redundant Relation heading to its label.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must place labels along its actual curve and above cards.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must avoid card and label overlap where space permits.
  - _Graph_Edge_ must expose its original assertions through line or label activation and keyboard activation, including when routes overlap.
.remarks
  Correction: derive Label candidates from the same cubic geometry as the rendered path and measure text before publishing the SVG. Redraw must not alternate replacing live SVG with synchronous native path and text geometry queries.
  Example: uses ×2 names two represented uses assertions; it is not a source-location count.
  Example: four distinct phrases produce two phrase lines followed by +2, even if one phrase represents several assertions.
  A visually straight edge can still be a cubic curve when its endpoints are collinear.
  Candidate placement belongs to _Graph_Anchor_; pair selection belongs to _Graph_Connection_Point_.
  Chosen: tangent reach is 35% of the endpoint distance, clamped to 24–96 graph units.
  Chosen: geometry and label placement share one renderer module independently of the node-layout engine.
  Candidate scoring reduces card penetration and shared attachments without adding obstacle routing.
}


concept _Graph_Layout_Policy_ in graph.layout = {
  The selected node-placement policy for _Graph_Projection_, independent of edge drawing and _Graph_Viewport_.
.relations
  positions _Graph_Projection_
  preserves _Graph_Containment_
  references _Graph_Viewport_
  used by _Arrange_Graph_
.contract
  - _Graph_Layout_Policy_ must offer Layered, fCoSE and CiSE.
  - _Graph_Layout_Policy_ must use Layered as the initial selection.
  - _Graph_Layout_Policy_ must preserve canonical identities and containment under every policy.
  - _Graph_Layout_Policy_ must leave edge drawing to the same connection rule for every policy.
  - _Graph_Layout_Policy_ must place each structural scope independently using layered leaf placement and component packing for Layered.
  - _Graph_Layout_Policy_ must include cross-scope connections when fCoSE places the whole visible compound graph.
  - _Graph_Layout_Policy_ must apply CiSE circular placement to each scope's direct children from inner scopes outward.
  - _Graph_Layout_Policy_ must project descendant connections onto the corresponding direct children for CiSE.
  - _Graph_Layout_Policy_ must keep batch layout calculations outside the UI thread using packaged assets.
  - _Graph_Layout_Policy_ must not treat manual node positions as persistent constraints.
  - Explore additions must preserve existing Node positions and camera; place new neighbors near the initiating Node, preferring Incoming on its left and Outgoing on its right, with overlap avoidance.
  - Tree additions must use the visible Canvas or included neighbors as their placement context without moving existing Nodes.
  - Explore full arrangement occurs only on explicit Arrange or layout choice. Layered Explore uses left-to-right flow; all available engines retain flat membership.
  - Explore manual movement must translate all selected Nodes by the same graph-space delta and update connected Edges while dragging; unselected Nodes, membership, reading and camera must remain unchanged.
  - Manual movement must be one Undo step per completed effective drag. A canceled or zero-displacement gesture must not create history. Escape, pointer cancellation, leaving the view or corpus refresh must restore the gesture start state.
  - Manual movement must cancel pending automatic placement so stale results cannot overwrite it. Explicit Arrange may replace manual positions; later membership changes must preserve them.
  - A focused Move handle must support arrow-key movement, with a larger step while Shift is held; the same Selection and Undo rules apply.
.remarks
  Chosen: combined neighbor expansion places incoming-only Terms to the left and outgoing or bidirectional Terms to the right. Existing Node positions remain unchanged.
  Chosen: Layered uses ELK; cross-scope behavior does not reorder its independently placed containers.
  Structure Layered retains top-to-bottom placement; force layouts do not impose flow direction.
  Chosen: fCoSE uses native compound structure and symmetric padding that reserves frame-header space.
  Chosen: CiSE accepts flat clusters; recursive application retains the rendering containment.
  These are placement policy values, not new Term Kinds or inferred memberships.
}


concept _Graph_Viewport_ in graph.views = {
  The camera transform selecting the visible portion and scale of _Graph_Canvas_.
.relations
  frames _Graph_Canvas_
.contract
  - _Graph_Viewport_ must hold pan offset and zoom independently of node positions.
  - _Graph_Viewport_ must support pointer panning and zoom controls.
  - Wheel zoom must retain the graph-space point under the pointer. Zoom buttons and plus/minus shortcuts must zoom around the Canvas center. All zoom inputs must share the same bounded camera transform in Structure and Explore.
  - _Graph_Viewport_ must support arrow-key panning and plus/minus zooming from the canvas.
  - _Graph_Viewport_ must preserve graph topology and node positions when panned or zoomed.
  - _Graph_Viewport_ must not start ordinary canvas panning from an interactive card, control or edge label. In Explore, explicit Pan mode, Space-drag or middle-button drag may pan from a card or Edge; action markers remain controls.
  - Explore must distinguish Select and Pan tools. Blank drag selects in Select and pans in Pan; Space-drag and middle-button drag temporarily pan without changing the chosen tool or Selection.
  - A pan gesture must not trigger Node activation on release. Zoom and camera commands must not change coordinate mapping during an active selection or movement gesture.
.remarks
  Correction: the active operation declares the effect; redundant changed by declarations have been removed.
  Correction: restore the shared zoom method removed during the shell refactor. Wheel, button and keyboard handlers must all reach it; controller regression checks cover both modes, pointer anchoring, centered zoom and scale limits.
  Layout determines where nodes are; Viewport determines which portion is on screen.
  Manual node positions are not persistent layout constraints.
}


specification.procedure _Expand_Container_ in graph.operations = {
  The operation exposing one container's direct children in _Graph_Projection_.
.relations
  changes _Graph_State_
  changes _Graph_Node_Body_
  preserves _Graph_Node_
  exposes _Graph_Group_
  exposes _Graph_Projection_
  invokes _Arrange_Graph_
.contract
  - _Expand_Container_ must accept a visible Knowledge or Group with direct children.
  - _Expand_Container_ must reveal the child graph in the owning node's _Graph_Node_Body_.
  - _Expand_Container_ must preserve the owning _Graph_Node_ identity and child membership.
  - _Expand_Container_ must expose an authored Group's assigned Term Declarations and immediate subgroups without opening previously closed deeper Groups.
  - _Expand_Container_ must expose a derived Group's root Term Declaration and direct part occurrences without opening previously closed deeper Groups.
  - _Expand_Container_ must open a Hidden Group for inspecting and selecting its members without unhiding them.
  - _Expand_Container_ must preserve Selection and hidden membership.
  - _Expand_Container_ must preserve remembered expansion of deeper structures.
  - _Expand_Container_ must focus the graph on a Knowledge when opening its card.
  - _Expand_Container_ must preserve library scope.
  - _Expand_Container_ must record the prior structural state for Undo.
  - _Expand_Container_ must arrange and fit the resulting visible graph.
.remarks
  A Term leaf offers Read; expanding belongs to its containing Group.
  Correction: opening Knowledge recomputes visible nodes after setting focus, so the same click removes unrelated Knowledge cards.
}


specification.procedure _Collapse_Containers_ in graph.operations = {
  The operation closing one container, or returning the whole graph to its collapsed overview.
.relations
  changes _Graph_State_
  changes _Graph_Node_Body_
  preserves _Graph_Node_
  preserves _Graph_Group_
  updates _Graph_Projection_
  invokes _Arrange_Graph_
.contract
  - _Collapse_Containers_ must hide one container's descendants by closing its _Graph_Node_Body_.
  - _Collapse_Containers_ must preserve that _Graph_Node_ identity and child membership when closing it.
  - _Collapse_Containers_ must leave the collapsed Group visible without replacing it with a member Term Declaration leaf.
  - _Collapse_Containers_ must retain deeper expansion state when closing one container.
  - _Collapse_Containers_ must clear expansion, focus, node selection and hiding for Collapse all.
  - _Collapse_Containers_ must retain layout selection, Relation visibility and library scope.
  - _Collapse_Containers_ must record the prior structural state for Undo.
  - _Collapse_Containers_ must arrange and fit the resulting visible graph.
.remarks
  Closing one container preserves its descendants' remembered state; Collapse all resets it.
}


specification.procedure _Focus_Graph_ in graph.operations = {
  The operation choosing the subtree shown by _Graph_Projection_ without changing library scope.
.relations
  changes _Graph_State_
  bounds _Graph_Projection_
  invokes _Arrange_Graph_
.contract
  - _Focus_Graph_ must accept an expanded original containment container, an ancestor breadcrumb or All Knowledge.
  - _Focus_Graph_ must expose a hidden target and its ancestor path without unhiding unrelated branches.
  - _Focus_Graph_ must preserve expansion when moving to a subtree or an ancestor.
  - _Focus_Graph_ must expose the whole-Knowledge overview boundary when All Knowledge is selected.
  - _Focus_Graph_ must preserve the library's selected Knowledge and current reading.
  - _Focus_Graph_ must record the prior structural state for Undo.
  - _Focus_Graph_ must arrange and fit the resulting visible graph.
.remarks
  Focus restricts graph visibility. It neither edits membership nor selects a library Knowledge.
}


specification.procedure _Find_Term_ in graph.operations = {
  The operation finding a canonical Term through the graph's Find Term dialog.
.relations
  finds _Term_
  invokes _Reveal_Term_
.contract
  - _Find_Term_ must search canonical names across the corpus independently of library scope.
  - _Find_Term_ must match the search text as a case-insensitive substring of the canonical qualified name.
  - _Find_Term_ must offer Term Kind and Viewpoint filters that match the same Term Declaration. Term Kind options must use qualified identities, so overlapping local names remain separately selectable; short-name filter values must match that local name across owners.
  - _Find_Term_ must list Structure matching Term Declarations with Term, Term Kind and authored Group context so Terms with equal local names in different Knowledges remain distinguishable.
  - _Find_Term_ must pass the Structure selected Term Declaration identity to Reveal and honor the active Term Kind filter.
  - _Find_Term_ must show an explicit no-match state when no Term matches.
  - _Find_Term_ must invoke _Reveal_Term_ when the user selects a Structure result; Explore must present matching canonical Terms with inclusion checkboxes.
  - _Find_Term_ must contain keyboard focus while its dialog is open.
  - _Find_Term_ must dismiss on Escape and return focus to the canvas.
  - Explore Find must use the same staged inclusion and cancellation rules as its neighbor dialog; a Term matches when one Term Declaration satisfies both Term Kind and Viewpoint.
  - Explore Find must use the same Select all toggle over current matches as the neighbor dialog.
.remarks
  Chosen: name matching is case-insensitive substring matching, as the current dialog implements.
  This dialog searches graph identities; the library's body search remains a separate feature.
  Chosen: Find identifies Terms by Knowledge and authored Group context; repeated display locations do not create another Term Declaration.
}


specification.procedure _Reveal_Term_ in graph.operations = {
  The operation making a known Term visible by opening its ancestor path.
.relations
  reveals _Term_
  uses _Graph_Containment_
  changes _Graph_State_
  invokes _Arrange_Graph_
.contract
  - _Reveal_Term_ must accept a Term and optionally a specific Term Declaration identity.
  - _Reveal_Term_ must leave exploration unchanged when its target no longer exists.
  - _Reveal_Term_ must open the ancestors needed to expose its target Term.
  - _Reveal_Term_ must move focus to the target's Knowledge when the target lies outside current graph focus.
  - _Reveal_Term_ must prefer the shortest existing visible occurrence matching the requested Term Declaration when supplied, breaking ties by display identity.
  - _Reveal_Term_ must otherwise choose a matching Term Declaration inside the current authored Group or a shortest part path inside the current derived Group.
  - _Reveal_Term_ must fall back to the target Term Declaration's authored location with canonical Term ordering.
  - _Reveal_Term_ must unhide the chosen occurrence and its ancestor path when necessary.
  - _Reveal_Term_ must open the target's own Group when needed to expose and select its Term leaf.
  - _Reveal_Term_ must leave deeper part Groups closed unless previously expanded.
  - _Reveal_Term_ must preserve library scope and current reading.
  - _Reveal_Term_ must record the prior structural state for Undo.
  - _Reveal_Term_ must arrange and fit the resulting visible graph.
.remarks
  Find Term and Reveal in structure invoke this operation.
}


specification.procedure _Read_Details_ in graph.operations = {
  The operation displaying a Term, Knowledge, Group or edge's original assertions in _Context_Pane_.
.relations
  updates _Context_Pane_
  reads _Graph_Node_
  reads _Graph_Edge_
.contract
  - _Read_Details_ must open the detail pane for a Read action, a Term hyperlink or an edge activation.
  - _Read_Details_ must resolve a Term display occurrence to its canonical Term when querying content.
  - _Read_Details_ must show Group membership without treating its display ID as a corpus Term.
  - _Read_Details_ must clear a removed Group reading target instead of displaying its internal identity as a Term.
  - _Read_Details_ must preserve graph focus, expansion, Selection and hiding.
  - _Read_Details_ must resolve hyperlinks using the Knowledge of the Term Declaration containing them.
  - _Read_Details_ must show the complete single Term Declaration of a Term in Schema order.
  - _Read_Details_ must keep older asynchronous responses from replacing newer reading targets.
  - _Read_Details_ must present an explicit error when requested content is unavailable.
  - In both modes, reading, hyperlinks and pane visibility changes must preserve inclusion, Node geometry, Selection and camera. Opening the pane must not automatically arrange, fit or recenter the Graph.
.remarks
  Read changes the reading target; Reveal changes visible graph structure.
  Chosen: Read preserves the current Viewport in both modes; Fit remains an explicit command even when opening the pane reduces the available Canvas width.
  Verification: browser checks compare exact Node transforms and camera before and after Read, closing and reopening details in both modes.
}


specification.procedure _Arrange_Graph_ in graph.operations = {
  The operation computing node and frame geometry using the selected _Graph_Layout_Policy_.
.relations
  uses _Graph_Layout_Policy_
  positions _Graph_Projection_
  positions _Graph_Node_
  updates _Graph_Anchor_
  updates _Graph_Connection_Point_
  preserves _Graph_State_
  invokes _Fit_Graph_
.contract
  - _Arrange_Graph_ must use the currently selected layout for the current visible graph.
  - _Arrange_Graph_ must update _Graph_Node_ outer bounds to enclose the current visible child graph.
  - _Arrange_Graph_ must refresh _Graph_Anchor_ and _Graph_Connection_Point_ coordinates from the accepted geometry.
  - _Arrange_Graph_ must preserve canonical identities, containment, expansion, focus, filters and reading.
  - _Arrange_Graph_ must run and fit after a layout selection change or Structure navigation; Explore membership changes retain existing positions without full arrangement.
  - _Arrange_Graph_ must display progress while placement is pending.
  - _Arrange_Graph_ must accept only the latest applicable layout result.
  - _Arrange_Graph_ must ignore stale failures as well as stale successful results.
  - _Arrange_Graph_ must terminate a force-layout Worker when superseded or when leaving Graph.
  - _Arrange_Graph_ must retry canceled pending placement when returning to Graph.
  - _Arrange_Graph_ must retain the visible graph with an explicit error if placement fails.
  - _Arrange_Graph_ must permit retrying or choosing another layout after failure.
.remarks
  Layout policy changes positions; it never changes corpus assertions.
  A Structure corpus refresh can recompute placement while retaining the camera transform; Explore retains surviving geometry.
}


specification.procedure _Fit_Graph_ in graph.operations = {
  The operation adjusting _Graph_Viewport_ to frame the currently visible graph.
.relations
  changes _Graph_Viewport_
  frames _Graph_Projection_
.contract
  - _Fit_Graph_ must adjust only camera offset and scale.
  - _Fit_Graph_ must preserve node positions, graph structure, focus and reading.
  - _Fit_Graph_ must use the available canvas dimensions, including changes in pane visibility.
  - _Fit_Graph_ must frame Nodes below the floating Toolbar and above the Canvas footer controls, using the Toolbar's current wrapped height. Toolbar changes alone must not move the camera.
  - _Fit_Graph_ must leave the camera unchanged when no visible nodes exist.
.remarks
  Fit changes the camera; Arrange changes node placement.
  Resizing the detail divider preserves node geometry.
}


specification.procedure _Highlight_Connections_ in graph.operations = {
  The interaction that highlights a hovered or keyboard-focused node's incident edges, or one hovered or keyboard-focused edge.
.relations
  highlights _Graph_Node_
  animates _Graph_Edge_
  previews _Hidden_Node_Group_
.contract
  - _Highlight_Connections_ must activate one edge when its line or label is hovered or keyboard-focused.
  - _Highlight_Connections_ must activate all visible incoming and outgoing edges when a Term card is hovered or keyboard-focused.
  - _Highlight_Connections_ must animate repeating dashes along the entire curve from each edge's source to its target.
  - _Highlight_Connections_ must preserve the original direction for incoming edges and collapsed bundles.
  - _Highlight_Connections_ must stop animation when interaction ends while retaining any Selection emphasis.
  - _Highlight_Connections_ must reveal and animate boundary Edges when their Hidden Group or a displayed member is hovered or keyboard-focused.
  - _Highlight_Connections_ must retain preview while the revealed Edge or label itself is hovered or keyboard-focused.
  - _Highlight_Connections_ must hide those Edges again when preview ends, even if an endpoint is selected.
  - _Highlight_Connections_ must not change layout, expansion, Selection or hidden membership.
  - _Highlight_Connections_ must retain static feedback without motion under a reduced-motion preference.
.remarks
  The direction follows the assertion, not the pointer or the currently read Term.
}


specification.procedure _Undo_Graph_Change_ in graph.operations = {
  The operation restoring the active mode's most recent change snapshot in _Graph_State_.
.relations
  restores _Graph_State_
  invokes _Arrange_Graph_
.contract
  - _Undo_Graph_Change_ must restore the active mode's latest snapshot: Structure expansion, focus, Selection and hiding, or Explore inclusion, Selection and Node geometry.
  - _Undo_Graph_Change_ must consume that snapshot when it restores it.
  - _Undo_Graph_Change_ must preserve current layout selection, filters and reading.
  - _Undo_Graph_Change_ must leave graph state unchanged when history is empty.
  - _Undo_Graph_Change_ must never restore identities from an earlier corpus revision.
  - _Undo_Graph_Change_ must arrange and fit restored Structure; Explore must restore recorded geometry without arranging or fitting.
.remarks
  Chosen: history contains up to thirty structural changes; camera and layout changes are not undoable.
}


specification.procedure _Refresh_Graph_ in graph.operations = {
  The operation reconciling graph exploration with a newer server corpus revision.
.relations
  reads _HTTP_Server_
  updates _Graph_Containment_
  updates _Graph_Projection_
  reconciles _Graph_State_
  invokes _Arrange_Graph_
.contract
  - _Refresh_Graph_ must rebuild Structure containment and visible representatives from the current corpus.
  - _Refresh_Graph_ must remove deleted identities and invalid occurrence paths from expansion, node selection and hidden subtree roots.
  - _Refresh_Graph_ must retain focus when its target remains valid.
  - _Refresh_Graph_ must return focus to All Knowledge when its target disappears.
  - _Refresh_Graph_ must regenerate necessary Hidden Groups and remove empty ones from temporary state.
  - _Refresh_Graph_ must remove redundant hidden roots beneath another wholly hidden subtree after containment changes.
  - _Refresh_Graph_ must preserve surviving hiding, expansion, layout selection and Relation visibility.
  - _Refresh_Graph_ must clear both modes' history across corpus revisions.
  - _Refresh_Graph_ must not let an older query result or failure overwrite a newer graph session.
  - _Refresh_Graph_ must retain an explicit error state when current graph data cannot be loaded.
  - Refresh must reconcile both modes, including the inactive one. Explore must retain surviving membership, Selection and geometry, remove deleted Terms and update Relations without adding newly discovered neighbors or changing camera.
  - Refresh must close pending neighbor or Find dialogs so choices from an old revision cannot be applied.
  - Refresh must cancel any active pointer gesture before reconciling Nodes so incomplete geometry or Selection cannot enter a new revision.
.remarks
  Server health, disconnection and corpus diagnostics remain governed by the Explorer.
  Refresh changes the available graph; Undo does not reverse corpus changes.
}


concept _Graph_Group_ in graph.rendering.structure = {
  A visual collection representing authored Term Declaration organization, derived part structure or temporarily hidden sibling subtrees.
.relations
  represents _Group_
  derives from _Relation_
  includes _Term_Declaration_
  represented by _Compound_Graph_Node_
.contract
  - _Graph_Group_ must distinguish authored, derived and hidden origins.
  - _Graph_Group_ must have an identity distinct from its member Term Declarations and Terms.
  - _Graph_Group_ must exist in both collapsed and expanded states.
  - An authored _Graph_Group_ must follow its Knowledge and Group path, including implicit prefixes.
  - An authored _Graph_Group_ must contain all Term Declarations naming its exact path and its immediate subgroups.
  - A derived _Graph_Group_ must include its root Term Declaration as an independent direct child.
  - A derived _Graph_Group_ must derive parts only from parthood declarations owned by its root Term Declaration.
  - A derived _Graph_Group_ must include every Term Declaration of each direct target Term as a leaf or nested derived Group.
  - A derived _Graph_Group_ must identify each occurrence by its root-to-member Term Declaration path.
  - A derived _Graph_Group_ must stop at a repeated ancestor Term Declaration and keep that occurrence as a leaf.
  - A derived _Graph_Group_ must preserve every Term Declaration's original authored Group membership.
  - _Graph_Group_ must retain its identity and membership when expanded or collapsed.
  - _Graph_Group_ must not create corpus Terms, Term Kinds or Relations.
.remarks
  Example: concept Test and procedure Test may appear in different authored Groups.
  Example: Machine group contains Machine itself and every Term Declaration of its part Terms.
  Derived means computed for rendering; collapse does not delete the Group.
  Authored Group paths are stored on Term Declarations; graph expansion is session state.
}


concept _Graph_Selection_ in graph.state = {
  The persistent set of display Node instances chosen for Graph commands and visual emphasis.
.relations
  selects _Graph_Node_
  emphasizes _Graph_Edge_
  held by _Graph_State_
.contract
  - _Graph_Selection_ must identify visible Node instances: Structure may have several occurrences of one Term, while Explore has one instance per included canonical Term.
  - _Graph_Selection_ must replace its set on ordinary Node activation and toggle membership on Shift or Ctrl/Command activation.
  - _Graph_Selection_ must clear on blank Canvas click without treating a pan gesture as that click.
  - _Graph_Selection_ must preserve expansion, hiding, graph focus, reading, layout and camera.
  - _Graph_Selection_ must display selected Nodes and their incident projected Edges with steady bright emphasis.
  - _Graph_Selection_ must not make a concealed Hidden Group boundary Edge visible by itself.
  - _Graph_Selection_ must permit selecting members inside an expanded Hidden Group without unhiding them.
  - In Explore Select mode, dragging blank Canvas must preview a rectangular Selection of intersecting Node cards in any drag direction. Shift or Ctrl/Command must add matches to the Selection captured at gesture start.
  - Box selection must use graph coordinates under the current pan and zoom, leave camera and geometry unchanged, and distinguish a drag from a click with a movement threshold.
  - Escape or pointer cancellation must restore the Selection before a Box selection gesture; releasing commits Selection without adding a geometry Undo snapshot.
.remarks
  Chosen: Explore offers Box selection; Structure retains containment navigation.
  Chosen: selecting an ordinary Group does not implicitly select its descendants.
  Hover supplies transient directed animation; Selection supplies persistent emphasis.
}


concept _Hidden_Node_Group_ in graph.rendering.structure = {
  A temporary collection representing hidden sibling subtrees under their original parent.
.relations
  is_a _Graph_Group_†
  represents _Graph_Node_
  preserves _Graph_Containment_
  conceals _Graph_Edge_
.contract
  - _Hidden_Node_Group_ must have one identity per original parent, including the virtual root of All Knowledge.
  - _Hidden_Node_Group_ must exist only when its parent has hidden children.
  - _Hidden_Node_Group_ must initially be collapsed and permit independent expansion.
  - _Hidden_Node_Group_ must preserve the original identity, membership and expansion state of its member subtrees.
  - _Hidden_Node_Group_ must not create nested Hidden Groups inside a subtree that is already wholly hidden.
  - _Hidden_Node_Group_ must keep boundary Edges concealed except during Hover or keyboard preview, even when expanded.
  - _Hidden_Node_Group_ must allow internal Edges between exposed members to remain visible normally.
  - _Hidden_Node_Group_ must disappear when its last hidden member leaves.
  - _Hidden_Node_Group_ must never create or edit a corpus Term Declaration or Relation.
.remarks
  Correction: the active operation declares the effect; redundant changed by declarations have been removed.
  Example: selecting A1 keeps Root and A; A2 goes into Hidden under A, while B and C go into Hidden under Root.
  Chosen: a selected Hidden Group represents its direct hidden members as command inputs.
  Chosen: Hidden Groups have no Focus action because focus addresses original containment.
}


specification.procedure _Hide_Other_Nodes_ in graph.operations = {
  The operation retaining selected Nodes, optionally their direct neighbors, and necessary ancestor paths while hiding the remaining branches.
.relations
  reads _Graph_Selection_
  reads _Graph_Edge_
  changes _Graph_State_
  produces _Hidden_Node_Group_
  follows _Graph_Containment_
  invokes _Arrange_Graph_
.contract
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must offer Selected only and Selected + neighbors modes through Hide others.
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must default to Selected only.
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must refuse without changing state or history when no visible Node is selected.
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must retain the selected display instances in both modes.
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must add endpoints connected directly to the original Selection by incoming or outgoing projected Edges in Selected + neighbors mode.
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must use the current Relation filter and pre-hide expansion to determine those Edges, disregarding an earlier hide mask.
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must not recursively add neighbors of newly retained neighbors.
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must retain the original ancestor paths of its retained Nodes.
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must traverse from the current focus root or All Knowledge roots, keeping retained Nodes and descending through them.
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must collect any other child subtree into a Hidden Group at its original parent without descending into that hidden subtree.
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must not retain an ordinary Group's descendants merely because the Group is selected.
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must replace hiding within its current focus while preserving hiding outside that boundary.
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must leave hiding unchanged when Selection alone changes later.
  - _Hide_Other_Nodes_ must record the preceding structural state for Undo and arrange the resulting graph.
.remarks
  Example: with A selected and A-B-C connections, Selected + neighbors retains A and B, not C.
  Chosen: a Group represents its collapsed members for projected adjacency; expanded Group headers are not semantic Edge endpoints.
}


specification.procedure _Unhide_Nodes_ in graph.operations = {
  The operation exposing selected hidden Nodes or clearing all hiding while preserving original containment.
.relations
  reads _Graph_Selection_
  changes _Graph_State_
  removes _Hidden_Node_Group_
  preserves _Graph_Containment_
  invokes _Arrange_Graph_
.contract
  - _Unhide_Nodes_ must offer Unhide selected and Show all.
  - _Unhide_Nodes_ must leave state and history unchanged when Unhide selected has no selected hidden input.
  - _Unhide_Nodes_ must expose each selected hidden Node at its original location with every necessary ancestor.
  - _Unhide_Nodes_ must split hidden branches along exposed paths, regrouping only the remaining hidden siblings.
  - _Unhide_Nodes_ must leave unselected descendants of an exposed ordinary Group hidden.
  - _Unhide_Nodes_ must open necessary ancestors without changing unrelated expansion.
  - _Unhide_Nodes_ must preserve selected visible Nodes alongside its selected hidden inputs.
  - _Unhide_Nodes_ must remove empty Hidden Groups and their temporary expansion state.
  - _Unhide_Nodes_ must clear all hiding for Show all while preserving original Node expansion and membership.
  - _Unhide_Nodes_ must leave state and history unchanged when Show all has no hidden state.
  - _Unhide_Nodes_ must record the preceding structural state for Undo and arrange the resulting graph.
.remarks
  Expanding a Hidden Group exposes items for inspection; Unhide changes their visibility membership.
}


concept _Markdown_Note_ in operations.ui = {
  A browser-cached Markdown explanation sent through _Control_UI_ to accompany reading in _Explorer_.
.relations
  delivered by _Control_UI_
  presented by _Explorer_
  accompanies _Graph_View_
  records _Working_Set_
  uses _Context_Pane_
.contract
  - A Markdown Note must retain its command UUID as identity, title, exact Markdown body, receipt timestamp and the receiving view's location and Working Set Terms.
  - A Note must contain nonempty Markdown of at most 65536 UTF-16 code units. A supplied title must contain 1–160 code units after trimming; an omitted title must use the first nonempty Markdown line, without its leading heading marker, limited to 80 code units.
  - The browser must cache the ten most recent Notes per origin and Home in localStorage, ordered by receipt time descending with UUID as the tie breaker. New Notes evict the oldest; page refresh, tab closure and server restart must not clear the cache.
  - Sending a Note must persist it before opening its reader and acknowledging applied. Unavailable storage must report an explicit failed outcome without claiming persistence or changing the view. Invalid cached records must not break the reader.
  - Repeated delivery of a retained UUID with the same content must reopen the existing Note without duplication or changing its receipt time. Reusing it for different content must fail.
  - Other tabs sharing the origin and Home must see updated history without automatic navigation or opening. Each tab must retain its own open/closed state and selected Note; eviction must fall back to the newest retained Note.
  - The reader must expose Note title, receipt time, cached count, a newest-first history chooser and a close control. Notes must remain reachable from the common shell, including after refresh, without requiring another command.
  - Opening, closing or selecting a Note must retain the current route, reading target, Graph membership, positions, camera and selection. The shared reader must show Note beside the active Main View; closing must collapse the reader, and selecting Context must restore its retained target.
  - Notes must reuse the safe Markdown, code highlighting, Mermaid and link behavior of _Explorer_. Qualified Term references must open Context using their Knowledge and support dragging into the Working Set; unqualified tokens must remain literal without an implicit Knowledge.
  - Captured context must identify the view at receipt; selecting an old Note must not restore a Graph workspace or execute its Markdown.
.remarks
  Chosen: Notes are local reader explanations rather than corpus knowledge. Cache scope follows origin and Home, not a transient Session connection.
  Chosen: Note history is available across tabs while selected Note and reader visibility remain tab-local. Individual storage keys avoid overwriting another tab's Notes with a stale list.
}


concept _UI_Session_ in operations.ui = {
  One live browser tab of _Explorer_, identified independently of its connection to _HTTP_Server_.
.relations
  belongs to _Explorer_
  references _HTTP_Server_
  reports _Working_Set_
.contract
  - The active view must distinguish graphql from Explorer views. Running a browser query contributes to busy state; query source and variable values are not UI Session metadata.
  - A UI Session must identify one tab by a browser-generated UUID v4 retained across page refresh, transport reconnect and server restart within that tab. A duplicate live tab must register a distinct UUID.
  - A UI Session must retain a private reconnect credential; a registered ID with a different credential must be refused. An independent live page must not replace another page's active connection. Reconnecting the same page may replace its obsolete connection.
  - A UI Session must expose full ID, shortest unique hexadecimal prefix of at least six characters, ready/busy/disconnected status, visibility, current location, reading target, Graph mode, ordered Working Set Terms, Graph selection, corpus revision, UI revision and timestamps.
  - A UI Session must expose cached Note count, selected Note UUID when present and Note reader visibility; Note bodies belong to local browser history and command payloads, not the Session summary.
  - Last active must reflect meaningful browser activity; last seen must reflect connection contact. Heartbeats and inspection must not advance last active. Lists must sort by last active descending and full ID ascending for ties.
  - Reconnection must retain the tab's creation and activity timestamps, including after server restart. Future client timestamps must be bounded by server time. UI revision must increase when reported state changes and persist with the tab identity across refresh.
  - A disconnected Session must retain its last reported state, labeled disconnected. Reconnection must report current state before accepting commands. A server restart may rebuild the registry from reconnecting tabs without changing their UUIDs.
  - Display prefixes must not be identities or reconnect credentials. Full UUIDs and unique prefixes of at least six hexadecimal characters must be accepted; unknown, invalid and ambiguous selectors must fail explicitly.
.remarks
  Chosen: identity and reconnect credential use tab-scoped sessionStorage. Working Set membership follows route state. Graph geometry, Selection and expansion remain in memory; retaining a UUID does not persist those states.
  Chosen: disconnected registrations and command records expire after one hour; active connections remain registered. Visibility is independent of connection status.
}


concept _UI_Command_ in operations.ui = {
  A named instruction for one _UI_Session_, with its delivery outcome and resulting browser state.
.relations
  targets _UI_Session_
  references _Term_
  carries _Markdown_Note_
.contract
  - A UI Command must contain a UUID, target Session UUID, named operation and exact Term IDs when required. Its record must retain the request, creation time, outcome, completion time when known and resulting state or error.
  - The outcome must be pending until acknowledged, applied after successful browser application, failed after an explicit refusal or application error, and unknown after timeout or connection loss without acknowledgement.
  - Applied must mean the target view and required Graph layout are ready. Server receipt alone must not count as success.
  - A Note command must carry the Markdown body and optional title. Applied must additionally mean the _Markdown_Note_ was cached and its reader opened; asynchronous diagrams may still be rendering.
  - Repeating the same command UUID and payload must return the existing record without another delivery. Reusing that UUID for another payload must fail.
  - A late acknowledgement on the owning live connection may resolve unknown. A disconnected command must never replay automatically after reconnection or restart.
  - The result must remain queryable by full command UUID during its retention interval; an expired or unknown command must fail explicitly. A server restart must not claim to know outcomes it no longer retains.
  - Browser messages above two MiB, Term or selection collections above ten thousand items, and UI locations above 8192 characters must be refused before processing.
.remarks
  Chosen: wait up to thirty seconds for acknowledgement; timeout reports unknown with its command UUID. Retain at most one thousand records, evicting only settled records when capacity is needed.
  Chosen: bounded message and collection sizes keep a controller request from exhausting a connected browser or server.
}


specification.procedure _Control_UI_ in operations.ui = {
  Send a _UI_Command_ to a selected _UI_Session_ and report its application, or inspect Sessions and command outcomes.
.relations
  applies _UI_Command_
  targets _UI_Session_
  presents _Term_
  changes _Graph_View_
  changes _Working_Set_
  uses _HTTP_Server_
  sends _Markdown_Note_
.contract
  - Control UI must provide ui session list, ui session view <session>, ui command view <id>, ui open <term> --session <session>, and ui explore set/add/remove <terms...> --session <session> plus ui explore clear --session <session>.
  - Control UI must provide ui note send --session <session> with optional --file <path> and --title <text>. An omitted file or - must read stdin through the shared command input adapter; file content must be sent as Markdown rather than exposed through a server file endpoint. _Markdown_Note_ owns payload, retention and reading behavior.
  - Control UI must select an existing Home and require its matching running server; it must not fall back to filesystem execution or choose a Session implicitly. Session and command inspection must support structured output; Session lists must use aligned tables.
  - Control UI must reject unsupported options, missing arguments and --knowledge; Term identities must instead supply Knowledge qualification where necessary.
  - Control UI must accept exact Term spellings, resolve unique local spellings to qualified IDs and reject unknown, ambiguous or glob selectors before any UI effect. Every requested Term must pass validation as one request.
  - Open must replace the Working Set with its one Term, activate Term Declarations and read that Term in Context. Explore operations must enter Explore mode and preserve the reading target and independent library filters.
  - Set must replace Working Set membership with the distinct requested Terms, select them, arrange and fit the result. Add and remove must change only requested membership and preserve surviving positions, selection and camera. Clear must empty the Working Set and clear selection while retaining the camera and reading target.
  - Membership changes must use the same operations as direct interaction and form one Undo transaction. No operation may automatically expand neighbors.
  - Control UI must refuse an unavailable or busy target before changing its state. A pending dialog, active pointer gesture, data refresh or layout must count as busy. The browser must recheck readiness and Terms immediately before application.
  - Control UI must report success only for an applied acknowledgement. Failed or unknown outcomes must return a nonzero exit status with the command UUID and available error; inspection of an existing record must remain a successful read.
  - The /api/ui route must permit controller requests without an Origin from loopback or the server's own bound local address. Other controller requests must require the configured server.controlToken as a Bearer credential. Session registration must use same-origin browser connections, matching Home and protocol, and must expose no corpus authoring or arbitrary code execution.
.remarks
  Chosen: HTTP controller requests and WebSocket browser messages share validated operation/state definitions. Direct UI actions and remote commands reuse navigation and Graph membership operations.
  Chosen: protocol 11 reports ordered Working Set membership in terms; open and Explore commands use the same membership owner as direct interaction.
  Correction: CLI help describes the shared Working Set effects; open replaces membership and opens Term Declarations and Context, rather than retaining the previous Explore graph. Help and isolated-Session command checks verify these effects.
}


